window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("model-internals", {"topics":[{"key":"model-internals/transformer","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Radford et al., 2019 — Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"Before 2017 sequence models were recurrent. An LSTM reads token *t* only after it has finished token\n*t−1*, so a 1,000-token document is 1,000 dependent steps. That has two costs. First, you cannot\nparallelize across positions inside a sequence, so GPUs sit mostly idle and wall-clock time grows with\nlength. Second, information from position 1 reaches position 1,000 only by surviving 999 updates of a\nfixed-size hidden vector; gradients through that path shrink, and long-range dependencies are learned\nbadly or not at all.\n\nAttention had already been bolted onto RNNs for translation to fix the second problem: the decoder could\nlook back at every encoder state instead of one summary vector. But the recurrent backbone kept the\nfirst problem. Convolutional alternatives (ByteNet, ConvS2S) parallelize, yet connecting two positions\n*n* apart needs O(n/k) stacked layers of kernel width *k*, or O(log n) with dilation, so distance still\ncosts depth.\n\nThe transformer's bet was to drop recurrence and convolution entirely and let attention do all the\nmixing between positions. Then every position in a layer is computed at once, and the path between any\ntwo positions is one layer long. The price is attention cost that grows with the square of sequence\nlength and a model with no built-in sense of order, which positional encodings must supply. That trade\nwas right because the binding constraint on quality turned out to be how much data and compute you can\npush through training, and a matmul-parallel architecture scales that far better than a sequential one.","how":"### The block\nToken ids go through an embedding table to give an `[n, d_model]` matrix, with position information\nadded or injected into attention. Then *L* identical blocks run in sequence. Each block has two\nsub-layers:\n\n1. **Attention sub-layer.** Every position builds a query, scores it against the keys of all positions it\n   is allowed to see, and takes the softmax-weighted sum of their values. This is the *only* place in the\n   network where tokens exchange information. Multi-head attention runs several of these in parallel in\n   different subspaces.\n2. **Feed-forward sub-layer (MLP).** Applied to every position independently with the same weights:\n   `FFN(x) = W₂·act(W₁·x)`, with an inner width of about 4×d_model (or roughly 2.7× when a gated SwiGLU\n   with three matrices is used, as in Llama). Attention is linear in the values once the weights are\n   fixed, so the nonlinearity and most of the parameters live here; the MLP holds roughly two thirds of a\n   layer's weights.\n\nEach sub-layer is wrapped as `x = x + SubLayer(Norm(x))`. The residual connection exists because it gives\ngradients an identity path through 30–100 layers; the useful picture is a *residual stream* of width\nd_model that each sub-layer reads from and writes a small update into. The original paper normalized\n*after* the addition (post-norm); GPT-2 and nearly everything since normalize *before* the sub-layer\n(pre-norm) because it keeps the identity path clean and trains deep stacks without delicate warmup\nschedules. Llama-style models replace LayerNorm with RMSNorm, which drops the mean-centering step\nbecause it is cheaper and works as well.\n\n### The head\nAfter the last block: a final norm, then a linear map from d_model to the vocabulary size (the LM head,\noften weight-tied to the input embedding), giving logits that a softmax turns into a next-token\ndistribution. Training predicts token *t+1* at every position *t* in the same forward pass under a\ncausal mask, so one pass over a 4k-token sequence yields 4k training signals. That density of signal per\nFLOP is a large part of why the architecture scales.\n\n### Three layouts\n- **Encoder–decoder** (original, T5): the encoder uses bidirectional self-attention over the input; the\n  decoder uses causal self-attention plus cross-attention into the encoder output. Natural for\n  translation where input and output are distinct sequences.\n- **Encoder-only** (BERT): bidirectional, every token sees every other. Strong for classification and\n  embeddings; cannot generate text autoregressively.\n- **Decoder-only** (GPT, Llama, most chat models): one causal stack. It won for LLMs because it is a\n  single component to scale, it trains on raw unlabeled text with one objective, prompt and completion\n  live in the same stream so any task becomes conditioning, and causality is what makes the KV cache\n  possible at inference.\n\n### Where parameters and FLOPs go\nPer layer with hidden size *d*: the four attention projections cost 4d² parameters; a 4× MLP costs 8d²;\na SwiGLU MLP costs 3·d·d_ff. For Llama-2-7B (d = 4096, L = 32, d_ff = 11008, vocab 32,000): attention\n67.1M and MLP 135.3M per layer, 202.4M × 32 = 6.48B, plus two untied 131M embedding matrices, ≈ 6.74B.\nForward compute is about 2 FLOPs per parameter per token (each weight takes part in one\nmultiply-accumulate) plus an attention-score term of roughly 4·n·d per layer per token that stays\nsmall until *n* approaches *d*. Hence the rules of thumb: inference ≈ 2N FLOPs per token, training ≈ 6N\nper token (forward plus a backward that costs about twice the forward). These two numbers let you\nestimate cost before touching a GPU.\n\n### What the pieces learn\nInterpretability work consistently finds attention heads that implement routing (attend to the previous\ntoken, find an earlier occurrence of the current token and copy what followed it) and MLP layers that\nact like key–value memories storing factual and lexical associations. Treat this as a useful picture,\nnot a complete theory.","example":"A minimal pre-norm decoder-only transformer in PyTorch. The block is the whole architecture; the rest is\nembedding, stacking and the tied LM head. `nn.RMSNorm` needs PyTorch 2.4+; swap in `nn.LayerNorm` otherwise.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn, torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass Block(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d=512, n_heads=8, d_ff=2048):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.h, self.dk = n_heads, d // n_heads\n        self.n1, self.n2 = nn.RMSNorm(d), nn.RMSNorm(d)\n        self.qkv, self.o = nn.Linear(d, 3 * d, bias=False), nn.Linear(d, d, bias=False)\n        self.up, self.down = nn.Linear(d, d_ff, bias=False), nn.Linear(d_ff, d, bias=False)\n\n    def forward(self, x):                                   # x: [B, n, d]\n        B, n, d = x.shape\n        q, k, v = self.qkv(self.n1(x)).split(d, dim=-1)\n        q, k, v = (t.view(B, n, self.h, self.dk).transpose(1, 2) for t in (q, k, v))  # [B, h, n, dk]\n        a = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\n        x = x + self.o(a.transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d))  # residual 1: tokens have now mixed\n        return x + self.down(F.gelu(self.up(self.n2(x))))   # residual 2: per-position transform\n\nclass TinyLM(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, vocab=32000, d=512, L=6, max_len=1024):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.emb, self.pos = nn.Embedding(vocab, d), nn.Embedding(max_len, d)\n        self.blocks = nn.ModuleList(Block(d) for _ in range(L))\n        self.norm, self.head = nn.RMSNorm(d), nn.Linear(d, vocab, bias=False)\n        self.head.weight = self.emb.weight                  # weight tying: one matrix, two jobs\n\n    def forward(self, ids):                                 # ids: [B, n] -> logits [B, n, vocab]\n        x = self.emb(ids) + self.pos(torch.arange(ids.shape[1], device=ids.device))\n        for b in self.blocks:\n            x = b(x)\n        return self.head(self.norm(x))\n\nm = TinyLM(); ids = torch.randint(0, 32000, (2, 128))\nprint(m(ids).shape, f\"{sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters())/1e6:.1f}M params\")\n# torch.Size([2, 128, 32000]) 35.8M params  (16.4M of them are the shared embedding)\n```","tradeoffs":["Full parallelism across positions and O(1) path length between any two tokens, paid for with attention cost quadratic in sequence length; the trade flips against plain attention when contexts reach hundreds of thousands of tokens, which is where sliding windows, retrieval and state-space hybrids enter.","No built-in locality or order bias means the model needs positional encodings and more data than a CNN or RNN to learn simple structure, but the same lack of bias is why one architecture handles prose, code, audio tokens and protein sequences.","Decoder-only gives one objective, one stack and a KV cache, but every token sees only its left context; for classification and embedding tasks an encoder that sees the whole input both ways is often better per parameter.","Pre-norm trains deep stacks reliably but lets the residual stream's magnitude grow with depth, which is why final norms and careful initialization matter at scale; post-norm has the opposite profile."],"pitfalls":["Estimating serving cost from parameter count alone and ignoring the attention term; the symptom is a cost model that is accurate at 2k context and badly wrong at 64k.","Treating the architecture diagram as the whole story: tokenizer, positional scheme, norm placement, activation and head layout differ between model families, and loading weights with the wrong variant produces a model that runs and emits fluent garbage.","Assuming an encoder-only model can be prompted to generate, or a decoder-only model gives good bidirectional embeddings out of the box; the symptom is puzzlingly weak results on a task the architecture was never trained to do.","Reading attention maps as explanations of a prediction; many heads over many layers plus the MLP path mean a single map rarely tells you why the model answered as it did."],"when":"You rarely choose *whether* to use a transformer; you choose which layout and size, and you need the\nblock structure in your head to reason about everything downstream: why the KV cache exists, why GQA\nand FlashAttention target the attention sub-layer, why quantization targets the MLP matrices, why\ncontext length is expensive, and how to turn a model config (`hidden_size`, `num_hidden_layers`,\n`intermediate_size`, `num_attention_heads`, `num_key_value_heads`) into parameter, FLOP and memory\nnumbers. Reach for an encoder when the task is classify-or-embed with the whole input visible; a\ndecoder when it is generate; an encoder–decoder when inputs are long and fixed and outputs short\n(translation, some summarization). In interviews, be ready to draw the block, name the two sub-layers\nand their roles, and derive the 2N-FLOPs-per-token estimate from the shapes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads a model's config to compute memory and FLOPs, picks encoder vs decoder models for embeddings vs generation, and knows which sub-layer a given optimization touches.","architect":"Owns the cost and capability model that follows from layout and size: context policy, GPU tier and whether a small encoder can replace an LLM call for classification.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a 70B model costs what it does, why their long documents change the bill non-linearly, and why \"just fine-tune BERT to write emails\" does not work."},"check":{"q":"Decoder-only transformers won for general-purpose LLMs over encoder–decoder designs. Explain the\nreasons, and name what was given up in the process.","a":"A single causal stack is one component to scale and one objective, next-token prediction, that\ntrains on unlabeled text. Because prompt and completion share the stream, any task becomes conditioning\nrather than a separate head, which is what makes in-context learning and instruction following natural.\nCausality also means earlier tokens' keys and values never change, so inference can cache them and\ngenerate in linear time per token. What was given up: the input is read only left-to-right, so per-token\nrepresentations of the prompt are weaker than an encoder's bidirectional ones, which still matters for\nembeddings and classification; and prompt plus output share one quadratic attention budget, whereas an\nencoder–decoder processes the input once and cross-attends to it cheaply from each output step."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a transformer block, which component is the only one that moves information between positions?","options":["The feed-forward (MLP) sub-layer","The attention sub-layer","The layer normalization","The residual connection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The MLP, norms and residuals all operate on each position independently with shared weights. Only attention computes a weighted sum over other positions, so it is the sole channel for cross-token information flow."},{"q":"Why did pre-norm (`x + f(Norm(x))`) replace the original post-norm in deep decoder stacks?","options":["It reduces the parameter count of each block","It is what makes the KV cache possible","It keeps the identity residual path clean, so very deep stacks train stably without delicate warmup","It lowers attention cost from quadratic to linear"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Post-norm puts a normalization on the main residual path, which makes gradients through deep stacks fragile. Pre-norm leaves the residual stream untouched between blocks. It has nothing to do with the cache or attention complexity, and it changes no parameter count."},{"q":"For a dense transformer with N parameters at modest context, roughly how many FLOPs does one forward token cost?","options":["About N","About 2N","About 6N","About N²"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every weight participates in one multiply-accumulate per token, which is two FLOPs, giving ≈2N; the attention-score term adds little until context approaches the hidden size. 6N is the training estimate, which includes the backward pass."}]},{"key":"model-internals/attention-mechanism","sources":["Bahdanau et al., 2014 — Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate","Luong et al., 2015 — Effective Approaches to Attention-based Neural Machine Translation","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need"],"why":"The 2014 sequence-to-sequence translators read the source sentence with an encoder RNN into a single\nfinal hidden state and generated the target from that vector. It worked for short sentences and\ndegraded sharply with length, for an obvious reason once stated: a fifty-word sentence had to pass\nthrough the same few hundred numbers as a five-word one, and the decoder needed different parts of it at\ndifferent steps.\n\nThe naive fix, a bigger vector, does not help much. What the decoder needs at output step *t* depends on\n*t*, so a static summary written before decoding starts must anticipate every future need at once. The\nrequired capacity grows with the number of (input position, output step) combinations, not with any\nfixed width.\n\nBahdanau et al. kept every encoder hidden state and let the decoder, at each step, score its current\nstate against each of them, normalize the scores, and build a context vector as the weighted sum. The\nweights, an alignment between output and input words, were learned with no alignment supervision, and\ntranslation quality on long sentences stopped collapsing. The general move is to replace \"summarize\nonce, then read the summary\" with \"look up what you need, when you need it\", and to make the lookup soft\nso that gradients flow through it. That single move is the ancestor of self-attention, cross-attention\nand, in hard top-k form, of vector retrieval.","how":"### Three roles\nEvery attention computation has a **query** (what am I looking for), a set of **keys** (what each item\nadvertises about itself) and a set of **values** (what each item hands over if selected). Keys and values\nare separate because the thing you match on need not be the thing you retrieve, the same way a database\nindex differs from the row it points to. In neural attention all three are usually produced by learned\nlinear projections `W_Q`, `W_K`, `W_V` of some hidden states, so the network learns what to match on and\nwhat to pass along.\n\n### Score, normalize, mix\nFor one query **q** and keys **k₁…kₙ**:\n\n```\nscore_i = f(q, k_i)\nα       = softmax(score)              # α_i ≥ 0, Σ α_i = 1\noutput  = Σ_i α_i · v_i\n```\n\nThe choice of *f* is the main design decision:\n- **Additive** (Bahdanau): `vᵀ·tanh(W₁q + W₂k)`, a tiny MLP per (query, key) pair. Expressive, but it\n  cannot be written as one matrix multiplication over all keys.\n- **Dot-product** (Luong): `qᵀk`. All scores for a query come from a single matrix–vector product, and\n  all queries at once from a matrix–matrix product, which is exactly what GPUs do fastest.\n- **Scaled dot-product** (Vaswani): `qᵀk / √d_k`. If the components of **q** and **k** are roughly\n  independent with zero mean and unit variance, `qᵀk` has variance d_k, so with d_k = 64 the scores have\n  standard deviation 8. Softmax of numbers spread that widely is nearly one-hot, its gradient is close to\n  zero almost everywhere, and learning stalls. Dividing by √d_k puts the scores back at unit variance.\n  The original paper reports additive and dot-product attention performing similarly at small d_k and\n  unscaled dot-product falling behind at larger d_k, which is what motivated the scaling.\n\nSoftmax is used rather than, say, dividing by the sum of raw scores, because it guarantees positive\nweights that sum to one (so the output is a convex combination of values whose scale does not depend on\nhow many keys there are), it is smooth everywhere, and its exponential sharpens contrasts so the model can\napproximate a hard selection while remaining trainable. Hard attention, which samples one key, needs\nreinforcement-style gradient estimators and has largely been abandoned for language.\n\n### Where the vectors come from decides the flavour\n- **Cross-attention:** queries from one sequence, keys and values from another. The decoder-to-encoder\n  attention in translation; also how vision-language models inject image features and how text\n  conditions a diffusion model.\n- **Self-attention:** queries, keys and values all from the same sequence, so each token builds its\n  representation from its neighbours. This is the transformer's core and has its own lesson.\n\n### Attention as a soft dictionary\nA Python `dict` looks up one exact key and returns one value. Attention looks up every key a little and\nreturns a blend, which is what makes it differentiable. Read this way, a vector database performing\ntop-k dot-product search over stored embeddings is the hard, external, disk-backed version of the same\noperation, and retrieval-augmented generation is attention over documents the model's weights never saw.\n\n### Cost\nOne query against *n* keys costs O(n·d) for scores plus O(n·d) for the weighted sum. With *n* queries,\nas in self-attention, that becomes O(n²·d), and the n×n score matrix is the quantity that drives every\nlater discussion of context length, memory and kernels.","example":"A worked instance in NumPy, first with two-dimensional vectors you can check by hand, then a\ndemonstration of why the √d_k scaling matters.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nnp.random.seed(0)\n\ndef attention(q, K, V, scale=True):\n    scores = K @ q                                     # one dot product per key\n    if scale:\n        scores = scores / np.sqrt(q.shape[-1])\n    w = np.exp(scores - scores.max()); w /= w.sum()    # numerically stable softmax\n    return w, w @ V\n\n# Three memories; the query points along the first key, half-way toward the third.\nK = np.array([[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0], [0.7, 0.7]])\nV = np.array([[10.0, 0.0], [0.0, 10.0], [5.0, 5.0]])\nq = np.array([1.0, 0.0])\nw, out = attention(q, K, V, scale=False)\nprint(np.round(w, 3), np.round(out, 2))\n# scores 1.0, 0.0, 0.7 -> weights [0.474 0.174 0.351] -> output [6.5 3.5]\n# mostly the first value, a third of the third, a little of the second: a blend, never a pick.\n\n# With d_k = 64 and unit-variance vectors, raw scores have std ~8 and softmax is nearly saturated.\nd, n = 64, 16\nK, q, V = np.random.randn(n, d), np.random.randn(d), np.eye(n)\nfor s in (False, True):\n    w, _ = attention(q, K, V, scale=s)\n    entropy = -(w * np.log(w + 1e-12)).sum()\n    print(\"scaled\" if s else \"raw   \", f\"max weight {w.max():.3f}  entropy {entropy:.2f} (uniform = {np.log(n):.2f})\")\n# raw    max weight 0.778  entropy 0.65 (uniform = 2.77)  <- almost all mass on 2-3 keys; near one-hot, gradients vanish\n# scaled max weight 0.176  entropy 2.53 (uniform = 2.77)  <- a real distribution the model can learn to sharpen\n# (exact values depend on the seed; the ratio of entropies is the point)\n```","tradeoffs":["Content-based lookup over the entire input, paid for with cost linear in the number of keys per query; once every position is a query the cost is quadratic, and past tens of thousands of positions you move work to sparse attention or external retrieval.","Soft weights are differentiable and train end to end, but the output is always a blend; when several keys match, the result is blurred, so tasks that need exact provenance (citations) use hard top-k retrieval in front of the model.","Learned projections let the model decide what \"similar\" means, at the price of parameters and of attention maps that look like alignments but are not reliable explanations.","Additive scoring is slightly more expressive; dot-product scoring maps onto a single matmul and therefore onto tensor cores, which is why it won once d_k was scaled."],"pitfalls":["Reading attention weights as feature importance; the symptom is explanations that change with the random seed or disagree with gradient-based attribution for the same prediction.","Omitting the √d_k scaling in a from-scratch implementation; the symptom is attention maps that are nearly one-hot from the first steps and a training loss that plateaus early.","Applying softmax along the wrong axis so weights sum to one across queries rather than across keys; the symptom is outputs whose magnitude changes with sequence length.","Forgetting to mask padding positions; the symptom is the same input producing different outputs depending on how much padding its batch-mates needed."],"when":"You rarely instantiate attention as a standalone primitive, but you meet the pattern constantly: in\nevery transformer layer, in cross-attention adapters that fuse modalities, and in the retrieval layer of\na RAG system, which is attention done externally with hard top-k. The decisions that follow from it are\npractical: how to mask padding and future positions, whether a task needs the model to attend over a\nlong input or a retriever to select a short one, and how to budget the n² cost. In interviews the\nexpected baseline is to explain query, key and value with an example, say why softmax and why √d_k, and\ndistinguish self- from cross-attention; a strong answer connects the soft-dictionary view to vector\nsearch and says when each is the right tool.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and debugs attention masks for padding and causality, and recognizes the wrong-axis and missing-scale bugs from their symptoms in training curves.","architect":"Decides where lookup happens: inside the model's context window at quadratic cost, or in a retrieval layer that pre-selects what enters the window.","fde":"Uses the dictionary analogy to explain to customers why \"the model attends to your whole document\" is a blend, not a citation, and why grounding needs a retrieval step."},"check":{"q":"Explain why scaled dot-product attention divides scores by √d_k, and what you would observe in\ntraining if a from-scratch implementation forgot to do it.","a":"A dot product of two d_k-dimensional vectors with independent, zero-mean, unit-variance components\nhas variance d_k, so its standard deviation grows with √d_k; at d_k = 64 raw scores spread with standard\ndeviation around 8. Softmax over values that far apart is almost one-hot, and in that regime its gradient\nwith respect to the inputs is near zero, so the weights stop moving and the layers below receive almost\nno signal. Dividing by √d_k restores unit variance regardless of head size. Without it you would see\nattention distributions that are sharply peaked from initialization, a loss that drops briefly then\nplateaus, and performance that gets worse, not better, as you increase the head dimension."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the softmax step provide that raw similarity scores do not?","options":["It makes attention run in linear time over the keys","Positive weights that sum to one, so the output is a convex combination independent of how many keys exist","It removes the need for separate key and value projections","It enforces that each position can only see earlier positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softmax normalizes scores into a distribution, keeping the output's scale stable and the operation smooth and trainable. Causality comes from a mask, not from softmax, and the cost per query is unchanged by it."},{"q":"In the cross-attention of an encoder–decoder translator, where do queries, keys and values come from?","options":["All three from the encoder outputs","Queries from the encoder, keys and values from the decoder","Queries from the decoder state, keys and values from the encoder outputs","All three from the decoder state"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The decoder asks, at each output step, which source positions are relevant; the source positions advertise themselves as keys and hand over their content as values. All-from-the-same-sequence would be self-attention."},{"q":"Which problem did attention originally solve in neural machine translation?","options":["The quadratic cost of processing long sentences","The fixed-size context vector that forced the whole source sentence through one bottleneck","The lack of positional information in the encoder","The vanishing gradient inside a single LSTM cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bahdanau et al. let the decoder look back at all encoder states instead of one summary vector, which fixed the collapse in quality on long sentences. Quadratic cost is a property attention introduced, not one it solved."}]},{"key":"model-internals/self-attention","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks","Olsson et al., 2022 — In-context Learning and Induction Heads"],"why":"The meaning of a token depends on its neighbours: \"bank\" next to \"river\" and \"bank\" next to \"account\"\nneed different representations. A recurrent network builds that context by reading left to right and\naccumulating a state, which is sequential and leaks information over distance. A convolution sees a\nfixed window and needs many stacked layers before distant tokens can interact.\n\nSelf-attention lets every position compute a context-dependent representation from all positions in a\nsingle step. The transformer paper compared layer types on three axes: compute per layer, number of\nsequential operations, and the longest path a signal must travel between two positions. Self-attention\ncosts O(n²·d) per layer against O(n·d²) for recurrence, needs O(1) sequential steps against O(n), and\nhas a path length of O(1) against O(n) for recurrence and O(log n) for dilated convolution. When\nsequence length *n* is smaller than the model width *d*, which held for the sentence-length inputs of\n2017, self-attention is also cheaper per layer than recurrence.\n\nThe deeper reason it won is that the routing is content-based. Which token influences which is decided by\nthe data through learned similarity, not by a fixed topology, so the same layer can implement \"look at\nthe previous token\", \"find the matching bracket\" or \"find the earlier occurrence of this name\". That\nflexibility is what let one architecture serve prose, code, protein sequences and audio tokens without\nredesign. The cost is that nothing is free: the n×n interaction is paid for in compute and memory, and\nthe layer has no notion of order unless you give it one.","how":"### Projections\nFrom the layer input **X** of shape `[n, d]`, three learned projections produce queries, keys and values:\n`Q = X·W_Q`, `K = X·W_K`, `V = X·W_V`. They are separate matrices because a token's roles as asker,\nadvertiser and content are different. If you used **X** directly for all three, every token would match\nitself most strongly, since a vector's dot product with itself is large, and the layer would mostly\nreturn each token unchanged.\n\n### Scores, mask, mix\n`S = Q·Kᵀ / √d_k` is an `[n, n]` matrix whose entry (i, j) says how much position *i* wants to read\nfrom position *j*. For a decoder, a **causal mask** sets `S[i, j] = −∞` for every *j > i*. Then each row\nis passed through softmax and the result multiplies the values: `A = softmax(S)·V`.\n\nTwo design choices carry reasons. The mask is applied *before* softmax with −∞ rather than by zeroing\nweights afterwards because `exp(−∞) = 0` exactly and the remaining weights renormalize over the allowed\npositions; zeroing after softmax would leave rows that no longer sum to one. The mask is causal at all\nbecause training predicts every next token in one pass with teacher forcing; if position *i* could see\nposition *i+1*, the prediction task would be trivial during training and impossible at inference, when\n*i+1* does not exist yet. Encoders such as BERT drop the causal mask and let every token see every\nother. Padding uses the same mechanism: −∞ on padded key positions.\n\n### Shapes and FLOPs for one layer\nCounting a matmul of `[m, k]·[k, p]` as 2·m·k·p FLOPs: the four projections (Q, K, V and the output\nprojection) cost 8·n·d²; `Q·Kᵀ` costs 2·n²·d and `A·V` another 2·n²·d, so the attention core costs\n4·n²·d. The ratio of core to projections is therefore n/(2d). For d = 4096: at n = 4k the ratio is 0.5,\nso the linear layers dominate; at 32k it is 4 and at 128k it is 16, so the quadratic term dominates.\nMemory follows the same shape: the score matrix is n×n per head, and with 32 heads at n = 8,192 in fp16\nthat is 32 × 8192² × 2 bytes ≈ 4.3 GB per layer per sequence if it is ever materialized, which is the\nproblem FlashAttention removes.\n\n### Permutation equivariance\nWith no positional signal, permuting the rows of **X** permutes the rows of the output identically: the\nlayer treats its input as a set. That is why positional encodings exist. A causal mask alone leaks some\norder information, since each token can tell roughly how many tokens precede it, and decoders trained\nwith no explicit positions do learn something usable, but nearly every production model adds an explicit\nscheme (a few recent families drop it in some layers).\n\n### What happens at inference\nUnder the causal mask, the keys and values of earlier positions never change once computed, so they are\ncached. During decoding only one new query row exists: the work per step is `[1, d]·[d, n]` per head,\nO(n·d) rather than O(n²·d), and it is bound by reading the cache from memory rather than by arithmetic.\nPrefill (the whole prompt at once) is the compute-bound n² case; decode is the bandwidth-bound linear\ncase. That split is the backbone of serving economics.\n\n### Patterns that emerge\nTrained models show recurring head types: previous-token heads, heads that attend to delimiters, and\ninduction heads that find an earlier occurrence of the current token and copy what followed it, which is\na mechanism behind in-context learning. Many heads also dump most of their weight on the first token as\nan \"attention sink\": softmax must put its mass somewhere, and a constant, content-free position is a\nconvenient no-op. This matters in practice because evicting the first tokens from a sliding-window cache\nbreaks those heads, which is why streaming schemes keep them.","example":"Causal self-attention written out with explicit shapes, then checked against PyTorch's fused kernel.\nThe explicit version is what you should be able to write on a whiteboard; the fused one is what runs in\nproduction.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nB, n, d, h = 2, 6, 64, 4            # batch, tokens, model width, heads\ndk = d // h\nx = torch.randn(B, n, d)\nWq, Wk, Wv, Wo = (torch.randn(d, d) / d ** 0.5 for _ in range(4))\n\ndef heads(t):                       # [B, n, d] -> [B, h, n, dk]\n    return t.view(B, n, h, dk).transpose(1, 2)\n\nq, k, v = heads(x @ Wq), heads(x @ Wk), heads(x @ Wv)\nS = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2) / dk ** 0.5                     # [B, h, n, n] raw scores\nfuture = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, dtype=torch.bool), diagonal=1)\nS = S.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\"))                    # j > i is invisible to i\nA = S.softmax(dim=-1)                                       # each row sums to 1 over j <= i\nprint(A[0, 0].round(decimals=2))                            # lower-triangular weight matrix\nout = (A @ v).transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d) @ Wo         # merge heads, output projection\n\nref = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\nref = ref.transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d) @ Wo\nprint(torch.allclose(out, ref, atol=1e-5))                  # True: same math, fused kernel\nprint(S.shape, \"scores per head are n x n ->\", n * n, \"entries; at n=8192 that is\", 8192 ** 2)\n```","tradeoffs":["O(1) path length and full parallelism across positions, paid for with O(n²) compute and memory in sequence length; the balance flips against dense attention when n is many times d, which is when sliding windows, sparse patterns or retrieval take over part of the job.","Content-based routing adapts to any structure in the data, but brings no built-in locality bias, so small-data regimes favour architectures with stronger priors and large-data regimes favour attention.","Bidirectional self-attention gives richer per-token representations; causal self-attention gives up the right context in exchange for generation and a reusable KV cache.","Exact softmax attention can retrieve a single specific earlier token (copying, induction); linear attention and state-space models cut cost to O(n) but compress history into a fixed state and give up some of that precise recall."],"pitfalls":["Masking with zeros after softmax, or masking the wrong triangle, lets tokens see the future; the symptom is a training loss that falls suspiciously close to zero followed by nonsense at inference.","Right-padding a batch for a decoder-only model during generation puts pad tokens after the prompt; the symptom is that shorter prompts in a batch produce junk while the longest one is fine. Use left padding with an attention mask.","A fully masked row (every key −∞, as with an all-padding query row) makes softmax return NaN; the symptom is NaN losses that appear only on certain batches.","Costing attention with only the quadratic term or only the linear term; the symptom is a capacity plan that is right at one context length and wrong by multiples at another."],"when":"Self-attention is not optional in a transformer, so the real decisions are around it: how long a context\nyou can afford, whether to use an encoder (bidirectional) or decoder (causal) model for a task, how to\npad and mask batches correctly, and which kernel serves it. Measure the share of attention time in\nyour profiles at your actual sequence lengths before optimizing anything; at short context the linear\nlayers dominate and attention kernels will not help. In interviews, be able to derive the O(n²·d)\ncost and the n/(2d) crossover, explain why the mask uses −∞ before softmax, state why positions must be\nadded explicitly, and show how the KV cache and the prefill/decode split follow from causality.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and debugs the masking and padding logic, chooses left padding for generation, and profiles whether attention or the MLP dominates at the team's real sequence lengths.","architect":"Sets context-length policy from the n/(2d) crossover and the memory curve, and decides where retrieval must shrink inputs instead of paying quadratic attention.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"it works for short inputs and breaks for long ones\" by checking context limits, padding direction and attention memory on their hardware."},"check":{"q":"A model with hidden size 4,096 processes an 8k-token prompt. In the attention layer, do the\nQ/K/V/output projections or the score computation dominate FLOPs, and how does that change at 128k?","a":"Projections cost 8·n·d² and the attention core (QKᵀ plus AV) costs 4·n²·d, so their ratio is\nn/(2d). At n = 8,192 and d = 4,096 the ratio is 1: the two halves are equal, and together with the MLP\n(which is larger than the projections) the linear layers still dominate the layer as a whole. At\nn = 131,072 the ratio is 16, so the quadratic core is sixteen times the projections and dwarfs the MLP\ntoo; the layer is now attention-dominated. The practical consequences follow: at 8k, kernel choices for\nmatmuls and quantizing weights matter most; at 128k, attention kernels, memory for the n×n scores (never\nmaterializing them) and possibly sparse or windowed attention are what determine cost and feasibility."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the causal mask applied as −∞ before the softmax instead of zeroing weights after it?","options":["Because −∞ is faster to compute on GPUs than multiplying by zero","Because exp(−∞) is exactly 0 and the remaining weights renormalize to sum to one over allowed positions","Because softmax cannot accept zeros as inputs","Because zeroing after softmax would leak information from earlier positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Zeroing after softmax leaves rows whose weights no longer sum to one, scaling outputs by how much was masked. Masking the logits lets softmax redistribute probability over the visible positions only."},{"q":"Which property of self-attention makes an explicit positional encoding necessary?","options":["Its quadratic cost in sequence length","The use of separate key and value projections","Permutation equivariance: without positions, reordering the input only reorders the output","The fact that softmax weights sum to one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-attention treats its input as a set; \"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" would yield the same per-token outputs. Positional encodings break that symmetry so order can influence attention."},{"q":"Per the original transformer analysis, when is self-attention cheaper per layer than a recurrent layer?","options":["When sequence length n is smaller than the representation width d","When sequence length n is larger than the representation width d","Always, regardless of n and d","Never; it is chosen only for parallelism"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Self-attention costs O(n²·d) per layer and recurrence O(n·d²), so attention is cheaper when n < d. At long contexts recurrence is cheaper in FLOPs, and attention is kept for its path length and parallelism."}]},{"key":"model-internals/multi-head-attention","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Michel et al., 2019 — Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","Bhojanapalli et al., 2020 — Low-Rank Bottleneck in Multi-head Attention Models"],"why":"A single attention head produces, for each query position, one probability distribution over the\nsequence and one blended output. Consider the verb in \"The keys that the engineer lost were on the\ntable\": to represent it well the layer wants to look at \"keys\" for number agreement, at \"engineer\" for\nwho did the losing, and at \"table\" for where things ended up. Those are different relations with\ndifferent relevant positions, and one softmax asked to serve all three at once averages them into a\nblur.\n\nThere is a second limitation. A dot product in a single d_model-dimensional space tests one notion of\nsimilarity. \"Is this the subject of my clause\" and \"is this the token right before me\" are different\ncomparisons that want different subspaces.\n\nThe naive fixes are poor. Stacking more layers so each layer picks one relation serializes work that\ncould be parallel and deepens the network. Widening d_k does not help at all, because one softmax still\nyields one weighting. The transformer paper's answer was to project the input into *h* = 8 subspaces of\n64 dimensions each, attend independently in each, concatenate the eight 64-dimensional outputs back to\n512, and mix them with an output projection. Since each head's score computation costs n²·d_k and\nh·d_k = d_model, the total matches a single full-width head, and the paper found that several heads with\nthe same parameter budget beat one head measurably. The design has survived unchanged in its essentials.","how":"### Shapes\nFrom input **X** `[n, d]`, head *i* uses its own slices of the projection matrices:\n`Q_i = X·W_Q^i`, `K_i = X·W_K^i`, `V_i = X·W_V^i`, each `[n, d_k]`, then\n`head_i = softmax(Q_i·K_iᵀ / √d_k)·V_i`. The outputs are concatenated into `[n, h·d_k]` and multiplied by\n`W_O` of shape `[h·d_k, d]`. In code the per-head matrices are not separate: one fused `W_QKV` of shape\n`[d, 3d]` is applied, the result is reshaped from `[B, n, 3d]` to `[B, 3, h, n, d_k]`, and a batched matmul\nover the head axis does all heads in one kernel. The heads are a *view* of one big matmul, not eight\nsmall modules.\n\nTypical sizes: d_k = 64 in the 2017 paper; 128 in Llama-2-7B (4096 / 32), Llama-3-8B and most current\nopen models, partly because fused attention kernels are tuned for head dimensions of 64, 128 and 256.\n\n### Why the output projection\nConcatenation only lays the heads' outputs side by side. Without `W_O`, head *i* could write only into\ndimensions `i·d_k … (i+1)·d_k` of the residual stream, tying each head to a fixed slot forever. `W_O`\nlets information from all heads combine and land anywhere in the stream, and it is where a head's\ncontribution can be scaled up or down.\n\n### Parameter and FLOP accounting\nPer layer, `W_Q`, `W_K`, `W_V` and `W_O` are each `d × d`, giving 4d² parameters regardless of *h*. Score\nFLOPs are h × 2·n²·d_k = 2·n²·d, again independent of *h*. Three things do change with *h*:\n- the materialized attention-probability tensor, `[B, h, n, n]`, grows linearly with *h*; at h = 32,\n  n = 8,192, fp16 it is about 4.3 GB per layer per sequence, which is why fused kernels that never\n  materialize it matter;\n- the rank of each head's attention pattern is bounded by d_k, so more heads means each is individually\n  weaker;\n- the KV cache per token per layer is 2·(number of KV heads)·d_k, which equals 2d for full multi-head\n  attention and is only reduced when KV heads are shared, the subject of grouped-query attention.\n\n### What heads learn\nInterpretability work finds recognizable specialists: previous-token heads, heads that track syntactic\nroles, heads that attend to delimiters or the first token as a sink, and induction heads that find an\nearlier copy of the current token and attend to what followed it. It also finds redundancy: Michel et\nal. showed that a large fraction of heads in trained translation and BERT models can be removed at test\ntime with little loss. That redundancy is part of why sharing keys and values across heads (GQA) costs\nso little quality.\n\n### Choosing h and d_k\nMore heads means more distinct relations per layer; smaller d_k means each head's score matrix has lower\nrank and sharper patterns become harder to express. Hardware adds constraints: d_k should be a value the\nattention kernels are tuned for, and *h* should be divisible by the tensor-parallel degree, because heads\nare the natural unit to shard across GPUs with no communication inside the attention block.","example":"A complete multi-head attention module. The loop at the bottom shows that changing *h* from 1 to 64 leaves\nthe parameter count at exactly 4·512² = 1,048,576 and changes only the shape of the attention tensor.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn\n\nclass MHA(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d, h):\n        super().__init__()\n        assert d % h == 0\n        self.h, self.dk = h, d // h\n        self.qkv = nn.Linear(d, 3 * d, bias=False)      # W_Q | W_K | W_V fused into one matmul\n        self.o = nn.Linear(d, d, bias=False)            # W_O: lets heads mix and land anywhere\n\n    def forward(self, x, causal=True):                  # x: [B, n, d]\n        B, n, d = x.shape\n        q, k, v = self.qkv(x).chunk(3, dim=-1)          # each [B, n, d]\n        split = lambda t: t.view(B, n, self.h, self.dk).transpose(1, 2)   # -> [B, h, n, dk]\n        q, k, v = split(q), split(k), split(v)\n        scores = q @ k.transpose(-2, -1) / self.dk ** 0.5                  # [B, h, n, n]\n        if causal:\n            future = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, dtype=torch.bool), 1)\n            scores = scores.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\"))\n        attn = scores.softmax(-1)                                          # one distribution per head\n        out = (attn @ v).transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, n, d)                  # concat heads\n        return self.o(out), attn\n\nx = torch.randn(2, 10, 512)\nfor h in (1, 8, 64):\n    m = MHA(512, h)\n    y, attn = m(x)\n    print(f\"h={h:2d} head_dim={512 // h:3d} params={sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters()):,} \"\n          f\"attn={tuple(attn.shape)} out={tuple(y.shape)}\")\n# h= 1 head_dim=512 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 1, 10, 10)  out=(2, 10, 512)\n# h= 8 head_dim= 64 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 8, 10, 10)  out=(2, 10, 512)\n# h=64 head_dim=  8 params=1,048,576 attn=(2, 64, 10, 10) out=(2, 10, 512)\n```","tradeoffs":["More heads give more parallel relations per layer, but each head's attention matrix has rank at most d_k, so past a point extra heads buy diversity at the price of per-head expressiveness; most current models settle at d_k = 128.","Same FLOPs and parameters as a single wide head, yet the attention-probability tensor is *h* times larger; this is harmless with fused kernels and a memory wall without them.","Heads shard across GPUs with no communication inside attention, which makes tensor parallelism simple, but *h* (and the number of KV heads) must divide by the parallel degree, which constrains hardware layouts.","Redundant heads make the model robust to pruning and to KV sharing, but mean some attention compute at inference is doing little useful work."],"pitfalls":["Reshaping `[B, n, h·d_k]` straight to `[B, h, n, d_k]` without the transpose, so heads mix positions instead of channels; the symptom is a model that trains without error but learns slowly and produces incoherent output.","Picking a head dimension the attention kernels are not tuned for (anything outside 64/128/256, or not a multiple of 8); the symptom is a silent fall-back to a slow path and throughput several times lower than the same model at d_k = 128.","Leaving out `W_O`, or applying it per head; the symptom is subtle, usually that adding heads stops helping because they cannot combine.","Explaining a prediction with one head's attention map; the symptom is contradictory stories from different heads and layers for the same output."],"when":"Multi-head attention is a given inside any transformer; what you decide is the split. When reading a\nmodel config, `num_attention_heads`, `num_key_value_heads` and `head_dim` tell you the KV cache per\ntoken, the shard granularity for tensor parallelism and whether fast kernels apply. When designing or\nfine-tuning a small model, keep d_k at a kernel-friendly size and choose *h* from that rather than the\nother way round. When debugging a from-scratch implementation, test with h = 1 first, then confirm the\nreshape by checking that the per-head output for a given head matches a single-head computation on that\nslice. In interviews, expect to explain why several heads beat one wide head at equal cost, what `W_O`\nis for, and which quantities do and do not change with *h*.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Gets the view/transpose right, keeps head_dim kernel-friendly, and reads head counts off a config to size caches and pick a tensor-parallel degree.","architect":"Treats head layout as a hardware constraint: the number of query and KV heads bounds how a model can be sharded across the GPUs the team actually owns.","fde":"Explains why a customer's chosen model cannot be split evenly across their 6-GPU node and what the options are, using head counts as the argument."},"check":{"q":"You change a 4,096-wide attention layer from 8 heads to 32 heads. State what happens to the\nparameter count, the FLOPs, the attention memory and the per-head expressiveness, and why.","a":"Parameters stay at 4d² ≈ 67M because W_Q, W_K, W_V and W_O remain d×d; the heads are slices of\nthe same matrices. FLOPs stay the same because h × 2·n²·d_k = 2·n²·d regardless of the split. The\nmaterialized attention tensor `[B, h, n, n]` grows four times, from 8 to 32 n×n maps, which matters if\nyou use a kernel that writes it to memory and is irrelevant with FlashAttention. Each head's d_k drops\nfrom 512 to 128, so each attention pattern is lower rank and individually less expressive, but there are\nfour times as many of them. The KV cache per token is 2d either way for full multi-head attention; it\nonly shrinks if you reduce KV heads through GQA, which the question did not do."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does multi-head attention cost roughly the same as a single head of width d_model?","options":["Because heads share their key and value projections","Because each head works in d_model / h dimensions, so h heads together do the work of one full-width head","Because only one head is active for each token","Because the softmax is computed once and shared across heads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting the width across heads keeps the projection matrices d×d and the score FLOPs at 2·n²·d. Sharing K/V projections across heads is grouped-query attention, a separate idea."},{"q":"What is the role of the output projection W_O?","options":["It normalizes the attention weights so each row sums to one","It applies the causal mask to the concatenated heads","It mixes information across heads and maps the concatenation back into the residual stream","It reduces the KV cache by compressing the heads"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concatenation alone pins each head to a fixed slice of the output; W_O lets heads combine and place their contribution anywhere. Masking and normalization happen inside each head before concatenation."},{"q":"Which quantity grows when you increase the number of heads at fixed d_model?","options":["The number of parameters in the attention layer","The FLOPs of the Q/K/V projections","The size of the materialized attention-probability tensor [B, h, n, n]","The KV cache per token for full multi-head attention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parameters and projection FLOPs depend only on d_model, and the full-MHA KV cache per token is 2d per layer. The n×n probability map is produced once per head, so its memory scales with h."}]},{"key":"model-internals/grouped-query-attention","sources":["Shazeer, 2019 — Fast Transformer Decoding: One Write-Head is All You Need","Ainslie et al., 2023 — GQA: Training Generalized Multi-Query Transformer Models from Multi-Head Checkpoints","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"During decoding each step processes one new token per sequence. The arithmetic is tiny; the time goes\ninto reading the model weights and every cached key and value for every layer from high-bandwidth\nmemory. Time per token is therefore roughly bytes read divided by bandwidth. For a 7B-class model with\nfull multi-head attention the cache costs about 0.5 MB per token, so 64 concurrent 4k-token sequences\nhold ~128 GB of cache against ~14 GB of weights. The cache, not the model, dominates both the memory\nbudget (how many sequences fit) and the traffic per step (how fast each one advances).\n\nShazeer's 2019 multi-query attention attacked exactly this: keep all the query heads, but give the layer\na single key head and a single value head shared by all of them. The cache shrinks by a factor of *h*\n(32× for a 32-head model). The problem is that it is a real loss of capacity; quality drops measurably,\ntraining can be less stable, and an existing multi-head checkpoint cannot be converted without harm.\n\nAinslie et al. observed that the extremes were not the only options. With *g* KV heads, each shared by\nh/g query heads, the cache shrinks by h/g, and at g = 8 for a 64-head model the quality is close to full\nmulti-head while the speed is close to multi-query. The naive alternatives all cost more quality per byte\nsaved: fewer layers cuts capacity everywhere, smaller head dimensions weaken every attention pattern,\nand cache quantization is orthogonal and can be stacked on top. GQA targets the one factor that was\nmostly redundancy, since the key and value projections of neighbouring heads in a trained model are\nsimilar enough to share.","how":"### The grouping\nTake *h* query heads and *g* KV heads with *h* divisible by *g*. The key and value projection matrices\nshrink from `[d, h·d_k]` to `[d, g·d_k]`; the query projection and `W_O` are unchanged. In the forward\npass, **K** and **V** come out as `[B, g, n, d_k]`, and query head *i* attends to KV head `i // (h/g)`.\nImplementations either expand **K** and **V** to `[B, h, n, d_k]` by repeating each KV head h/g times\n(the `repeat_kv` helper in Hugging Face) or, in fused kernels, index the shared head directly without\ncopying. The attention math per head is unchanged. For Llama-3-8B (h = 32, g = 8, d_k = 128, d = 4096)\nthe K and V projections hold 8.4M parameters per layer instead of 33.6M, about 0.8B fewer parameters\nacross 32 layers than an all-MHA layout of the same width would need.\n\n### Cache arithmetic\nPer token, the cache holds `2 × n_layers × g × d_k × bytes_per_element`. For 32 layers, d_k = 128 and\nfp16:\n\n```\nMHA  g = 32 : 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 = 524,288 B = 512 KB per token\nGQA  g = 8  : 2 × 32 ×  8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 B = 128 KB per token\nMQA  g = 1  : 2 × 32 ×  1 × 128 × 2 =  16,384 B =  16 KB per token\n```\n\nAt 64 sequences of 8k tokens that is 256 GB, 64 GB and 8 GB respectively. The GQA workload fits on one\n80 GB card with room for weights; the MHA one does not fit on three.\n\n### Decode speed\nBytes read per decode step ≈ weights + the cache of every sequence in the batch. At batch 64 and 4k\naverage length with an 8B fp16 model: ~16 GB of weights plus ~128 GB of MHA cache, against 16 + 32 GB\nwith GQA. Roughly three times fewer bytes per step means up to roughly three times the decode throughput\nbefore compute becomes the limit; treat the factor as illustrative, since real engines overlap and batch\ndifferently. Prefill is unaffected: it is compute-bound and the attention FLOPs are identical because the\nqueries are not shared.\n\n### Uptraining existing checkpoints\nThe GQA paper's second contribution was conversion: take a multi-head checkpoint, **mean-pool** the key\nand value projection matrices of the heads in each group into one, then continue pre-training for about\n5% of the original compute. Mean pooling is used because it preserves the group's average behaviour\nbetter than keeping one head or re-initializing. That is why GQA could be retrofitted onto already-trained\nmodels; newer families (Llama 3, Mistral) simply train with it from the start.\n\n### Interaction with tensor parallelism\nHeads are the unit sharded across GPUs. With g = 8 KV heads on 8 GPUs each GPU holds one KV head and the\nfour query heads that use it: ideal. With a tensor-parallel degree larger than *g*, KV heads must be\nreplicated so each GPU has the one its query heads need, and the per-GPU cache saving shrinks\naccordingly. Serving engines handle the replication, but the capacity arithmetic changes.\n\n### Beyond GQA\nMulti-head latent attention (DeepSeek-V2) projects keys and values into a low-rank latent vector per\ntoken and caches only that, reconstructing K and V on use; it reports a smaller cache than GQA at\ncomparable or better quality, a vendor claim worth verifying per model. Cache quantization to 8-bit or\n4-bit stacks multiplicatively with any head layout, and sliding-window attention bounds cache *length*\nrather than width.","example":"The first half shows how query heads map onto shared KV heads with a `repeat_kv` expansion; the second\ncomputes the cache table for the three layouts from a config.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef repeat_kv(x, n_rep):                      # [B, g, n, dk] -> [B, g * n_rep, n, dk]\n    B, g, n, dk = x.shape\n    if n_rep == 1:\n        return x\n    return x[:, :, None].expand(B, g, n_rep, n, dk).reshape(B, g * n_rep, n, dk)\n\nB, n, dk, h, g = 1, 5, 128, 32, 8\nq = torch.randn(B, h, n, dk)\nk, v = torch.randn(B, g, n, dk), torch.randn(B, g, n, dk)      # only 8 KV heads are ever cached\nk_full, v_full = repeat_kv(k, h // g), repeat_kv(v, h // g)   # query head i uses KV head i // 4\nout = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k_full, v_full, is_causal=True)\nprint(out.shape, torch.equal(k_full[:, 0], k_full[:, 3]), torch.equal(k_full[:, 3], k_full[:, 4]))\n# torch.Size([1, 32, 5, 128]) True False   -> heads 0..3 share KV head 0; head 4 starts group 1\n\ndef kv_bytes_per_token(layers, kv_heads, head_dim, dtype_bytes=2):\n    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes\n\nfor name, kv_heads in ((\"MHA 32 kv heads\", 32), (\"GQA  8 kv heads\", 8), (\"MQA  1 kv head \", 1)):\n    per_tok = kv_bytes_per_token(32, kv_heads, 128)\n    print(f\"{name}: {per_tok // 1024:4d} KB/token, {per_tok * 8192 * 64 / 2**30:6.0f} GiB for 64 x 8k\")\n# MHA 32 kv heads:  512 KB/token,    256 GiB for 64 x 8k\n# GQA  8 kv heads:  128 KB/token,     64 GiB for 64 x 8k\n# MQA  1 kv head :   16 KB/token,      8 GiB for 64 x 8k\n```","tradeoffs":["Cache size and decode bytes drop by h/g, paid for with a small quality loss; the trade moves toward MQA when memory is the hard limit (edge devices, extreme batch) and back toward full MHA for small models at short context where the cache was never the constraint.","Fewer K/V parameters save a little memory and prefill compute, but the attention FLOPs are unchanged because queries are not shared, so GQA is nearly invisible in time-to-first-token and only shows in per-token decode latency and throughput.","Uptraining lets a team retrofit GQA onto a model they already have, but it costs around 5% of pre-training compute plus the data pipeline; for almost everyone the right move is to pick a model that was trained with GQA.","The KV-head count sets the finest tensor-parallel split without replication, so g = 8 fits 8-GPU nodes neatly and awkwardly fits anything else."],"pitfalls":["Using `num_attention_heads` instead of `num_key_value_heads` in a capacity estimate; the symptom is a cache budget four times too large, and a cluster sized or rejected on the wrong number.","Expecting GQA to cut time-to-first-token; the symptom is an unchanged TTFT after switching models while only throughput and per-token latency improved.","Materializing `repeat_kv` copies in a custom attention path at prefill; the symptom is transient activation memory as large as MHA even though the cache itself is small.","Converting an MHA checkpoint to a GQA layout by slicing or averaging heads without the uptraining step; the symptom is perplexity that jumps by multiples on the first evaluation.","Tensor-parallel degree larger than the KV-head count; the symptom is per-GPU cache memory that does not shrink as expected when you add GPUs."],"when":"When choosing a model to serve at long context or high concurrency, read `num_key_value_heads` before\nreading the benchmark table, and compute cache-per-token for your sequence lengths; two models with\nequal quality on your evaluation can differ by four in how many users one GPU serves. Prefer GQA models\nfor chat and agent workloads where decode dominates; the difference matters less for short-prompt\nclassification where prefill dominates. If you train or fine-tune, keep the layout the checkpoint was\ntrained with. Measure tokens per second per GPU at your target batch, not single-stream latency, because\nthat is where the saving appears. In interviews, derive the cache formula, explain why decode speed\nfollows bytes rather than FLOPs, and place MHA, GQA and MQA on that line.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Plugs `num_key_value_heads` into the cache estimator, sets batch and context limits in the serving engine from it, and checks that the kernel path indexes shared heads instead of copying.","architect":"Decides model families and GPU counts with the KV-head count as a first-class input, since it fixes both cache capacity and how evenly the model shards.","fde":"Shows a customer why the GQA model serves four times their concurrent users on the same hardware as the MHA model they benchmarked, with the per-token bytes as the evidence."},"check":{"q":"Two 7B-class models score identically on your evaluation. One uses full multi-head attention with\n32 KV heads, the other GQA with 8. You serve 4k-token chats on one 80 GB GPU. Quantify the difference and\nsay what else you would check.","a":"Both have 32 layers and head_dim 128, so the cache per token is 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 bytes\n= 512 KB for MHA and 128 KB for GQA. A 4k chat costs 2 GB versus 0.5 GB. After ~14 GB of fp16 weights,\nroughly 66 GB remain, so about 30 MHA chats fit against about 130 GQA chats, before fragmentation and\nactivations. Per decode step, bytes read drop from weights plus 2 GB per active chat to weights plus\n0.5 GB, so at equal batch the GQA model generates tokens faster, and at equal memory it serves four times\nthe concurrency. I would also check that the two evaluations covered long-context behaviour, that the\nserving engine actually uses a GQA-aware kernel, and that my tensor-parallel plan divides 8 KV heads\nevenly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does grouped-query attention primarily reduce?","options":["The number of query heads and therefore attention FLOPs","The number of key/value heads, and with them the KV cache size and bytes read per decode step","The number of transformer layers needed for a given quality","The size of the vocabulary embedding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Queries stay at full head count; only keys and values are shared across groups. The win is memory and bandwidth during decoding, not arithmetic, which is why prefill speed is essentially unchanged."},{"q":"Llama-3-8B has 32 query heads and 8 KV heads. How many query heads share each KV head?","options":["1","2","4","8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group size is 32 / 8 = 4: query heads 0–3 use KV head 0, 4–7 use KV head 1, and so on. The cache is therefore a quarter of what full multi-head attention would need."},{"q":"Why does GQA give almost no improvement to time-to-first-token?","options":["Because the prompt is processed without attention","Because the cache is only created after the first token","Because prefill is compute-bound and the attention FLOPs are unchanged when only K and V are shared","Because the output projection dominates prefill time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prefill multiplies large matrices and is limited by arithmetic, not by reading the cache. Sharing K/V heads changes how many bytes decode must read per step, which is where GQA's gain appears."}]},{"key":"model-internals/flashattention","sources":["Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Dao, 2023 — FlashAttention-2: Faster Attention with Better Parallelism and Work Partitioning","Milakov and Gimelshein, 2018 — Online normalizer calculation for softmax"],"why":"The standard implementation of attention does three things in sequence: `S = Q·Kᵀ`, `P = softmax(S)`,\n`O = P·V`. Each step is a separate kernel, so **S** is written to GPU memory, read back for the softmax,\n**P** is written, then read again for the final matmul. For 32 heads at 8k tokens in fp16, **S** alone is\nabout 4.3 GB per layer, written and re-read several times per forward pass and again in backward.\n\nThe FLOPs of attention are modest, about 4·n²·d per layer, and the two matmuls run on tensor cores. The\ntrouble is everything between them. A GPU like the A100 can do on the order of 300 TFLOPs in fp16 but\nmove only about 1.5–2 TB/s from HBM, so an operation needs well over a hundred FLOPs per byte to be\ncompute-bound. Softmax, masking and dropout do about one FLOP per byte. They are purely memory-bound,\nand the n² traffic they generate dominated attention time while the n² memory capped sequence length;\nmodels of that era trained at 512–2,048 tokens.\n\nThe naive fix was approximation: sparse patterns, low-rank projections, kernel feature maps. They reduce\nFLOPs, but they often did not reduce wall-clock time because the memory traffic stayed, and they changed\nthe model's behaviour. FlashAttention's insight was that the bottleneck was IO, so the right thing to\noptimize was the number of HBM reads and writes, the way external-memory algorithms are designed, while\nleaving the math exact.","how":"### The memory hierarchy the kernel is written for\nHBM is large and slow relative to compute: tens of gigabytes at a few TB/s. On-chip SRAM (shared memory\nper streaming multiprocessor) is tiny, around 20 MB in total on an A100 in the paper's accounting, but an\norder of magnitude faster. Kernel fusion means doing as much work as possible on data while it sits in\nSRAM before writing anything back.\n\n### Tiling\nQueries are split into blocks of B_r rows and keys and values into blocks of B_c rows, sized so that one\nblock of each fits in SRAM. For each query block, the kernel iterates over the key/value blocks: load\nK_j and V_j, compute the partial scores `S_ij = Q_i·K_jᵀ` (a B_r × B_c tile that lives in SRAM), fold it\ninto the running output, and move on. The full **S** and **P** never exist in HBM.\n\n### Online softmax\nSoftmax needs each row's maximum and the sum of exponentials over *all* n columns, which are unknown\nuntil the last block. The trick, from Milakov and Gimelshein, is to maintain per row a running max *m*\nand running denominator *l*, and to rescale when a new block raises the max:\n\n```\nm_new = max(m, rowmax(S_ij))\nl_new = exp(m - m_new) · l + rowsum(exp(S_ij - m_new))\nO_new = exp(m - m_new) · O + exp(S_ij - m_new) · V_j\n```\n\nEverything accumulated so far was computed relative to the old max, and multiplying by `exp(m − m_new)`\nre-expresses it relative to the new one. After the last block, `O / l` is the exact softmax-weighted sum;\nit is a re-association of the same sum, not an approximation. FlashAttention-2 defers the division by\n*l* to the very end to cut non-matmul work.\n\n### Backward pass by recomputation\nA standard backward pass needs **P** saved from the forward pass, n² per head. FlashAttention saves only\nthe output **O** and one number per row, the log-sum-exp `L = m + log l`. In backward it recomputes the\ntiles of **S** and **P** from **Q**, **K** and *L* on the fly, inside SRAM. That costs extra FLOPs, roughly\none more matmul's worth, yet it is faster than the standard backward because recomputation is cheaper\nthan reading n² values from HBM. It is the same compute-for-memory trade as gradient checkpointing,\napplied at the kernel level.\n\n### IO complexity\nThe paper counts HBM accesses: the standard algorithm needs Θ(n·d + n²); FlashAttention needs\nΘ(n²·d² / M), where *M* is the SRAM size. For typical head dimensions (64–128) and SRAM sizes, that is\nmany times fewer, and the measured speedups follow the IO count rather than the FLOP count.\n\n### Versions\nFlashAttention (2022) is the algorithm above. FlashAttention-2 (2023) parallelizes across the sequence\ndimension as well as batch and heads, so long sequences at small batch still fill the GPU, reduces\nnon-matmul FLOPs and partitions work between warps to cut shared-memory traffic; it roughly doubled\nthroughput over the first version. FlashAttention-3 (2024) targets Hopper GPUs with asynchronous\noverlap of tensor-core matmuls and softmax, and adds FP8 support. Exact figures vary by shape and\nhardware, so measure on yours.\n\n### Where you meet it\nPyTorch's `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` dispatches to a flash kernel when dtype, head dimension and\nmask allow, otherwise to a memory-efficient or plain math backend. Hugging Face exposes\n`attn_implementation=\"flash_attention_2\"` or `\"sdpa\"`. vLLM and SGLang use FlashAttention-family or\nFlashInfer kernels, and PagedAttention is FlashAttention-style tiling where the K/V tiles are fetched\nthrough a block table instead of a contiguous buffer.\n\n### What it does not do\nIt is exact softmax attention, so the FLOPs are still quadratic; it changes the constant and the memory,\nnot the asymptotics. FlashAttention-2 requires fp16 or bf16 inputs, head dimensions up to 256 and Ampere\nor newer GPUs. It supports causal, sliding-window, variable-length (packed) sequences and ALiBi slopes,\nbut not an arbitrary dense additive mask; such masks fall back to other backends. PyTorch's FlexAttention\ncompiles custom mask patterns into fused kernels for the cases FlashAttention does not cover.","example":"Naive attention against the flash backend on one GPU, comparing peak memory, time and the outputs. The\nnaive version allocates three ~4.3 GB tensors in sequence (scores, masked scores, probabilities), which\nare all live at once at the softmax; the flash version adds only a few hundred megabytes beyond the inputs.\n\n```python\nimport time, torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom torch.nn.attention import sdpa_kernel, SDPBackend          # PyTorch 2.3+\n\nB, h, n, dk = 1, 32, 8192, 128\nq, k, v = (torch.randn(B, h, n, dk, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float16) for _ in range(3))\nfuture = torch.triu(torch.ones(n, n, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bool), 1)\n\ndef naive():\n    s = (q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)) / dk ** 0.5             # [1, 32, 8192, 8192] fp16 -> ~4.3 GB in HBM\n    p = s.masked_fill(future, float(\"-inf\")).softmax(-1)  # another ~4.3 GB, read back for the matmul\n    return p @ v\n\ndef flash():\n    with sdpa_kernel(SDPBackend.FLASH_ATTENTION):          # raises if the flash kernel cannot be used\n        return F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True)\n\nfor name, fn in ((\"naive\", naive), (\"flash\", flash)):\n    torch.cuda.synchronize(); torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    fn(); torch.cuda.synchronize(); t = time.perf_counter()\n    for _ in range(5):\n        out = fn()\n    torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    print(f\"{name}: {(time.perf_counter() - t) / 5 * 1e3:6.1f} ms/call  \"\n          f\"peak {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30:5.1f} GiB\")\n\nprint(torch.allclose(naive(), flash(), atol=2e-3, rtol=2e-3))   # True: same result up to fp16 rounding\n# Expect roughly 12 GiB peak for naive (3 x 4 GiB plus inputs) versus well under 1 GiB for flash, and flash several times faster;\n# the exact numbers depend on the GPU. Passing an explicit attn_mask instead of is_causal would disable flash.\n```","tradeoffs":["Exact results with O(n) extra memory instead of O(n²), paid for with constraints on dtype (fp16/bf16), head dimension and GPU generation; the trade flips only when you genuinely need fp32 attention or a dense custom bias the kernel does not support.","The backward pass recomputes probabilities rather than storing them, which is more FLOPs for less memory; at very short sequences, where the n² term is small anyway, the gain over a good fused baseline is modest.","The fused kernel is a black box that never materializes attention weights, so interpretability and debugging that need the maps must run a separate, slow eager path.","It makes long contexts fit, but it does not make them cheap: compute is still quadratic, so beyond roughly 100k tokens you still need windowing, sparsity, sequence parallelism or retrieval."],"pitfalls":["Believing the flash path is active when it silently fell back to the math backend because of fp32 inputs, an odd head dimension or an explicit dense mask; the symptom is memory that scales with n² and no speedup. Force the backend with `sdpa_kernel` during testing so it fails loudly.","Passing an explicit causal mask tensor instead of `is_causal=True`; the symptom is the same n² memory curve because the kernel cannot use the fast path.","Comparing flash and eager outputs with fp32-level tolerances; the symptom is spurious \"mismatch\" alarms caused by the different summation order under fp16 rounding.","Leaving `output_attentions=True` or an eager attention implementation in a production config; the symptom is throughput that collapses and OOMs at lengths the model handled in testing.","Padding batches to the longest sequence and running flash on dense tensors; the symptom is GPU time that scales with the longest prompt rather than the total number of real tokens. Use variable-length (packed) attention or a continuous-batching engine."],"when":"Use it whenever it is available, which in practice means ensuring you have not accidentally disabled it:\nkeep inputs in bf16 or fp16, head dimensions in the supported set, masks expressed as `is_causal` or\nsliding-window parameters rather than dense tensors, and sequences packed rather than padded. Verify\nwith a profiler that the attention kernels carry flash names and that peak memory grows linearly with\nsequence length. When planning hardware, note that FlashAttention-2 needs Ampere or newer and the\nHopper-specific version needs H100-class parts, which affects whether a customer's older GPUs can run\nlong-context workloads. In interviews, the expected story is: attention was memory-bound, tiling plus\nonline softmax avoids the n² matrix, recomputation makes backward fit, and all of it is exact.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the model on the fast path (dtype, head_dim, mask form, packing) and checks kernel names and memory curves in the profiler rather than trusting a config flag.","architect":"Sets maximum context and GPU generation knowing that long-context feasibility depends on these kernels existing for the target hardware, and decides when exact attention must give way to windowed or retrieval-based designs.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's out-of-memory at long prompts on V100 or T4 cards, where the flash kernels are unavailable, and explains the fp16 and hardware prerequisites."},"check":{"q":"At 2k context, attention is a small fraction of a transformer's total FLOPs, yet FlashAttention\ndelivered large end-to-end training speedups. Explain why.","a":"FLOP share is the wrong lens for a memory-bound operation. Standard attention writes the n×n\nscores to HBM, reads them for softmax, writes the probabilities, reads them again for the value matmul,\nand repeats most of that in backward. Those elementwise passes do about one FLOP per byte moved, far\nbelow the ratio a GPU needs to be compute-bound, so the streaming multiprocessors idle while waiting on\nmemory, and attention takes wall-clock time out of proportion to its arithmetic. Fusing the steps and\nkeeping the tiles in SRAM removes that traffic, and storing only O(n) state per head frees memory, which\nlets you raise batch size or sequence length and improves utilization of the matmul-heavy layers too.\nThe speedup is a bandwidth saving, not a FLOP saving."},"quiz":[{"q":"FlashAttention is faster than standard attention mainly because it","options":["Computes fewer floating-point operations by approximating the softmax","Performs far fewer reads and writes to high-bandwidth memory by never materializing the n×n matrix","Uses lower precision than the standard implementation","Skips attention for positions far from the query"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The math is exact and the FLOPs are slightly higher because of recomputation; the win is IO. Tiling keeps score tiles in SRAM and avoids writing and re-reading the quadratic matrix."},{"q":"What allows the softmax to be computed block by block without seeing a whole row first?","options":["Rows are sorted before processing so the maximum comes first","Each block's softmax is computed independently and averaged","A running row maximum and running denominator are kept and earlier partial sums are rescaled when the maximum changes","The softmax is replaced by a linear normalization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Online softmax maintains m and l per row and multiplies accumulated terms by exp(m_old − m_new) when a new block raises the maximum, which reproduces the exact result after the last block."},{"q":"What does the FlashAttention backward pass store instead of the n×n probability matrix?","options":["Nothing; it recomputes everything including the forward output","The forward output and one log-sum-exp value per row, recomputing probability tiles on the fly","A compressed low-rank copy of the probability matrix","Only the attention scores of the diagonal blocks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Keeping O and the per-row log-sum-exp L is enough to regenerate any tile of P from Q and K inside SRAM, which costs some FLOPs but avoids storing and reading n² values from memory."}]},{"key":"model-internals/positional-encoding","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Su et al., 2021 — RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding","Press et al., 2022 — Train Short, Test Long: Attention with Linear Biases Enables Input Length Extrapolation","Chen et al., 2023 — Extending Context Window of Large Language Models via Positional Interpolation"],"why":"Without position information, self-attention gives identical per-token outputs for \"dog bites man\" and\n\"man bites dog\"; permuting the input permutes the output and nothing else. Order has to be injected, and\na good scheme has to satisfy several demands at once: every position must be distinguishable; the model\nmust be able to learn *relative* offsets, because most linguistic structure is local (adjective before\nnoun, matching brackets, the previous token); it should degrade gracefully past the lengths seen in\ntraining; and it must be cheap and compatible with caching keys and values.\n\nThe original transformer added fixed sinusoidal vectors to the embeddings, chosen because a shift by\n*k* positions is a linear function of the sinusoids, so relative offsets are in principle learnable.\nBut the signal is added once at the input, gets mixed into values and content, and the model has to\ndisentangle it. GPT-2 and BERT replaced sinusoids with a learned table, one vector per position, which\nis simple and strong at trained lengths but hard-caps the context at the table size and leaves rarely\nseen positions undertrained. Relative-position schemes (Shaw et al., the T5 bias) add a learned bias per\noffset directly to the attention scores, a better inductive bias, but they bucket offsets, add a term to\nthe attention kernel and still extrapolate poorly.\n\nRoPE's move is to encode absolute position as a rotation of the query and key vectors, so that the score\n`qₘ·kₙ` depends only on `n − m` and the contents. It needs no parameters, adds nothing to the score\ncomputation beyond a cheap elementwise rotation, works with any attention kernel, and lets cached keys\nstay valid as the sequence grows. ALiBi, the main alternative, instead adds a fixed distance penalty to\nthe scores; it extrapolates better out of the box but imposes a recency prior that can hurt long-range\nretrieval. The open-model ecosystem converged on RoPE.","how":"### The rotation\nSplit the head dimension *d* into d/2 pairs. Pair *i* is assigned a frequency `θ_i = base^(−2i/d)`, with\nbase = 10,000 in the original paper and Llama 2 and 500,000 in Llama 3. A vector at position *m* has each\npair rotated by the angle `m·θ_i`:\n\n```\n[x, y] -> [x·cos(mθ_i) − y·sin(mθ_i),  x·sin(mθ_i) + y·cos(mθ_i)]\n```\n\nThis is applied to queries and keys, **not** to values. Why the dot product becomes relative: a 2-D\nrotation matrix satisfies `R_mᵀ·R_n = R_(n−m)`, so `(R_m q)·(R_n k) = q·R_(n−m) k`. The absolute positions\ncancel; only the offset and the contents remain. Values are left alone because they carry content that\nis summed into the output; rotating them would scramble what is retrieved by where it sits, whereas\npositions should only influence *who attends to whom*.\n\n### Frequencies and what they encode\nPair 0 rotates one radian per token and wraps every ~6 tokens, so it resolves very local order. The\nslowest pair, with d = 128 and base 10,000, turns by about 27° across 4,096 tokens and completes a full\nrotation only every ~54,000 tokens, so within a typical context it barely moves and acts as a\nnear-absolute, long-range channel. Raising the base to 500,000 slows every frequency; the slowest pair\nthen needs millions of tokens for a full turn. The spread of frequencies lets different dimensions\nspecialize in different ranges, the same geometric schedule the sinusoidal encoding used additively.\nThe RoPE paper also shows that the inner product between a fixed query and key decays with distance on\naverage, a built-in mild preference for nearby tokens.\n\n### Implementation\nTwo equivalent layouts exist: interleaved pairs `(2i, 2i+1)` as in the paper and in Meta's reference\nLlama code (which multiplies complex numbers), or \"rotate-half\", which pairs dimension *i* with\n*i + d/2* and computes `q·cos + rotate_half(q)·sin`, as in the Hugging Face Llama implementation. They\ndiffer by a permutation of dimensions, and the permutation is baked into trained `W_Q` and `W_K`, so\nconverting checkpoints between layouts requires permuting those weights; the official conversion script\ndoes exactly that, and forgetting it is a classic porting bug. The cos/sin tables `[max_len, d/2]` are precomputed, usually in fp32, and applied\nafter the projections and before the score matmul.\n\n### Interaction with the KV cache\nEach key is rotated by its own absolute position when it is inserted into the cache, and each query by\nits position at query time; the relative property emerges in the dot product, so nothing in the cache\nneeds re-rotation as the sequence grows. The consequences follow: evicting tokens from the front of a\nsliding-window cache leaves the survivors at their original absolute angles (streaming schemes re-assign\npositions inside the cache for this reason), and editing anything before position *i* invalidates every\ncached key from *i* onward.\n\n### Context extension\nPast the training length, the low-frequency pairs reach angles the model never saw, attention patterns\nbreak and perplexity rises sharply within a few hundred tokens of the limit. Position interpolation\n(Chen et al.) rescales positions by `L_train / L_new` so all angles stay inside the trained range, then\nfine-tunes briefly; it works but squeezes the high-frequency pairs, costing resolution between adjacent\ntokens. NTK-aware scaling instead raises the base, which stretches low frequencies a lot and high ones\nlittle. YaRN combines per-frequency treatment, leaving high frequencies untouched and interpolating low\nones, with a temperature on the logits, and needs fewer fine-tuning tokens. Llama 3.1's 128k context uses\na frequency-dependent scaling of this family on top of an 8k-trained base. The common lesson is that\n\"max context\" is a property of the trained angles, not of a config field.\n\n### ALiBi, briefly\nALiBi adds `−slope_h·(i − j)` to the score of query *i* and key *j ≤ i*, with per-head slopes forming a\ngeometric series. No parameters, decent extrapolation, used by BLOOM and MPT; its recency bias is the\ntrade.","example":"A minimal RoPE in NumPy that demonstrates the relative property numerically and shows why extension\nbeyond the trained length needs care.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef rope(x, pos, base=10000.0):\n    \"\"\"Rotate x (even length d) to position pos: pair (2i, 2i+1) turns by pos * theta_i.\"\"\"\n    d = x.shape[-1]\n    theta = base ** (-np.arange(0, d, 2) / d)       # d/2 frequencies from 1 down to ~1/base\n    ang = pos * theta\n    cos, sin = np.cos(ang), np.sin(ang)\n    x1, x2 = x[0::2], x[1::2]\n    out = np.empty_like(x)\n    out[0::2] = x1 * cos - x2 * sin\n    out[1::2] = x1 * sin + x2 * cos\n    return out\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nd = 128\nq, k = rng.standard_normal(d), rng.standard_normal(d)\n\n# Same offset (3 apart), different absolute positions -> identical attention score.\nprint(round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 2), 6), round(rope(q, 1005) @ rope(k, 1002), 6))\n# Different offsets -> different scores although q and k never changed.\nprint(round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 4), 6), round(rope(q, 5) @ rope(k, 0), 6))\n\n# Why extrapolation fails: the slowest pair barely moves inside a 4k window ...\ntheta_min = 10000 ** (-(d - 2) / d)\nprint(f\"slowest pair: {np.degrees(4096 * theta_min):.1f} degrees over 4096 tokens, \"\n      f\"full turn every {2 * np.pi / theta_min:,.0f} tokens\")\n# ... so positions past the trained length put those pairs at angles the model has never seen.\n# Position interpolation: rope(x, pos * L_train / L_new) keeps every pair inside its trained range.\n```","tradeoffs":["RoPE gives relative attention with zero parameters and a cache that never needs re-rotation, but it extrapolates poorly past the trained length; if you need length generalization with no fine-tuning, ALiBi's fixed penalty behaves better out of the box.","Learned absolute tables are simple and strong at trained lengths, with a hard cap on context; they remain reasonable for fixed-length encoders and wrong for anything that must grow.","A larger base (500,000 versus 10,000) improves long-context behaviour by slowing all frequencies, at the cost of less angular resolution between nearby tokens at short range; whether that costs quality depends on model and data, and vendors tune it empirically.","Position interpolation is simple and robust after a short fine-tune but blurs local resolution; YaRN preserves high frequencies and needs fewer tokens but has more knobs to get wrong."],"pitfalls":["Mixing the interleaved and rotate-half layouts when loading ported weights; the symptom is a model with correct shapes and no errors whose perplexity is in the hundreds.","Applying the rotation to values, or after the softmax; the symptom is a model that trains but never learns sharp positional patterns because the relative property is gone.","Running past `max_position_embeddings` with no scaling; the symptom is output that degenerates into repetition or nonsense right after the trained length, with no exception raised.","Computing the cos/sin tables in bf16 or fp16 for large positions; the symptom is slowly degrading quality at high positions caused by rounding of large angles. Keep the frequency computation in fp32.","Setting `rope_scaling` in a serving config without the fine-tuning the method assumes; the symptom is degraded short-context quality and only partial recovery at long context."],"when":"You choose a positional scheme indirectly by choosing a model, but you meet it directly in three places:\nreading `rope_theta` and `rope_scaling` in a config to know what context the model really supports,\nrunning a context-extension project (interpolation or YaRN plus a fine-tune, evaluated at the target\nlength with needle-in-haystack and real tasks, not just perplexity), and debugging ported checkpoints\nor custom kernels where the rotation layout matters. Measure quality at the lengths you will actually\nserve, because the loss past the trained length is a cliff, not a slope. In interviews, be ready to\nderive the relative property from rotation composition, explain why only queries and keys are rotated,\nand describe why extrapolation fails and how interpolation fixes it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `rope_scaling` correctly when serving extended context, verifies the rotation layout when converting weights, and tests at target lengths before shipping.","architect":"Decides whether to buy long context through RoPE scaling plus fine-tuning or through retrieval, knowing that the trained length, not the config, bounds reliable behaviour.","fde":"Answers a customer's \"can we just raise max tokens to 64k?\" by explaining trained angles versus config fields and proposing an evaluated extension or a retrieval design."},"check":{"q":"Explain how RoPE encodes absolute position yet produces relative attention, and why a model trained\nat 4k tokens degrades sharply at 8k if nothing is changed.","a":"Each query and key is rotated, pair of dimensions by pair, through an angle equal to its absolute\nposition times a per-pair frequency. Because rotations compose, the dot product between a query rotated\nby *m* and a key rotated by *n* equals the dot product between the original query and the key rotated by\n*n − m*; the absolute positions cancel and only the offset and contents remain. Values are not rotated so\nretrieved content is unaffected. During training at 4k, the low-frequency pairs only ever sweep a small\narc, so the model's weights are tuned to scores produced within that arc. At positions 4k–8k those pairs\nenter angles never seen, the scores they contribute are effectively random, attention patterns break and\nperplexity rises within a few hundred tokens. Position interpolation rescales positions so 8k maps into\nthe trained arc, then a short fine-tune restores quality."},"quiz":[{"q":"In RoPE, which tensors are rotated?","options":["Queries, keys and values","Queries and keys only","The token embeddings before the first layer","The attention probabilities after the softmax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotating queries and keys makes their dot product depend on relative position. Values are left alone because they carry content that is summed into the output, and positions should affect only who attends to whom."},{"q":"After RoPE, the attention score between a query at position m and a key at position n depends on","options":["m and n separately, as with learned absolute embeddings","The offset n − m and the content of the two vectors","Only the content, with no positional influence","The absolute position of the key alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rotation matrices compose, so R_mᵀ·R_n = R_(n−m) and the absolute positions cancel inside the dot product. Learned absolute tables, by contrast, give the model separate vectors for m and n."},{"q":"Position interpolation extends a RoPE model's context by","options":["Adding new learned position vectors for the extra positions","Increasing the head dimension so more frequencies are available","Scaling positions down so all rotation angles stay within the range seen in training, then fine-tuning briefly","Removing the positional signal for tokens beyond the trained length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Beyond the trained length the slow frequencies reach unseen angles. Compressing positions keeps every pair inside its trained arc, at the cost of finer-grained local resolution, which a short fine-tune largely recovers."}]},{"key":"model-internals/tokenization","sources":["Sennrich et al., 2016 — Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units","Kudo and Richardson, 2018 — SentencePiece: A simple and language independent subword tokenizer and detokenizer for Neural Text Processing","Kudo, 2018 — Subword Regularization: Improving Neural Network Translation Models with Multiple Subword Candidates","Radford et al., 2019 — Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners"],"why":"A language model needs a fixed, finite set of symbols: an embedding row for each and a softmax over all\nof them at every step. Three ways to define the symbols were tried, and two fail. Characters or raw bytes\ngive a tiny vocabulary (256 for bytes) and nothing is ever unknown, but a sequence becomes four to five\ntimes longer than in words, which multiplies attention cost and forces the model to spend capacity\nlearning spelling before it can learn meaning; each token carries very little information. Whole words\ngive short, meaningful sequences but an unbounded vocabulary: every typo, URL, identifier, number and new\ncoinage is a new word, the embedding table explodes, and anything unseen at inference becomes an\n`<unk>` token. Morphologically rich languages such as Finnish or Turkish make the word approach fail even\non ordinary text.\n\nSubword tokenization is the compromise. Frequent words stay whole, rare words split into frequent pieces,\nand a byte or character fallback guarantees any input can be encoded. Sennrich et al. adapted Gage's\n1994 byte-pair compression algorithm to neural translation for exactly the rare-word problem, and\nbyte-level BPE in GPT-2 removed the last unknown-token case. The result bounds the vocabulary (tens of\nthousands to a few hundred thousand entries), keeps sequences short, and makes the embedding and output\nlayers a fixed, affordable size. Everything about cost and context in practice is downstream of this\nchoice.","how":"### BPE training\n1. **Pre-tokenize.** Split the text into chunks with a regular expression, typically at spaces,\n   punctuation and digit runs, so merges never cross a word boundary. GPT-2's pattern attaches the\n   leading space to the word, which is why `\" hello\"` and `\"hello\"` are different tokens; GPT-4-era\n   tokenizers split digit runs into groups of at most three.\n2. **Start from a base vocabulary.** Every character in the corpus, or all 256 byte values for byte-level\n   BPE, so any string can be represented from the start.\n3. **Count adjacent pairs** of symbols across the corpus, weighted by frequency, and **merge the most\n   frequent pair** into a new symbol. Record the merge as a rule.\n4. **Repeat** until the vocabulary reaches the target size: 50,257 for GPT-2, 32,000 for Llama 2,\n   roughly 100k for GPT-4's `cl100k_base`, 128,256 for Llama 3.\n\nThe greedy frequency criterion is a compression objective: each merge removes the most occurrences of a\npair, so the corpus shrinks fastest in token count. It is cheap, deterministic and good enough that\nnothing has displaced it at scale.\n\n### BPE encoding\nTo encode a new string, pre-tokenize it, start each chunk as bytes or characters, and repeatedly apply\nthe merge with the lowest rank (earliest learned) that matches, until no merge applies. The resulting\nsymbols map to ids. Decoding is concatenation; with byte-level BPE the bytes are then decoded as UTF-8.\n\n### SentencePiece\nSentencePiece (Kudo and Richardson) is a library and model format rather than a single algorithm, and it\ndiffers from GPT-style BPE in how it sees text. It operates on the raw Unicode stream with whitespace\ntreated as a normal character (the `▁` symbol, U+2581), so it needs no language-specific pre-tokenizer\nand works unchanged for Chinese, Japanese or Thai, which have no spaces. Decoding is lossless: join the\npieces and replace `▁` with a space. It applies Unicode normalization (NFKC by default), offers a\n`byte_fallback` option so unknown characters decompose into bytes, and ships as one self-contained model\nfile, so there is no separate regex to keep in sync. It trains either BPE or the unigram model. Llama 1\nand 2, T5 and Mistral used SentencePiece models; Llama 3 moved to a tiktoken-style byte-level BPE with a\nlarger vocabulary for better multilingual efficiency.\n\n### The unigram model\nUnigram tokenization (Kudo, 2018), SentencePiece's default, works in the opposite direction from BPE.\nStart from a large candidate vocabulary of frequent substrings, assign each a probability, and define\nthe tokenization of a string as the most probable segmentation under the assumption that tokens are\nindependent (found with the Viterbi algorithm). Then iteratively remove the tokens whose removal hurts\ncorpus likelihood least until the vocabulary reaches its target size. Because it is probabilistic, it can\nsample alternative segmentations during training as a form of data augmentation (subword\nregularization), and its pieces tend to align more with morphology. WordPiece, used by BERT, is a BPE\nvariant that picks merges by likelihood gain rather than raw count and marks word-internal pieces with\n`##`.\n\n### Consequences you will meet\n- **Cost and context are counted in tokens.** English prose runs around four characters or three\n  quarters of a word per token with OpenAI-style tokenizers; code, numbers and non-Latin scripts cost\n  more per character, often several times more (an order of magnitude figure that varies by tokenizer\n  and language). Vocabularies were enlarged in part to fix this.\n- **Numbers.** Llama 2 splits every digit into its own token; GPT-4-era tokenizers group up to three.\n  Arithmetic behaviour and number formatting sensitivity follow from this.\n- **Spaces and case.** A prompt ending in a trailing space pushes the next token off the distribution\n  the model saw, because the space would normally be attached to the following word.\n- **Character-level tasks fail.** Counting letters, reversing strings or spelling rules are hard because\n  the model never sees characters, only chunks.\n- **Glitch tokens.** Tokens that exist in the vocabulary but barely appeared in training produce erratic\n  behaviour; the \"SolidGoldMagikarp\" family in GPT-2/3 vocabularies is the known example.\n- **The tokenizer is part of the model.** Fine-tuning must use the same tokenizer and the same special\n  tokens and chat template; adding tokens means resizing and initializing embeddings; constrained\n  decoding and grammars operate on token boundaries, not characters.","example":"A complete, runnable BPE trainer and encoder in about thirty lines. The corpus is tiny so you can trace\nthe merges by hand; the mechanism is identical to what produced GPT-2's 50k merges.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ncorpus = \"low low low lower lower lowest newer newer newest wider widest\".split()\nwords = Counter(tuple(w) + (\"</w>\",) for w in corpus)      # symbols per word, with end-of-word marker\n\ndef pair_stats(words):\n    stats = Counter()\n    for sym, freq in words.items():\n        for a, b in zip(sym, sym[1:]):\n            stats[(a, b)] += freq\n    return stats\n\ndef apply_merge(seq, pair):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    while i < len(seq):\n        if i < len(seq) - 1 and (seq[i], seq[i + 1]) == pair:\n            out.append(pair[0] + pair[1]); i += 2\n        else:\n            out.append(seq[i]); i += 1\n    return out\n\nmerges = []\nfor step in range(8):                                        # vocab = base symbols + 8 merges\n    stats = pair_stats(words)\n    best = max(stats, key=stats.get)                         # most frequent adjacent pair\n    merges.append(best)\n    words = Counter({tuple(apply_merge(list(s), best)): f for s, f in words.items()})\n    print(step, best, stats[best])\n# 0 ('l','o') 6 | 1 ('lo','w') 6 | 2 ('e','r') 5 | 3 ('er','</w>') 5 | 4 ('low','</w>') 3 | ...\n\ndef encode(word):                                            # apply merges in learned order\n    seq = list(word) + [\"</w>\"]\n    for pair in merges:\n        seq = apply_merge(seq, pair)\n    return seq\n\nprint(encode(\"lowest\"), encode(\"slowest\"))\n# ['low', 'est</w>'] ['s', 'low', 'est</w>']  <- unseen \"slowest\" still encodes, no <unk>\n```\n\nFor real counting use the model's own tokenizer: `tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\").encode(text)`\nfor OpenAI models, `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)(text).input_ids` for open models.","tradeoffs":["A larger vocabulary means fewer tokens per text, so lower cost, longer effective context and faster generation, but a bigger embedding and output layer (128k × 4096 in fp16 is about 1 GB per untied matrix) and rarer tokens that are undertrained; it pays off when the workload is multilingual or code-heavy.","Byte-level BPE can encode anything and never emits unknown tokens, but scripts whose merges were underrepresented in training fall apart into byte fragments; character-level SentencePiece with byte fallback handles CJK more gracefully at the cost of normalization decisions.","BPE is greedy and deterministic; unigram is probabilistic, supports subword regularization and tends toward morphological pieces, which helps most when training a tokenizer and model from scratch on limited data.","Aggressive pre-tokenization (splitting digits, isolating punctuation) makes behaviour on numbers and code more consistent but raises token counts on numeric or structured text."],"pitfalls":["Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer, such as `cl100k_base` for a Llama model; the symptom is context-overflow errors or silently truncated prompts in production although the estimate said they fit.","Ending a prompt with a trailing space or stitching completions at a non-token boundary; the symptom is a first generated token that starts mid-word, doubles a space or is otherwise odd.","Chunking documents by a fixed characters-per-token ratio; the symptom is code or non-English chunks that exceed the embedding model's limit and get truncated without error.","Resizing the vocabulary for new special tokens without initializing the new embedding rows sensibly; the symptom is a loss spike in the first steps of fine-tuning.","Asking the model for character-level work (letter counts, exact string reversal, regex-like validation); the symptom is confident, inconsistent answers that no prompt reliably fixes.","Mismatched chat-template special tokens between training and serving; the symptom is a fine-tuned model that ignores its system prompt or never emits a stop token."],"when":"You do not choose a tokenizer per request, but it shapes several decisions you do make. When selecting a\nmodel for a market, measure tokens per character on a sample of your real corpus across candidate\ntokenizers; the ratio can differ by multiples between languages and drives cost and latency directly.\nBudget prompts and chunk documents with the model's own tokenizer, never a character heuristic. Train a\nnew tokenizer only when you are also training a model from scratch on a domain whose symbols existing\nvocabularies handle badly, such as DNA, logs or a new programming language. In interviews, be ready to\nwalk through the merge loop, contrast byte-level BPE with SentencePiece and unigram, and explain from\ntokenization why a model miscounts letters or why Hindi costs more than English.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Counts tokens with the correct tokenizer before every call, handles chat-template special tokens exactly, and tokenizes before chunking rather than after.","architect":"Builds the cost model per language and content type from measured tokens per character, and weighs tokenizer efficiency alongside benchmark scores when choosing a model.","fde":"Explains to a customer in Japan or India why bills and latency per character exceed the English pilot, and why a chunker tuned on English truncates their documents."},"check":{"q":"A customer's Hindi support assistant costs about three times more per conversation than the\nEnglish pilot on the same model, with the same number of turns. Explain the mechanism and what you would\ndo about it.","a":"The model's tokenizer learned its merges from a corpus dominated by English, so English words map\nto one or two tokens while Devanagari text falls apart into many short pieces or, with byte-level BPE,\ninto byte fragments; the same message costs several times more tokens, and cost, latency and context\nconsumption all scale with tokens. I would first measure tokens per character on a sample of real Hindi\nconversations across the candidate tokenizers to quantify the ratio. Then I would evaluate a model whose\nvocabulary was built with multilingual data, such as one with a larger vocabulary, since fewer tokens\nper message cuts cost and leaves more room in the context window; check that quality holds on their\ntasks; and revisit the chunking and context budgets, which were tuned on English token counts and are\nprobably truncating Hindi content."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does BPE do at each step of training?","options":["Removes the least likely token from a large initial vocabulary","Merges the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols in the pre-tokenized corpus into a new symbol","Splits the longest word in the corpus into two halves","Assigns a probability to every substring and keeps the top-k"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BPE grows a vocabulary bottom-up by greedy frequency merges. Pruning a large vocabulary by likelihood is the unigram model, which SentencePiece also implements and which runs in the opposite direction."},{"q":"What distinguishes SentencePiece from a GPT-style BPE tokenizer?","options":["It can only produce character-level tokens","It requires a language-specific word splitter before training","It treats raw text, including whitespace, as a stream of symbols and trains BPE or unigram models without a pre-tokenizer","It cannot be used for languages that use spaces"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SentencePiece encodes whitespace as the ▁ symbol and segments the raw Unicode stream directly, which is why it works for Japanese or Thai without a word splitter and decodes losslessly."},{"q":"Why do LLMs make mistakes counting the letters in a word?","options":["Because the softmax cannot represent small integers","Because attention cannot look at more than one token at a time","Because the model sees subword tokens, not characters, so the letters inside a token are not individually visible","Because the tokenizer removes repeated letters"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A word like \"strawberry\" arrives as a few chunks whose internal spelling is only indirectly learned. Character-level questions ask the model to recover information the tokenizer collapsed."}]},{"key":"model-internals/embeddings","sources":["Mikolov et al., 2013 — Efficient Estimation of Word Representations in Vector Space","Press & Wolf, 2017 — Using the Output Embedding to Improve Language Models","Reimers & Gurevych, 2019 — Sentence-BERT","Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning"],"why":"A transformer is a stack of matrix multiplications. It cannot consume the string \"cat\"; it needs a\nvector. The obvious encoding is one-hot: a vector of length *V* (the vocabulary size, tens of thousands\nto over a hundred thousand entries) with a single 1. One-hot has two fatal properties. It is enormous\nand almost entirely zero, so the first layer would multiply a 100k-wide sparse vector on every token.\nWorse, every pair of one-hot vectors is equidistant and orthogonal, so \"cat\" is exactly as far from \"kitten\"\nas from \"parliament\". The representation carries no information the model can exploit; every regularity\nwould have to be relearned from scratch for every token.\n\nAn embedding replaces the one-hot with a short dense vector, typically a few hundred to several thousand\ndimensions, whose values are learned. Because the vectors are trained by gradient descent on the task\n(next-token prediction), tokens that behave similarly in context are pushed to similar places, and that\ngeometry is free information for every later layer. The same reasoning applies one level up: if you want\nto find the document most relevant to a question, comparing strings fails on paraphrase, while comparing\nlearned vectors succeeds, because the vectors were trained so that meaning, not spelling, determines\ndistance.","how":"### Token embeddings inside an LLM\nThe model owns a matrix `E` of shape `[V, d_model]`. Looking up token id *i* means taking row *i*; this\nis exactly `one_hot(i) · E`, so an embedding layer is a linear layer applied to a one-hot input, with the\nmultiplication skipped *because* all but one row would be multiplied by zero. The row is a trainable\nparameter like any weight, updated by backpropagation through the whole network. Nothing about the\ngeometry is hand-designed; it emerges because tokens that predict similar continuations receive similar\ngradients.\n\nThe table is a real fraction of the parameter budget. GPT-2 small has 50,257 × 768 ≈ 38.6M embedding\nparameters out of ~124M. Llama-2-7B has 32,000 × 4,096 ≈ 131M; Llama 3's 128,256-token vocabulary at the\nsame width is ≈ 525M. Larger vocabularies tokenize text into fewer tokens (cheaper context, better\nmultilingual coverage) at the price of a bigger table, which is one of the trade-offs behind choosing a\ntokenizer.\n\nAfter lookup, the model needs to know *where* each token is, because attention itself is permutation\ninvariant. Older models add a learned positional vector to the token embedding; most current models apply\nrotary position embeddings inside attention instead, so the input embedding carries only identity. The\n`positional-encoding` topic covers that choice.\n\n### The unembedding and weight tying\nAt the top of the stack, the final hidden state `h` (dimension `d_model`) is projected to a score per\nvocabulary entry: `logits = h · W_Uᵀ` with `W_U` of shape `[V, d_model]`. This matrix has the same shape\nas `E`, and some models (GPT-2 among them) **tie** them, using one matrix for both. Tying saves a full\ntable of parameters and encourages consistency, *because* the vector used to recognize a token on the\nway in is the same one used to predict it on the way out. Many larger models (Llama-2, for instance)\nuntie them, trading parameters for flexibility. Either way, the output of the model is a dot product\nbetween a hidden state and every token's output embedding, which is why embeddings and logits are two\nends of one mechanism.\n\n### Sentence and document embeddings\nAn embedding *model* produces one vector for an entire input. Architecturally it is usually a\ntransformer encoder followed by a pooling step: mean-pool the last hidden states, take a special\nfirst-token state, or take the last token for decoder-based embedders. Pooling alone is not enough. The\nraw hidden states of a language model trained only on next-token prediction make poor sentence vectors,\nbecause nothing in that objective asks the pooled vector of two paraphrases to coincide. Embedding models\nare therefore trained **contrastively**: given (query, relevant passage) pairs, pull each pair together\nand push the query away from the other passages in the batch (in-batch negatives, InfoNCE loss). That\nobjective is what makes cosine similarity meaningful.\n\nPractical consequences of that training:\n- Vectors are compared with cosine similarity or dot product; with L2-normalized vectors these are\n  identical, and most models expect normalization.\n- Dimension is a design choice, from 384 (the `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` family) to 1,536 or 3,072 (OpenAI's\n  `text-embedding-3` models). Matryoshka-trained models order information so the first *k* dimensions\n  are usable on their own, letting you truncate to save index memory with a measured recall cost.\n- Some models are **asymmetric**: queries and passages are prefixed differently (the E5 family uses\n  `query:` and `passage:`), because a short question and a long document should not be embedded the same\n  way even when they mean the same thing.\n- A vector is only comparable to vectors from the *same model and version*; re-embedding everything is\n  mandatory when the model changes.","example":"The first block shows that an LLM's embedding layer is a row lookup equal to a one-hot matmul, and that\nGPT-2 ties its input and output embeddings. The second uses a contrastively trained encoder to show the\ngeometry that retrieval relies on: two paraphrases score far higher than an unrelated sentence.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\nlm = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nE = lm.get_input_embeddings().weight                   # [50257, 768] trainable table\nids = tok(\"The cat sat\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids # [1, n_tokens]\nx = E[ids]                                             # row lookup -> [1, n_tokens, 768]\nonehot = torch.nn.functional.one_hot(ids, E.shape[0]).float()\nassert torch.allclose(onehot @ E, x)                   # lookup == one-hot matmul\nprint(E.shape, x.shape)\n# Weight tying: the output projection is the very same matrix.\nprint(lm.get_output_embeddings().weight.data_ptr() == E.data_ptr())   # True for GPT-2\n\nst = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")   # 384-dim encoder\nsents = [\"How do I reset my password?\",\n         \"Steps to recover access to my account\",\n         \"Best pizza places in Naples\"]\nv = st.encode(sents, normalize_embeddings=True)        # [3, 384], unit length\nprint((v @ v.T).round(2))     # cosine matrix: [0,1] clearly highest, [0,2] and [1,2] low\n```","tradeoffs":["A bigger vocabulary means fewer tokens per document and cheaper context, but a larger embedding table and more rarely-updated rows; it pays off when the corpus is multilingual or code-heavy.","Higher embedding dimension stores finer distinctions but multiplies vector-index memory and search cost linearly; below a few hundred dimensions recall drops noticeably, above a couple of thousand the gains usually do not justify the index size unless the corpus is very large and diverse.","Tying input and output embeddings saves parameters and helps small models, while untying gives large models more capacity; the benefit flips as the table becomes a small fraction of total parameters.","A general-purpose embedding model works out of the box, whereas a fine-tuned one on domain pairs improves retrieval on jargon-heavy corpora at the cost of a training pipeline and re-indexing on every model change."],"pitfalls":["Mixing vectors from two embedding models or two versions of one model in a single index; the symptom is retrieval that looks random for part of the corpus, exactly the part embedded with the other model.","Using an LLM's raw hidden states, or mean-pooled token embeddings, as sentence vectors; similarity scores cluster in a narrow band and nearest neighbours are dominated by length and frequent words.","Forgetting the query/passage prefixes an asymmetric model was trained with; recall silently drops, often by a lot, with no error anywhere.","Comparing unnormalized vectors with cosine in one place and dot product in another; rankings disagree between your offline evaluation and the production index.","Embedding chunks that are longer than the model's input limit; the tail is truncated silently and content past the cut is never retrievable."],"when":"Token embeddings are not a choice; every LLM has them, and you meet them when you count parameters,\nextend a vocabulary (new rows must be initialized and trained), or inspect why two tokens behave alike.\nSentence embeddings are a choice: reach for them whenever the problem is \"find things that mean the\nsame as this\" at scale, including semantic search, RAG retrieval, deduplication, clustering and\nclassification with a light head on top. Do not reach for them when exact lexical match matters (IDs,\nerror codes, rare names), where BM25 or hybrid search wins. Measure with recall@k on a labelled set of\nquery–passage pairs before and after any change of model, dimension or chunking. In interviews, expect\nto explain the difference between the LLM's embedding table and an embedding model, why cosine\nsimilarity works for one and not the other, and what re-embedding costs when a model is upgraded.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the embedding model, normalizes and stores vectors, versions them alongside the model name, and writes the recall@k evaluation that gates any swap.","architect":"Owns the embedding contract across the platform: one model per index, dimension and storage budgets, and the re-indexing plan when a model is deprecated.","fde":"Diagnoses \"search returns nonsense\" at a customer by checking model mismatch, missing prefixes and truncated chunks before touching the retrieval algorithm."},"check":{"q":"A team upgraded their embedding model, re-embedded new documents only, and now users report that\nolder documents never show up in search. Explain the mechanism and what you would do.","a":"Vectors are only comparable within one model's learned space. The new model places new documents\nin a geometry that has no relationship to the old model's, so a new-model query vector lands near new\ndocuments and effectively at random distance from old ones; cosine scores between the spaces are\nmeaningless, not merely slightly off. The fix is a full re-embedding of the corpus with the new model\ninto a fresh index, cut over atomically once it is complete, with the model name and version stored\nwith every vector so the mismatch is detectable next time. While the backfill runs, either keep\nquerying the old index or run both and merge results per index, never across them."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is an embedding lookup mathematically identical to a linear layer applied to a one-hot vector?","options":["Because the embedding table is initialized from the identity matrix","Because multiplying a one-hot vector by the table selects exactly one row, so the lookup just skips the zero multiplications","Because embeddings are normalized to unit length after lookup","Because both operations are followed by a softmax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A one-hot vector has a single 1, so its product with the table is that one row. The lookup is an optimization that avoids a V-wide multiplication whose result is known in advance."},{"q":"Why do contrastively trained embedding models produce useful cosine similarities when an LLM's pooled hidden states do not?","options":["Because embedding models use a larger vocabulary","Because LLM hidden states are not normalized","Because the contrastive objective explicitly pulls paraphrase pairs together and pushes unrelated texts apart, while next-token prediction never asks for that","Because embedding models have more layers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Geometry follows the training objective. Next-token prediction shapes vectors for predicting the next token, not for comparing whole texts; contrastive training directly optimizes the distance you later query."},{"q":"What does weight tying between the input embedding and the output projection buy?","options":["A larger context window","Faster attention computation","Fewer parameters and a shared representation for recognizing and predicting each token","Immunity to tokenizer changes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both matrices have shape [V, d_model], so sharing one saves a whole table and forces the same vector to serve on the way in and on the way out. It matters most when the table is a large fraction of the model."}]},{"key":"model-internals/llm-architecture","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Raffel et al., 2020 — Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer","Radford et al., 2018 — Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training","Wang et al., 2022 — What Language Model Architecture and Pretraining Objective Work Best for Zero-Shot Generalization?"],"why":"Sequence-to-sequence tasks such as translation have a clear shape: a complete input you may read in any\norder, then an output you must produce left to right. The 2017 transformer mirrored that shape. The\nencoder lets every source token attend to every other, in both directions, *because* understanding a\nsentence benefits from seeing its end. The decoder is masked so each output token sees only earlier\noutput tokens, *because* at inference time later tokens do not exist yet. Cross-attention connects the\ntwo.\n\nThree questions then forced the architecture choice that defines today's LLMs. First, which single\nobjective yields the most general model from unlabelled text? Next-token prediction over a causal stream\nturns every token into a training example, with no masking scheme or input/output split to design.\nSecond, how do you handle a conversation, where this turn's output is next turn's input? In a two-stack\nmodel you must re-encode the growing transcript on every turn; in a one-stack model you append tokens and\nkeep the cache. Third, how does few-shot prompting work? Examples and the request live in the same\nstream as the answer, so in-context learning falls out of the training objective instead of needing a\nseparate mechanism. Decoder-only answered all three with less machinery, and at scale that simplicity\ncompounded.","how":"### Three families\n- **Encoder-only** (BERT and descendants). Bidirectional self-attention, trained by masking tokens and\n  predicting them. Produces representations, not text. Used for classification, token tagging, embedding\n  models and cross-encoder rerankers, where the job is to *read* an input and score it.\n- **Encoder–decoder** (T5, BART, mT5, Whisper). The encoder is bidirectional; the decoder is causal and\n  adds a cross-attention block in every layer whose queries come from the decoder state and whose keys\n  and values come from the encoder's final output. T5 was pre-trained with span corruption: hide spans of\n  the input, generate them in the output.\n- **Decoder-only** (the GPT line, Llama, Mistral, and essentially every frontier chat model). One causal\n  stack. Prompt, retrieved documents, tool results and the model's own answer are a single token sequence;\n  the only difference between \"input\" and \"output\" is which positions the loss was computed on.\n\n### What the mask changes\nIn a decoder-only model a prompt token at position *i* attends only to positions ≤ *i*. The\nrepresentation of the first word of a question is built without seeing the rest of the question. An\nencoder sees everything at once, so each token's representation can use the whole input. This is the\nstandard argument that encoders \"understand\" input more richly per token; decoder-only models compensate\nwith depth and scale, and later positions in the prompt do see everything before them, so by the end of\nthe prompt the model has a complete picture.\n\n### Where the compute goes\nIn an encoder–decoder, the encoder runs once per input and its outputs are reused by every decoding step\nthrough cross-attention; the decoder's self-attention KV cache grows with the output only. That lets you\npair a large encoder with a small decoder when the input is long and the output short, which is why\nspeech models like Whisper keep the design: the audio encoder is expensive and runs once, the text decoder\nis cheap and runs per token. In a decoder-only model the prompt is processed by the same full stack as\nthe answer (the prefill phase), populating one KV cache that the answer then extends. Prefill is fast in\nwall-clock terms because all prompt positions run in parallel, but in FLOPs per token \"reading\" costs\nthe same full stack as \"writing\"; there is no smaller encoder to delegate it to. The compensation is that\nnothing has to be re-encoded when the conversation grows: the cache from turn one is still valid for turn\ntwo.\n\n### Why decoder-only scaled better for general use\n- **Objective density.** Every position in a causal stream contributes a loss term. Span-corruption and\n  masked-LM objectives compute loss on a fraction of positions, so each document yields less signal.\n- **Zero-shot behaviour.** Controlled comparisons (Wang et al., 2022) found causal decoder-only models\n  trained with the plain language-modelling objective generalized best zero-shot after pre-training\n  alone; encoder–decoders caught up only after additional multitask fine-tuning.\n- **Serving.** One stack means one set of weights, one KV cache and one batching scheme. Prefix caching\n  and multi-turn reuse are natural because history is just earlier tokens.\n- **Unification.** Instruction following, tool calling and multi-document prompts all become \"put it in\n  the stream\". A prefix-LM variant (bidirectional attention over the prompt, causal over the answer) is a\n  hybrid that keeps one stack; it has been used in some models but did not displace the plain causal\n  design.\n\n### What encoder–decoder still does better\nFixed input, short output, no multi-turn: translation, summarization pipelines, speech-to-text, OCR\npost-correction. The bidirectional encoder gives a better per-token view of the source, cross-attention\ngives a direct alignment between output and input, and the encoder cost is paid once. Encoder-only\nmodels remain the right tool for embeddings and rerankers, where no generation happens at all.","example":"The two loops below make the structural difference visible. The T5 encoder runs once and its output is\nhanded, unchanged, to every decoder step; the GPT-2 \"input\" grows by one token per step, with no\nseparate encoder at all. Outputs from these tiny base models will be rough; the point is the data flow.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\n# Encoder–decoder: two streams joined by cross-attention\nt5_tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"google/flan-t5-small\")\nt5 = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(\"google/flan-t5-small\").eval()\nsrc = t5_tok(\"Translate English to German: The cat sleeps.\", return_tensors=\"pt\")\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    enc = t5.get_encoder()(**src)                        # bidirectional pass, done ONCE\n    dec = torch.tensor([[t5.config.decoder_start_token_id]])\n    for _ in range(8):\n        out = t5(encoder_outputs=enc, attention_mask=src.attention_mask, decoder_input_ids=dec)\n        dec = torch.cat([dec, out.logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)], dim=1)\nprint(t5_tok.decode(dec[0], skip_special_tokens=True))\n\n# Decoder-only: prompt and completion are one causal stream\ng_tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\ng = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nids = g_tok(\"English: The cat sleeps. German:\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for _ in range(8):\n        ids = torch.cat([ids, g(ids).logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)], dim=1)\nprint(g_tok.decode(ids[0]))\n# T5 re-reads a fixed encoding each step; GPT-2's \"input\" is the whole growing sequence.\n# A production decoder-only loop would pass past_key_values instead of re-running the prefix.\n```","tradeoffs":["Decoder-only gives one stack, one cache and natural multi-turn reuse, but every prompt token costs a full-stack forward pass; encoder–decoder pays encoding once and can decode with a smaller stack, which wins when inputs are long and outputs short.","Bidirectional encoding sees the whole input per token, while causal prompts see only the past; the gap matters for small models on reading-heavy tasks and shrinks as decoder-only models scale.","A single next-token objective is dense and label-free, but it bakes in left-to-right generation; tasks that are naturally classification or scoring are served more cheaply by an encoder with a head.","Cross-attention gives an explicit source-to-output alignment that is useful for attribution, at the cost of a second attention block per layer and a design that must be retrained to change the input modality."],"pitfalls":["Reaching for a decoder-only chat model to do bulk classification or reranking; cost per item is far higher than an encoder with a head, and latency budgets blow up at scale.","Assuming an encoder–decoder can \"continue the conversation\" cheaply; the transcript must be re-encoded every turn, so latency grows with history even for short replies.","Treating the prompt as if the model read it bidirectionally; instructions placed after a long document cannot influence how earlier tokens were represented, which is why instruction placement matters.","Confusing the architecture family with the training recipe; a decoder-only base model that was never instruction-tuned will happily continue your prompt instead of answering it."],"when":"Default to decoder-only for anything conversational, agentic, tool-using or open-ended: it is where the\nbest-trained weights are, and its serving stack (KV cache, prefix caching, continuous batching) is the\nmost mature. Choose encoder–decoder when the task is a fixed transformation of a known input with no\ndialogue (translation at volume, speech recognition, structured summarization) and the input is much\nlonger than the output. Choose encoder-only when you need a score or a vector, not text: embeddings,\nrerankers, classifiers and PII detectors. Measure cost per request and p95 latency under your real\ninput/output length ratio before deciding; the ratio is what decides. In interviews, be ready to draw the\nthree families, explain what cross-attention connects, and argue why one causal stack won.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks `AutoModelForCausalLM` versus `AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM` versus an encoder with a head, and knows the generation loop and cache semantics differ between them.","architect":"Decides the model family per workload in a system, keeping the expensive decoder-only model for dialogue and routing scoring and retrieval tasks to encoders.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their translation or transcription pipeline should not be rebuilt on the chat model, and sizes the two differently."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to transcribe call-centre audio and then let agents chat about each transcript.\nWhich architecture family fits each half, and why not one model for both?","a":"Transcription is a fixed-input, read-heavy, write-light task with no dialogue: an encoder–decoder\nsuch as Whisper runs its expensive audio encoder once and decodes text with a small decoder, which is\ncheap per token and well aligned to the source. The chat half is multi-turn with growing history, tool\ncalls and open-ended answers, which is exactly what a decoder-only model handles by appending tokens and\nreusing its KV cache. One model for both would either force the chat model to consume audio tokens it was\nnot trained on, or force the transcription model to re-encode the whole transcript plus conversation on\nevery turn. Two models, with the transcript passed as text, is cheaper and better at each job."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does cross-attention in an encoder–decoder connect?","options":["Decoder queries to decoder keys from earlier layers","Decoder queries to the encoder's final keys and values","Encoder queries to decoder keys and values","Positional encodings to token embeddings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each decoder layer has a self-attention block over the output so far and a cross-attention block whose keys and values come from the encoder output, letting every generated token consult the full input."},{"q":"Why is multi-turn chat cheaper in a decoder-only model than in an encoder–decoder?","options":["Decoder-only models have fewer parameters","Encoder–decoders cannot use a KV cache","History is just earlier tokens in one stream, so the existing cache stays valid; an encoder–decoder must re-encode the growing transcript","Decoder-only models skip attention over the prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a single causal stream, nothing before the new tokens changes, so cached keys and values are reused. An encoder–decoder treats the transcript as input, and input is re-encoded whenever it changes."},{"q":"Which task is the weakest fit for a decoder-only chat model?","options":["Multi-step agent with tool calls","Few-shot question answering","Scoring ten million query–document pairs for relevance","Open-ended summarization in a conversation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scoring is a read-and-rate task with no generation; an encoder with a classification head, or a cross-encoder reranker, does it at a fraction of the cost per pair."}]},{"key":"model-internals/context-window","sources":["Liu et al., 2023 — Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","Su et al., 2021 — RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding","Peng et al., 2023 — YaRN: Efficient Context Window Extension of Large Language Models"],"why":"A transformer processes a fixed set of positions at once, and three costs scale with how many. Attention\ncompares every token with every earlier token, so prefill compute has a term quadratic in length.\nThe KV cache stores keys and values for every position in every layer, so memory grows linearly with\nlength and the cache of a single long request can exceed the model's own weights. Positional encodings\nare learned or tuned for the lengths seen in training; feed a model positions it has never seen and\nquality degrades sharply unless the encoding is deliberately extended. Vendors therefore publish a hard\nlimit, and serving engines enforce one (`max_model_len` in vLLM) because the memory for the worst case\nmust be reserved in advance.\n\nThe naive mental model, \"the chatbot remembers our conversation\", is wrong in a way that matters. The\nmodel is stateless between calls. The client re-sends the entire transcript each turn, and the model\nre-reads it. Memory is an illusion maintained by the application, paid for in tokens on every request,\nand it ends abruptly at the window boundary: either the request is rejected or something has to be\ndropped. Deciding what to drop, summarize or retrieve is the problem the context window forces on you.","how":"### Accounting\nEvery request must satisfy `input_tokens + max_output_tokens ≤ window`. Input is everything in the\nprompt after chat templating: system prompt, each prior user and assistant turn, tool definitions, tool\noutputs, retrieved chunks, and the template's own role markers. Many APIs cap output separately at a\nfigure well below the window, so the practical limit on a long answer is the output cap, not the window.\nTokens are counted by the model's own tokenizer, and the same text can differ in token count by tens of\npercent across tokenizers (more for non-English text and code), so budgets must be computed with the\nright one.\n\n### Why the limit sits where it does\nThree constraints set the number. **Training length**: models are pre-trained at some length and often\nextended afterwards by continued training at longer lengths with the rotary base frequency adjusted\n(RoPE scaling methods such as YaRN); the advertised window is where that extension was validated.\n**Memory**: the serving engine must be able to hold the KV cache for the longest allowed request, so the\noperator may configure a smaller window than the model supports to fit more concurrent users. **Compute\nand latency**: prefill time grows faster than linearly in prompt length, so time-to-first-token on a\nwindow-filling prompt can be seconds, and every turn re-pays it unless the prefix is cached.\n\nPublished windows have grown from about 2k tokens (GPT-3 era) through 4k, 8k and 32k to 128k, 200k and,\nfor some models, a million or more. Treat the exact figures as moving targets; the mechanisms are not.\n\n### Nominal versus effective window\nA model accepting 128k tokens does not use all of them equally. Liu et al. (2023) showed accuracy on a\nretrieval-style task is highest when the relevant passage sits at the start or end of the context and\ndrops when it sits in the middle. Needle-in-a-haystack tests probe the same thing. The effective window,\nthe length over which the model reliably uses information, is task-dependent and usually shorter than the\nnominal one, and it must be measured for your task rather than read off a spec sheet.\n\n### Living within it\n- **Sliding window**: drop the oldest turns. Simple, loses early instructions and facts.\n- **Summarization / compaction**: replace old turns with a model-written summary. Keeps the gist, loses\n  detail and costs a call.\n- **Retrieval**: keep history or documents outside the prompt and pull in only what the current turn\n  needs. The default for large corpora; the `long-context-vs-rag` topic covers the trade.\n- **Prompt caching**: does not enlarge the window, but makes a long shared prefix cheap to re-send by\n  reusing its KV cache across requests, which changes the economics of a big system prompt.\n- **Sliding-window attention** in the model itself (Mistral 7B v0.1 attends over a 4,096-token window per\n  layer) bounds per-layer cost while deeper layers still propagate information further back; it is an\n  architectural choice, not something you configure per request.","example":"A token budgeter for a support assistant on an 8,192-token window. The fixed parts (system prompt,\nretrieved chunks, the new question) are paid first, the output reservation is held back, and history is\nadmitted newest-first until the budget runs out. Count with the deployed model's tokenizer; `tiktoken`\nis used here only as a stand-in.\n\n```python\nimport tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")          # stand-in; use your model's tokenizer\ncount = lambda s: len(enc.encode(s))\n\nWINDOW, MAX_OUT, TEMPLATE_OVERHEAD = 8_192, 800, 64  # overhead: role markers, illustrative\n\ndef fit_history(system, chunks, history, question):\n    fixed = count(system) + sum(map(count, chunks)) + count(question) + TEMPLATE_OVERHEAD\n    budget = WINDOW - MAX_OUT - fixed\n    if budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(f\"fixed parts exceed window by {-budget} tokens: trim chunks\")\n    kept = []\n    for turn in reversed(history):                  # newest turns are worth the most\n        n = count(turn)\n        if n > budget:\n            break\n        kept.insert(0, turn); budget -= n\n    return kept, budget\n\nsystem = \"You are a support assistant. \" * 60                 # ~360 tokens\nchunks = [\"Policy section ... \" * 120 for _ in range(6)]        # 6 chunks, ~360 tokens each\nhistory = [f\"turn {i}: \" + \"words \" * 250 for i in range(20)]  # ~250 tokens per turn\nkept, left = fit_history(system, chunks, history, \"How do I get a refund?\")\nprint(f\"kept {len(kept)} of {len(history)} turns, {left} tokens spare\")\n# Dropping the oldest turns is the crude option; summarizing them is the usual upgrade.\n```","tradeoffs":["A larger window lets you skip retrieval and paste everything in, but prefill latency and per-request cost grow with every token on every turn; retrieval wins once the corpus exceeds a few requests' worth.","Keeping full history maximizes fidelity, while summarizing it cuts tokens and latency at the price of lost detail; the flip happens when old turns stop being referenced.","Reserving a large output budget protects long answers but shrinks the space for input; size it to the real distribution of answer lengths rather than the maximum.","Operators can shrink the engine's window to fit more concurrent users in GPU memory, trading per-request capability for throughput; raise it only when traffic actually uses it."],"pitfalls":["Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer or forgetting template and tool-definition overhead; the symptom is intermittent \"context length exceeded\" errors on requests that looked within budget.","Assuming the model will find a fact anywhere in a 100k-token prompt; the symptom is answers that ignore material placed in the middle while the same material at the end works.","Letting an agent loop append tool outputs forever; latency climbs turn by turn, cost per turn grows linearly, and the run eventually fails at the boundary.","Truncating from the front and cutting the system prompt or the schema of a tool; behaviour changes abruptly mid-conversation with no error.","Setting `max_tokens` far above real answer lengths; engines whose schedulers budget KV memory against a request's worst-case length then admit fewer concurrent requests even though answers are short."],"when":"There is no opting out; every request has a window. The decisions are how much of it to spend on\ninstructions, on examples, on retrieved material, on history, and on the answer. Measure the token\ndistribution of real requests, the rate of truncation events, and task accuracy as a function of where\nkey information sits in the prompt. Prefer retrieval when the corpus is large or changes, long context\nwhen a single document must be reasoned over as a whole, and compaction for long agent runs. In\nday-to-day work the window appears as a budgeting function, a truncation policy and a cost line; in\ninterviews, expect to be asked why windows are finite, what the KV cache has to do with it, and whether\na bigger window makes RAG unnecessary.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the budgeting and truncation logic, counts tokens with the right tokenizer, and monitors truncation and context-exceeded errors as first-class metrics.","architect":"Sets the context policy per workload, chooses between long-context and retrieval designs, and ties the window size to GPU memory and concurrency targets.","fde":"Shows a customer why pasting their entire knowledge base into the prompt is slow and expensive, and measures effective context on their real documents."},"check":{"q":"A product manager argues that with a 200k-token window, the team can drop its RAG pipeline and\npaste the whole product manual into every request. Make the engineering case for and against.","a":"For: simpler system, no retrieval failures, and the model can cross-reference the whole manual.\nAgainst: every request pays prefill over the full manual, so time-to-first-token and cost per turn rise\nwith manual size rather than with the question; effective use of information in the middle of a very\nlong prompt is measurably weaker than at the edges, so accuracy can fall even as the window is nominally\nlarge; the KV cache for a 200k-token request is large enough to cut concurrency on a shared GPU or to\nhit vendor rate limits. Prompt caching softens the cost if the manual is a stable prefix, but not the\nattention-quality issue. The right test is accuracy, latency and cost on real questions with the manual\npasted versus retrieved, measured before the pipeline is deleted."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is NOT a reason the context window is finite?","options":["KV-cache memory grows with the number of positions","Attention compute grows with sequence length","The tokenizer vocabulary has a fixed size","Positional encodings are only trained up to a certain length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vocabulary size bounds how many distinct tokens exist, not how many positions a request may contain. Memory, compute and positional training length are the real constraints."},{"q":"A chat assistant \"forgets\" instructions given early in a long session. What is the most likely mechanism?","options":["The model's weights were updated by the conversation","The application truncated or summarized early turns to fit the window, so the instructions are no longer in the prompt","The KV cache was evicted by the GPU","Temperature was too high"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The model has no state between calls; it only sees what the client re-sends. Once early turns are dropped to stay within the window, their content is gone for every later turn."},{"q":"What does prompt caching change about a long shared system prompt?","options":["It raises the model's maximum context length","It makes the model attend to the prompt more accurately","It reuses the prefix's KV cache across requests, cutting prefill cost and latency for that prefix","It compresses the prompt into fewer tokens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Caching stores the computed keys and values for a byte-identical prefix so later requests skip that prefill work. The window and the model's use of the content are unchanged."}]},{"key":"model-internals/kv-cache","sources":["Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention"],"why":"Autoregressive generation is a loop: feed the prompt, get a token, append it, feed everything again.\nWithout caching, step *t* re-runs the full forward pass over all *t* tokens, so producing *n* tokens costs\nroughly 1 + 2 + … + n = O(n²) token-forward-passes. For a 4,000-token answer that is eight million\ntoken-passes instead of four thousand.\n\nLook at what attention actually needs. For the newest token, the query vector **q** is new, but the keys\n**K** and values **V** of every earlier position are pure functions of those earlier tokens and the model\nweights. In a causal decoder they never change once computed, because position *i* cannot see position\n*j > i*. Recomputing them is therefore wasted work with an identical result. The cache simply memoizes\nthat work per layer.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"just recompute, GPUs are fast\", fails for two reasons. First, the quadratic cost\nmakes long outputs unaffordable: step *t* costs *t* tokens of compute, so a long answer spends most of\nits FLOPs re-deriving results it already had. Second, it buys nothing: the recomputed K and V are\nbit-for-bit what the previous step computed, so no quality or flexibility is gained for the extra work.\nCaching does change the character of a decode step, though. With a single new token per step, the\nweights are read once for very little arithmetic, so decode becomes memory-bandwidth bound rather than\nFLOP bound; that regime is what batching and speculative decoding later exploit.","how":"### What is stored\nIn each transformer layer, the attention block projects each token's hidden state into a key and a\nvalue: `k = x·W_K`, `v = x·W_V`. For a token at position *i*, these depend only on the hidden state at\n*i* in that layer, which in turn depends only on tokens ≤ *i*. So after a token has been processed, its\n**k** and **v** for every layer can be stored and never recomputed.\n\n### The two phases\n1. **Prefill.** The whole prompt is processed in one batched forward pass. This is compute-bound\n   (large matrix multiplications) and it populates the cache with K and V for every prompt position in\n   every layer.\n2. **Decode.** One new token per step. The model computes **q, k, v** for just that token, appends its\n   **k** and **v** to the cache, and attention is `softmax(q·Kᵀ/√d)·V` over the cached K and V. Only one\n   row of queries is involved, so the step is dominated by *reading* the cache and the weights, not by\n   arithmetic. Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, which is why tokens/second per request barely improves\n   with a faster GPU if its bandwidth is similar.\n\n### How big it is\nPer token, the cache holds one key and one value vector per layer per KV head:\n\n```\nbytes_per_token = 2 (K and V) × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\n```\n\nFor a Llama-2-7B-style model (32 layers, 32 heads, head_dim 128, fp16): 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 = 524,288\nbytes ≈ 0.5 MB per token. A 4k-token sequence is ~2 GB; 32 concurrent such sequences would need ~64 GB,\nwhich is the whole card. This is the arithmetic behind the sentence \"LLM serving is memory-bound\".\nGrouped-query attention shrinks `n_kv_heads` (8 instead of 32 in Llama-3-8B), cutting the cache 4×, and\nthat is exactly why GQA exists.\n\n### Why it is per layer\nEach layer has its own W_K and W_V and operates on a different hidden state, so the cache is a 4-D\ntensor `[layer, position, kv_head, head_dim]` for K and the same for V. There is no sharing across layers\nin a standard transformer.\n\n### Interaction with batching\nBecause each request's cache grows at a different rate and finishes at a different time, naive\ncontiguous allocation fragments GPU memory badly. PagedAttention (vLLM) stores the cache in fixed-size\nblocks addressed through a page table, the same trick operating systems use for virtual memory, which\nlets a server pack many more sequences and share prompt prefixes between requests.","example":"A minimal decode loop with and without a cache, using Hugging Face Transformers. The cached version\nfeeds only the new token each step and passes `past_key_values` back in.\n\n```python\nimport torch, time\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"          # any small causal LM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\nids = tok(\"The KV cache exists because\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef generate(use_cache, steps=64):\n    x, past = ids, None\n    out = []\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        if use_cache:\n            r = model(input_ids=x if past is None else x[:, -1:], past_key_values=past, use_cache=True)\n            past = r.past_key_values              # grows by one position per step\n        else:\n            r = model(input_ids=x, use_cache=False)  # re-processes the whole prefix every step\n        nxt = r.logits[:, -1].argmax(-1, keepdim=True)\n        x = torch.cat([x, nxt], dim=1); out.append(nxt.item())\n    return out\n\nfor flag in (False, True):\n    torch.cuda.synchronize(); t = time.time(); generate(flag); torch.cuda.synchronize()\n    print(f\"use_cache={flag}: {time.time()-t:.2f}s\")\n# Expect the cached loop to be several times faster, with the gap widening as steps grows.\n# Inspect size: layer-0 keys have shape [batch, n_kv_heads, seq_len, head_dim]. On recent Transformers\n# `past` is a DynamicCache (past.layers[0].keys); older versions return tuples (past[0][0]).\n```","tradeoffs":["Compute drops from O(n²) to O(n) per generation, but memory grows linearly with sequence length and with concurrent requests, so the binding constraint moves from FLOPs to VRAM and bandwidth.","Longer context windows multiply cache size directly; a 128k-token window is only usable because of GQA, quantized caches and paging, each of which costs some quality or engineering complexity.","Caching the prompt prefix across requests (prefix caching) saves prefill compute for shared system prompts, but only when prefixes are byte-identical and the cache can be kept resident between calls.","Quantizing the cache to 8-bit (or 4-bit) halves memory and raises batch size, at a measurable but usually small accuracy cost that must be evaluated per model and task."],"pitfalls":["Estimating serving capacity from model weights alone and forgetting the cache; the symptom is out-of-memory errors or aggressive request preemption once traffic has long contexts.","Assuming a faster GPU will speed decode proportionally; decode is bandwidth-bound, so tokens/second tracks memory bandwidth more than peak FLOPs.","Editing or truncating conversation history in the middle and reusing the old cache; cached keys carry their position (RoPE is applied to them) and every entry depends on all earlier content, so any change before position *i* invalidates everything from *i* onward.","Benchmarking \"time per token\" on a single request and extrapolating to a batch; with batching, the cache of all concurrent requests competes for the same memory bandwidth."],"when":"You do not choose whether to use a KV cache; every production inference engine does. What you choose is\nhow to live within its memory budget: the maximum context length you allow, the batch size and\nconcurrency limits, whether to enable prefix caching for shared system prompts, whether to quantize the\ncache, and which model (GQA vs full multi-head attention) fits your hardware. Reach for the formula above\nwhenever someone asks \"how many concurrent users can this GPU serve\" or \"why did latency collapse when\nwe raised the context limit\". In interviews, being able to derive bytes-per-token and connect it to GQA,\nPagedAttention and the prefill/decode split is the expected depth for an AI engineer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `max_model_len`, batch limits and prefix-caching flags in vLLM or SGLang, and debugs OOMs by computing cache size rather than guessing.","architect":"Owns the capacity model: context window policy, GPU selection and concurrency targets all follow from cache arithmetic and the prefill/decode cost split.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their 100-page-document use case needs a different GPU tier or a retrieval step, using the per-token memory number as the argument."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a 32-layer, 32-head, head-dim-128 model in fp16 on an 80 GB GPU; weights take 14 GB.\nThey want 64 concurrent chats averaging 6k tokens. Does it fit, and what are their options if not?","a":"Cache per token is 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 0.5 MB. Per chat at 6k tokens that is ~3 GB; 64\nchats need ~192 GB, far beyond the ~66 GB left after weights. Options, in rough order of cost: cap\nconcurrency or context (about 20 chats fit); switch to a GQA model with 8 KV heads, cutting the cache 4×\nso ~80 chats fit; quantize the cache to 8-bit for another 2×; use paged attention so memory is not lost to\nfragmentation; add prefix caching if chats share a long system prompt; or shard across more GPUs. A strong\nanswer also notes decode is bandwidth-bound, so higher concurrency raises per-request latency."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the KV cache store keys and values but not queries?","options":["Queries are too large to store efficiently","Only the newest token's query is needed at each step, while past keys and values are reused by every later token","Queries are recomputed because they change after each layer's normalization","Storing queries would break causal masking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Attention for the new token needs its own query against all previous keys and values. Earlier queries were only needed when those tokens were generated, so caching them would be wasted memory."},{"q":"Roughly how does KV-cache memory scale with sequence length and batch size?","options":["Constant in sequence length, linear in batch size","Quadratic in sequence length, constant in batch size","Linear in sequence length and linear in batch size","Logarithmic in both, because of paging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each token in each sequence adds one K and one V vector per layer, so memory is proportional to tokens × concurrent sequences. Paging reduces waste from fragmentation but not the total needed."},{"q":"Which change reduces KV-cache size without changing sequence length or precision?","options":["Increasing the number of transformer layers","Switching from RoPE to learned positional embeddings","Using grouped-query attention with fewer KV heads","Enabling speculative decoding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cache size is proportional to the number of KV heads; GQA shares each K/V head across several query heads, so Llama-3-8B's 8 KV heads need a quarter of the cache of a 32-head layout. Speculative decoding changes compute per step, not cache per token."}]},{"key":"model-internals/logits","sources":["Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning","nostalgebraist, 2020 — interpreting GPT: the logit lens"],"why":"A classifier over *V* classes needs a score per class, and a language model is a classifier over the\nvocabulary at every position. Two design choices follow. The network could emit probabilities directly,\nbut a layer whose outputs must be non-negative and sum to one is awkward to build and worse to train:\ngradients would have to respect the constraint, and the loss would saturate near the boundaries. Emitting\nan unconstrained real number per class and normalizing afterwards separates the two jobs. The linear\nlayer scores, softmax normalizes, and the cross-entropy loss can be computed in a numerically stable\nfused form directly from the scores.\n\nKeeping scores and probabilities distinct is also what makes inference controllable. Probabilities are\nlocked to the simplex; scores are not. Add a constant to a token's logit and its probability rises\nsmoothly; set a logit to negative infinity and that token can never be sampled; divide all logits by a\ntemperature and the distribution sharpens or flattens without touching the model. Grammar-constrained\ndecoding, JSON mode, stop sequences, repetition penalties and the API's `logit_bias` parameter all work\nby editing logits between the final layer and the sampler. The name is older than deep learning: a\nlogit is a log-odds, and in the multi-class case the logits are log-probabilities up to an additive\nconstant.","how":"### Where they come from\nAfter the last transformer block, each position's hidden state `h` (a `d_model` vector) is multiplied\nby the unembedding matrix `W_U` of shape `[V, d_model]`, giving `z = W_U · h`, a vector of *V* scores.\nDuring training this is done for every position, because the loss is computed everywhere. During\ndecoding only the last position's logits are needed, since the next token depends on nothing else, and\nengines compute just that row. The tensor an inference call returns is `[batch, seq_len, vocab]`; the one\nyou sample from is `logits[:, -1, :]`.\n\n### Relationship to probabilities\n`p_i = exp(z_i) / Σ_j exp(z_j)`, so `log p_i = z_i − logsumexp(z)`. Two consequences are used daily:\n- **Shift invariance.** Adding the same constant to every logit leaves the probabilities unchanged, so\n  the absolute value of a logit means nothing; only differences between logits matter. Implementations\n  exploit this by subtracting the maximum before exponentiating to avoid overflow.\n- **Logprobs.** What an API returns under `logprobs` is `log_softmax(z)` for the chosen token and a few\n  alternatives. A logprob of −0.05 means the model put about 95% on that token; −2.3 means about 10%.\n  Summing per-token logprobs gives the sequence log-likelihood; exponentiating the negative average gives\n  perplexity.\n\n### Editing logits\nThe sampler sees logits, not text, so steering means arithmetic:\n- **Temperature** divides the whole vector by *T* before softmax.\n- **Logit bias** adds a per-token offset. OpenAI's API accepts values from −100 to 100; a large negative\n  value effectively bans a token, a large positive one forces it.\n- **Masking** sets disallowed tokens to −∞, giving them exactly zero probability. Constrained decoding\n  builds this mask each step from a grammar or JSON schema, which is why structured output can be\n  guaranteed rather than merely requested.\n- **Repetition and frequency penalties** lower the logits of tokens already present in the context.\n\nThe order of these edits matters because they are not commutative; engines apply them in a fixed\npipeline, and the `sampling-strategies` topic covers the consequences.\n\n### Numerical care and interpretability\nLogits are produced in the model's compute dtype, but softmax and cross-entropy should be taken in\nfp32, *because* `exp` of a half-precision score overflows easily and small probability differences\nvanish in 16-bit. Some architectures bound the logits explicitly (Gemma 2 applies a `tanh` soft cap to\nits final logits) to keep training stable. Finally, since `W_U` can be applied to *any* hidden state,\napplying it to intermediate layers shows what the model would predict if it stopped early; this\n\"logit lens\" is a standard interpretability tool and a reminder that logits are just a dot product\nbetween a residual-stream vector and each token's output embedding.","example":"The script pulls the next-token logits from GPT-2, shows their range and shape, converts them to\nlogprobs the way an API does, demonstrates shift invariance, and then does the two edits every serving\nstack performs: banning a token and biasing another.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")\nm = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\").eval()\nids = tok(\"The capital of France is\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    z = m(ids).logits[0, -1]                 # [50257]: one raw score per vocabulary entry\nprint(z.shape, round(z.min().item(), 1), round(z.max().item(), 1))   # unbounded reals\n\nlogp = torch.log_softmax(z.float(), -1)      # what APIs return as \"logprobs\"\ntop = torch.topk(logp, 5)\nfor lp, i in zip(top.values, top.indices):\n    print(f\"{tok.decode(i)!r:12} logit={z[i]:7.2f} logprob={lp:6.2f} p={lp.exp():.3f}\")\n\n# Shift invariance: a constant added to every logit changes nothing after softmax.\nassert torch.allclose(torch.softmax(z + 123.0, -1), torch.softmax(z, -1), atol=1e-6)\n\n# Steering happens here, before the sampler ever sees a probability.\nz2 = z.clone()\nz2[top.indices[0]] = float(\"-inf\")           # ban the top token: probability exactly 0\nz2[tok.encode(\" Berlin\")[0]] += 10.0         # logit bias: push an alternative up\nprint(\"after edits:\", repr(tok.decode(torch.softmax(z2, -1).argmax())))\n```","tradeoffs":["Returning logprobs gives callers calibration signals and cheap confidence filters, but exposes information about the model and adds payload, which is why vendors cap how many alternatives you get.","Logit bias is instant and needs no retraining, while a fine-tune changes behaviour more robustly; bias wins for a handful of tokens, loses when the desired change is semantic rather than lexical.","Hard masking guarantees format compliance at zero probability leakage, but a mask that excludes the token the model wanted can push it into low-probability continuations and hurt content quality.","Computing the full vocabulary projection at every position is what training needs, but at decode time it is wasted beyond the last position, so engines compute only the last row."],"pitfalls":["Reading logits as probabilities or comparing their absolute values across prompts; the symptom is \"confidence\" numbers that make no sense, since only differences within one vector are meaningful.","Taking softmax or computing cross-entropy in fp16; the symptom is NaN losses or probabilities that collapse to exactly 0 and 1, especially for long sequences.","Applying a logit bias to a word that tokenizes into several pieces and biasing only the first; the symptom is the model starting the word and then veering off.","Using top-1 logprob as a hallucination detector without calibration; instruction-tuned models are often confidently wrong, so high probability does not imply correctness.","Forgetting that the token you bias must exist in the vocabulary of *this* tokenizer; the id you copied from another model points at an unrelated token."],"when":"You touch logits whenever you need control or measurement that prompting alone cannot give: forcing a\nclassification into a fixed label set, guaranteeing JSON, banning a competitor's name, computing\nperplexity on an evaluation set, or scoring candidate answers by likelihood. Do not reach for logit\nedits to fix what is really a prompt or data problem, and do not over-read logprobs as truth signals\nwithout a calibration study. Measure the effect of any edit on task accuracy, not just on format\ncompliance. In interviews, the expected depth is to explain the path from hidden state to logits to\nsoftmax to sampler, why shift invariance makes masking with −∞ clean, and how constrained decoding\nplugs in.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses `logit_bias`, `logprobs` and logits processors in generation code, and knows to compute scores in fp32 and only for the last position.","architect":"Decides whether structured output is enforced at the logit level or validated after the fact, and whether logprobs are exposed to downstream services.","fde":"Uses per-token logprobs to show a customer where the model was unsure in a bad answer, and bans or biases tokens to meet a hard output constraint on site."},"check":{"q":"You need a model to answer only \"yes\" or \"no\". Compare doing it by prompt, by logit bias and by\nhard masking, including what each guarantees and what can go wrong.","a":"A prompt asks politely; most of the time it works, but nothing prevents \"Yes, because...\" or an\nempty answer, so you need parsing and retries. Logit bias adds a large positive offset to the two tokens\nand a negative one to others; it strongly favours them but is probabilistic, and you must bias the exact\ntoken ids of this tokenizer, including leading-space variants. Hard masking sets every other logit to −∞\nso only the two tokens have non-zero probability; the output is guaranteed, and you can read the two\nlogprobs as a calibrated-ish score. The risk with masking is that the model's preferred continuation was\nsomething else, so the forced choice can be lower quality than a free answer followed by classification.\nAny of the three should be evaluated on labelled data rather than trusted on format alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding the same constant to every logit leave the sampled distribution unchanged?","options":["Because logits are clipped to a fixed range before sampling","Because softmax divides each exponentiated logit by their sum, and the common factor cancels","Because the model is trained with weight decay","Because the tokenizer normalizes scores"],"answer":1,"explanation":"exp(z + c) = exp(c)·exp(z), so the constant multiplies numerator and denominator alike. Only differences between logits carry information, which is why absolute logit values are meaningless."},{"q":"What does setting a token's logit to negative infinity achieve that a large negative bias does not?","options":["It removes the token from the vocabulary permanently","It makes generation faster","Its probability becomes exactly zero, so it can never be sampled regardless of temperature","It forces the model to pick the next-highest token deterministically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A finite bias leaves a tiny but non-zero probability that temperature or long sampling runs can hit; −∞ becomes exactly 0 after softmax, which is what constrained decoding relies on."},{"q":"An API returns a logprob of −0.69 for the chosen token. Roughly what probability did the model assign?","options":["About 69%","About 7%","About 50%","About 99%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Probability is exp(logprob); exp(−0.69) ≈ 0.50. Logprobs are natural logs of probabilities, not percentages, and −0.69 is ln(0.5)."}]},{"key":"model-internals/softmax","sources":["Bridle, 1990 — Probabilistic Interpretation of Feedforward Classification Network Outputs","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Milakov & Gimelshein, 2018 — Online normalizer calculation for softmax","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks"],"why":"A network that scores *K* options needs a way to choose among them that it can learn through. Taking\nthe argmax is the obvious choice, but it is piecewise constant: a tiny change in scores either does\nnothing or flips the answer, so the gradient is zero almost everywhere and nothing propagates back to\nthe scores that were not chosen. Dividing each score by the sum of scores fails too: scores can be\nnegative, the result is not a distribution, and the map does nothing to sharpen or soften preferences.\n\nExponentiating first solves both problems. `exp` is positive, so the normalized values are a valid\ndistribution for any real input; it is smooth, so every score receives a gradient; and it is monotone,\nso ranking is preserved. The result is the \"soft\" version of argmax: as the gap between the best score\nand the rest grows, the output approaches a one-hot, and as scores converge it approaches uniform. Paired\nwith cross-entropy loss, the gradient with respect to the logits is simply `p − y`, prediction minus\ntarget, which is as clean a training signal as exists. Softmax also has a principled reading: it is the\nmaximum-entropy distribution consistent with the scores as expected energies, the same form as the\nBoltzmann distribution in physics, which is where the word \"temperature\" comes from.","how":"### The function and its properties\nFor scores `z` and temperature `T > 0`:\n\n```\np_i = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)\nlog p_i = z_i / T − logsumexp(z / T)\n```\n\n- **Shift invariance.** `softmax(z + c) = softmax(z)` for any constant *c*, because `exp(c)` factors out\n  of numerator and denominator. Implementations subtract `max(z)` before exponentiating *because* the\n  result is identical and the largest exponent becomes `exp(0) = 1`, so nothing overflows.\n- **Temperature.** Dividing by `T < 1` widens gaps between scores and sharpens the distribution; `T > 1`\n  flattens it. As `T → 0` softmax becomes argmax; as `T → ∞` it becomes uniform.\n- **Strictly positive.** Every output is greater than zero for finite inputs. Only `−∞` produces an\n  exact zero, which is how masks and token bans work.\n- **Sums to one.** Mass removed from one entry must go somewhere. This is a constraint, not a feature,\n  and it has consequences in attention (below).\n- **Gradient.** `∂p_i/∂z_j = p_i(δ_ij − p_j)`. With cross-entropy against a one-hot target the combined\n  gradient is `p − y`.\n- **Two classes.** With `K = 2`, softmax reduces to the sigmoid of the score difference; the logistic\n  function is the special case.\n\n### At the output layer\nThe vocabulary softmax converts the last position's logits into the next-token distribution over tens\nof thousands of entries. Because every entry stays positive, the distribution has a long tail of\nindividually tiny but collectively significant probabilities; sampling from the full distribution will\neventually draw one of them, which is why top-k and top-p truncation exist. Temperature here is a\nuser-facing knob; temperature inside attention is not.\n\n### Inside attention\nEach head computes `softmax(q·Kᵀ / √d_k)` over positions, producing weights that sum to one across the\npositions the query may see. The `√d_k` scale is a fixed temperature: dot products of *d_k*-dimensional\nvectors grow in magnitude with *d_k*, and without the scale the softmax would saturate early in training,\ngiving near-one-hot weights and vanishing gradients. Causal masking adds `−∞` to future positions before\nthe softmax so they get exactly zero weight. Because the weights must sum to one even when no position is\nrelevant, models learn to dump excess attention onto an uninformative position, usually the first token;\nthese **attention sinks** are a direct consequence of the normalization constraint, and streaming methods\nthat evict old tokens must keep the sink tokens or quality collapses.\n\n### Computing it efficiently\nA naive softmax over a long row needs the whole row in memory to find the max and the sum. The online\nformulation keeps a running maximum and rescales the running sum when the maximum changes, so the result\ncan be accumulated in tiles that never leave fast on-chip memory. That trick is the heart of\nFlashAttention: exact attention without materializing the full `n × n` score matrix.","example":"The code shows a stable softmax with temperature on a three-way score vector and prints how entropy\nmoves with *T*; the numbers are worked out below so you can check them by hand. The last two lines\nshow the overflow the max-subtraction trick prevents.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef softmax(z, T=1.0):\n    z = np.asarray(z, dtype=np.float64) / T\n    z = z - z.max()                      # shift invariance: same result, no overflow\n    e = np.exp(z)\n    return e / e.sum()\n\nz = [2.0, 1.0, 0.1]\nfor T in (1.0, 0.5, 2.0):\n    p = softmax(z, T)\n    H = -(p * np.log(p)).sum()\n    print(f\"T={T}: p={p.round(3)} entropy={H:.2f} nats\")\n\nbig = np.array([1000.0, 0.0], dtype=np.float32)\nprint(np.exp(big) / np.exp(big).sum())   # overflow: inf/inf -> nan in the first slot\nprint(softmax(big))                      # [1. 0.]\n```\n\nBy hand at `T = 1`: `exp(2.0) = 7.389`, `exp(1.0) = 2.718`, `exp(0.1) = 1.105`, sum `11.213`, so\n`p ≈ [0.659, 0.242, 0.099]`. At `T = 0.5` the scores become `[4, 2, 0.2]`, exponentials\n`[54.598, 7.389, 1.221]`, sum `63.209`, `p ≈ [0.864, 0.117, 0.019]`: the leader gained and the tail\nshrank by a factor of five. At `T = 2` the scores are `[1, 0.5, 0.05]`, exponentials\n`[2.718, 1.649, 1.051]`, sum `5.418`, `p ≈ [0.502, 0.304, 0.194]`: close to uniform. Same ranking in\nall three cases; only the confidence changed.","tradeoffs":["Smooth, differentiable normalization gives clean gradients everywhere, but it can never express a hard zero, so sampling must be truncated externally and attention must park unused mass somewhere.","Lower temperature raises consistency and accuracy on tasks with one right answer, while higher temperature raises diversity; the right value flips with whether the task rewards variety.","The exact softmax needs the whole row, which is memory-heavy for long attention rows; the online version is exact and tile-friendly at the cost of extra rescaling work per tile.","Scaling by `√d_k` stabilizes attention at initialization, but it is a fixed temperature; heads that need very sharp or very flat attention must learn to grow or shrink their query and key norms."],"pitfalls":["Exponentiating in half precision or without subtracting the max; the symptom is NaN or inf probabilities on long or confident rows, often only on some batches.","Masking after the softmax (multiplying probabilities by zero) instead of adding `−∞` before it; the symptom is rows that no longer sum to one. The sibling bug, masking with a large finite negative such as −1e4 that underflows to nothing in fp16, shows up as attention leaking to masked positions.","Treating \"99% probability\" as 99% correctness; softmax outputs are scores shaped by training, and calibration must be measured rather than assumed.","Evicting the first tokens of a long context to save KV memory; attention weights that used to land on the sink get redistributed and output quality degrades sharply.","Reusing an output-layer temperature inside attention or vice versa; they are separate knobs with separate meanings."],"when":"You do not choose softmax; you inherit it at two places in every transformer and in nearly every\nclassifier. What you choose is the temperature at the output, the truncation rules layered on top, the\nprecision the normalization runs in, and whether a problem is better framed as multi-class (softmax) or\nmulti-label (independent sigmoids, because labels are not mutually exclusive). Measure calibration if\nyou intend to read probabilities as confidence. The function shows up daily when debugging NaNs, when\ntuning sampling, and when reasoning about why attention behaves as it does. In interviews, expect to\nderive the gradient with cross-entropy, explain the max-subtraction trick, and say why attention scores\nare scaled by `√d_k`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets temperature per endpoint, computes softmax and losses in fp32, and masks with `−∞` before normalization rather than zeroing afterwards.","architect":"Decides whether a classification problem is mutually exclusive (softmax) or multi-label (sigmoids), and whether exposed probabilities are calibrated enough for downstream policy.","fde":"Explains to a customer why temperature 0.2 made their extraction consistent and why the \"confidence\" column still needs a calibration check on their data."},"check":{"q":"Why are attention scores divided by the square root of the key dimension before the softmax, and\nwhat would you expect to see in training if that scale were removed?","a":"A dot product of two *d_k*-dimensional vectors with roughly unit-variance components has variance\nproportional to *d_k*, so with `d_k = 128` raw scores are about eleven times larger in spread than with\n`d_k = 1`. Softmax of large-magnitude inputs is nearly one-hot, and in that regime its gradient\n`p_i(δ_ij − p_j)` is close to zero for every entry, so the query and key projections receive almost no\nlearning signal. Dividing by `√d_k` restores unit variance, keeping the softmax in its responsive range\nat initialization. Without it you would expect attention maps that collapse to single positions early,\nslow or stalled loss decrease, and sensitivity to initialization, which is exactly the instability the\nscale was introduced to prevent."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do implementations subtract the maximum score before exponentiating?","options":["To make the output sum to one","Because the result is identical by shift invariance and the largest exponent becomes exp(0), preventing overflow","To apply the temperature","To zero out masked positions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softmax is unchanged by adding a constant to all inputs, so subtracting the max costs nothing in accuracy and bounds every exponent at or below 1, which keeps the sum finite in fp32 or fp16."},{"q":"What happens to the next-token distribution as temperature approaches zero?","options":["It becomes uniform over the vocabulary","It becomes undefined because of division by zero","It concentrates all mass on the highest-logit token, equivalent to greedy decoding","It removes tokens below a probability threshold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dividing by a tiny T widens the gaps between scores without bound, so the top entry dominates the sum and the rest vanish. Engines special-case T = 0 as argmax rather than dividing."},{"q":"Why do attention sinks arise in trained transformers?","options":["Because the first token has a special embedding","Because rotary position encoding gives position zero a distinctive rotation","Because attention weights must sum to one even when no position is relevant, so the model learns to dump excess mass onto a convenient token","Because of weight decay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The normalization constraint forces every query to allocate its full weight somewhere. Early tokens are visible to all later positions, so they become the default place to park attention mass."}]},{"key":"model-internals/sampling-strategies","sources":["Holtzman et al., 2020 — The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration","Fan et al., 2018 — Hierarchical Neural Story Generation","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers generation strategies"],"why":"The model does not output a token; it outputs a distribution, and a 100k-entry distribution has a long\ntail of individually unlikely tokens. Something must collapse it to one choice, and every obvious way of\ndoing so fails somewhere. Greedy decoding picks the argmax each step. It is deterministic and cheap, but\na sequence of locally best tokens is not the globally best sequence, and in practice greedy text falls\ninto repetition loops: once a phrase has appeared, repeating it becomes the most likely continuation,\nwhich makes repeating it again even more likely. Beam search fixes the local-versus-global problem by\nkeeping the *B* best partial sequences, yet Holtzman et al. (2020) showed that for open-ended text the\nhighest-likelihood continuations are exactly the bland, repetitive ones; human text is consistently\n*less* likely than beam output under the model, because people say surprising things.\n\nPure sampling from the full distribution avoids blandness but has the opposite failure. Each step\nplaces a few percent of mass on the tail collectively, so over a few hundred tokens the sampler will\ndraw a nonsensical token, and once a derailment is in the context, the model conditions on it and the\ntext degrades. Temperature, top-k and top-p exist to keep the diversity of sampling while cutting off\nthe tail that causes incoherence.","how":"### Reshaping: temperature\nLogits `z` are divided by *T* before softmax. `T < 1` sharpens the distribution toward the top token,\n`T > 1` flattens it, and `T = 0` is treated as greedy by every engine rather than as a division. Because\ntemperature rescales all logits, it does not change the ranking, only how much mass the leaders hold.\nIt is the single most used knob: near 0 for extraction, classification and code where consistency\nmatters; around 0.7–1.0 for drafting and brainstorming.\n\n### Truncating: top-k and top-p\n- **Top-k** keeps the *k* highest-probability tokens, sets the rest to zero and renormalizes. It is\n  simple and bounded, but *k* is blind to the shape of the distribution: when the model is certain, 40\n  candidates include junk; when it is uncertain among 200 reasonable words, 40 is too few.\n- **Top-p (nucleus)** sorts tokens by probability and keeps the smallest set whose cumulative mass\n  reaches *p*, then renormalizes. The set adapts: a peaked distribution yields one or two tokens, a flat\n  one yields many. This is why nucleus sampling became the default over top-k.\n- **Min-p** keeps tokens whose probability is at least a fraction of the top token's probability\n  (`p_i ≥ min_p · p_max`). It adapts like top-p but scales with the leader, which makes it behave well at\n  high temperature; it is supported in vLLM and llama.cpp among others.\n\nEngines apply these as a pipeline over logits, typically temperature first, then top-k, then top-p, and\nthe order matters because truncation after temperature sees a reshaped distribution. Repetition,\nfrequency and presence penalties sit earlier in the same pipeline and lower the logits of tokens that\nalready appeared.\n\n### Searching: greedy versus beam\nGreedy is beam search with `B = 1`. Beam search expands each of *B* partial sequences by the vocabulary,\nscores all candidates by cumulative log-probability, keeps the best *B*, and repeats until each beam\nends; a length penalty is usually applied because longer sequences accumulate more negative log-prob and\nwould otherwise lose unfairly. The cost is *B* sequences' worth of compute per step (one batched pass)\nand *B* KV caches, and results\ncannot be streamed cleanly because the winning beam is only known at the end. Beam search is the right\ntool when there is a single correct target and the metric rewards likelihood: machine translation,\nspeech recognition, grammar correction. For chat and creative generation it is rarely used.\n\n### Determinism\n`T = 0` makes the choice deterministic given the logits, but it does not make the logits bit-identical\nacross runs: batched kernels, different batch compositions and non-associative floating-point reductions\ncan change low-order bits, and when two tokens are nearly tied the argmax flips. A seed parameter makes\nsampling reproducible under the same conditions, not across hardware or engine versions. Treat\n\"temperature 0\" as \"much more stable\", not \"reproducible\".\n\n### Interactions worth knowing\nConstrained decoding (JSON schemas, grammars) masks logits *before* sampling, so it composes with any\nstrategy. Some reasoning-oriented APIs fix the temperature and reject the parameter, because their\ntraining assumed a particular sampling regime. And vendor documentation generally advises changing\ntemperature *or* top-p, not both at once, because their effects overlap and tuning both obscures which\none moved the result.","example":"A compact sampler that applies temperature, top-k and top-p in the usual order, run against a toy\nseven-token distribution so the effect of each setting is visible. With `top_p = 0.9` the nucleus is\ntokens 0–4 (cumulative mass 0.93 is the first to reach 0.9); `top_k = 3` keeps tokens 0–2; adding\n`temperature = 0.5` sharpens the distribution so the same `top_p` nucleus shrinks to tokens 0–1.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef sample_next(logits, temperature=1.0, top_k=0, top_p=1.0, generator=None):\n    \"\"\"logits: [vocab]. Order matches common engines: temperature, then top-k, then top-p.\"\"\"\n    if temperature == 0:                                   # \"T = 0\" means greedy, not a division\n        return int(logits.argmax())\n    z = logits.float() / temperature\n    if top_k > 0:\n        kth = torch.topk(z, top_k).values[-1]\n        z = z.masked_fill(z < kth, float(\"-inf\"))          # drop everything below the k-th score\n    if top_p < 1.0:\n        sz, idx = torch.sort(z, descending=True)\n        p = torch.softmax(sz, -1)\n        before = p.cumsum(-1) - p                          # mass strictly ahead of each token\n        sz = sz.masked_fill(before >= top_p, float(\"-inf\"))  # keep the smallest set reaching top_p\n        z = torch.full_like(z, float(\"-inf\")).scatter(0, idx, sz)\n    probs = torch.softmax(z, -1)                           # renormalize over the survivors\n    return int(torch.multinomial(probs, 1, generator=generator))\n\nprobs = torch.tensor([0.50, 0.20, 0.10, 0.08, 0.05, 0.04, 0.03])\nlogits = probs.log()\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(0)\nfor cfg in [dict(temperature=0), dict(top_k=3), dict(top_p=0.9),\n            dict(temperature=0.5, top_p=0.9), dict(temperature=1.5)]:\n    draws = [sample_next(logits, generator=g, **cfg) for _ in range(2000)]\n    print(f\"{cfg}: tokens seen = {sorted(set(draws))}\")\n```","tradeoffs":["Lower temperature buys consistency and higher per-token accuracy on single-answer tasks, but costs diversity; the trade flips when the task rewards variety, such as generating multiple candidates.","Top-p adapts the candidate set to the model's confidence while top-k gives a hard bound on candidates; top-k is safer when a flat distribution must never admit hundreds of options.","Beam search raises likelihood and translation-style accuracy, but multiplies compute and memory by the beam width and blocks streaming; it loses for open-ended text, where likelihood is the wrong objective.","Sampling several diverse answers and voting or reranking improves accuracy on reasoning tasks, at a linear cost in tokens that only pays off when a verifier or majority signal exists."],"pitfalls":["Setting temperature 0 and expecting identical outputs across runs or deployments; the symptom is flaky golden tests that fail on near-tied tokens after an engine or batch-size change.","Using greedy or beam for long free-form generation; the symptom is paragraphs that repeat a sentence or phrase verbatim, sometimes indefinitely until the token limit.","Raising temperature to \"make the model more creative\" on an extraction or code task; the symptom is a rise in malformed output and invented fields that evaluation then attributes to the prompt.","Tuning temperature and top-p simultaneously; results move but it is unclear which knob did it, and settings copied between models behave differently because logit scales differ.","Applying a strong repetition penalty to structured output; the symptom is JSON that avoids repeating a required key or closing bracket because the token was penalized."],"when":"Pick the strategy from the task's error model. One right answer and a downstream parser: temperature at\nor near 0, optionally with constrained decoding, and keep top-p at its default. Drafting, ideation or\nmultiple candidates for a reranker: temperature 0.7–1.0 with top-p around 0.9, or min-p if the engine\noffers it. Translation or transcription scored by likelihood-correlated metrics: beam search with a\nmodest width and a length penalty. Measure task accuracy and format-failure rate across a small grid of\nsettings rather than copying numbers from a blog; the same temperature means different things for\ndifferent models. In day-to-day work the strategy appears as a few request parameters; in interviews,\nexpect to explain why greedy repeats, why nucleus beat top-k, and why beam search is wrong for chat.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets temperature, top-p and penalties per endpoint, pins them in config alongside the model version, and tests format-failure rate when either changes.","architect":"Defines sampling policy per workload class, from deterministic-leaning extraction to diverse candidate generation, and how determinism claims are communicated to customers.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"the model is inconsistent\" or \"it keeps repeating itself\" by reading the sampling parameters before touching the prompt."},"check":{"q":"A team generating product descriptions complains that greedy decoding repeats phrases, but when\nthey switched to plain sampling at temperature 1 the text occasionally became nonsensical. What would you\nrecommend and why does each failure happen?","a":"Greedy repeats because repetition is self-reinforcing under the model: once a phrase appears,\nrepeating it is the single most likely continuation, and taking the argmax every step never escapes.\nPlain sampling fixes that but draws from the full tail, where a few percent of mass per step sits on\ntokens that make no sense in context; over a long description one of them is eventually drawn, and the\nmodel then conditions on its own mistake. The fix is to keep sampling but truncate the tail: top-p\naround 0.9 (or min-p) so the candidate set adapts to confidence, temperature around 0.7–0.8 to favour\nthe leaders without flattening, and a mild frequency penalty if repeats persist. Validate with a\nrepetition metric and a human spot-check across a small parameter grid rather than one setting."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did nucleus (top-p) sampling largely replace top-k as the default truncation method?","options":["It is cheaper to compute than top-k","Its candidate set adapts to the shape of the distribution, keeping few tokens when the model is confident and many when it is not","It guarantees deterministic output","It removes the need for temperature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed k admits junk when the model is sure and excludes good options when it is unsure. A cumulative-mass threshold sizes the set to the model's confidence at each step."},{"q":"Why is beam search a poor choice for open-ended chat responses?","options":["It cannot be combined with a KV cache","It only works with encoder–decoder models","It maximizes sequence likelihood, and for open-ended text the most likely continuations are bland and repetitive","It ignores the system prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Human text is less likely than beam output under the model because it is surprising. Beam search finds high-likelihood text, which is what you want in translation and exactly what you do not want in chat."},{"q":"With temperature set to 0, why can two identical requests still produce different outputs?","options":["The engine ignores temperature 0 and samples anyway","Floating-point reductions and batch composition can change logits slightly, flipping near-tied argmax choices","The tokenizer is non-deterministic","Top-p is still applied randomly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Argmax is deterministic given the logits, but the logits themselves vary at the low-order bits with kernel choice and batching; when two tokens are almost tied, that is enough to change the winner."}]},{"key":"model-internals/speculative-decoding","sources":["Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding","Chen et al., 2023 — Accelerating Large Language Model Decoding with Speculative Sampling","Cai et al., 2024 — Medusa: Simple LLM Inference Acceleration Framework with Multiple Decoding Heads","Li et al., 2024 — EAGLE: Speculative Sampling Requires Rethinking Feature Uncertainty"],"why":"During decode the large model produces one token per forward pass, and that pass is dominated by moving\nweights (and the KV cache) from GPU memory into the compute units, not by arithmetic. Processing two\ntokens in the same pass costs almost the same as processing one, because the weights are read once\neither way; the compute to multiply a second row is nearly free until the batch grows large enough to\nbecome compute-bound. Single-request latency therefore has a floor set by bandwidth, and buying a GPU\nwith more FLOPs does little.\n\nThe obstacle to using that slack is the autoregressive dependency: token *t+1* is unknown until token *t*\nis chosen, so you cannot simply ask for two tokens at once. The naive workaround, \"use a smaller model\",\ntrades quality for speed, which is usually unacceptable. The insight behind speculative decoding is that\nyou do not need to *know* the next tokens, only to *guess* them. If something cheap proposes a\ncontinuation, the large model can score every proposed position in one pass, because given a candidate\nprefix the model's distributions at all positions are computed in parallel, exactly as in prefill. Guess\nright and you advanced several tokens for the price of one pass; guess wrong and you lost only the cheap\ndraft work.","how":"### The loop\n1. **Draft.** A small model (or a cheaper heuristic) generates γ candidate tokens autoregressively from\n   the current prefix, recording its probability `q(x)` for each. γ is typically a handful.\n2. **Verify.** The target model runs one forward pass over `prefix + γ candidates`. Because a causal\n   model computes a distribution at every position in parallel, this yields the target's `p(·)` at all\n   γ draft positions plus one more position after the last candidate, with the same per-pass cost\n   profile as a short prefill.\n3. **Accept or reject, left to right.** For each candidate token *x* in turn, accept it with probability\n   `min(1, p(x) / q(x))`. On the first rejection, sample a replacement from the residual distribution\n   `normalize(max(0, p − q))` and stop. If all γ are accepted, sample one bonus token from the target's\n   distribution at the extra position.\n4. **Roll back.** KV-cache entries for rejected positions, in both models, are discarded; the accepted\n   tokens are appended and the loop repeats.\n\n### Why the output is exactly the target's\nThe accept rule is a form of rejection sampling. Where the draft puts too little mass on a token\n(`q < p`), it is always accepted when drafted and the shortfall is made up by the residual on\nrejections; where the draft puts too much (`q > p`), it is accepted only a fraction `p/q` of the time.\nSummed over cases, the probability of emitting each token equals `p(x)` exactly, which is what makes the\nmethod lossless rather than an approximation. Under greedy decoding the rule collapses to \"accept while\nthe draft matches the target's argmax\", which is the easiest version to reason about.\n\n### What the speedup depends on\nLet α be the probability a draft token is accepted (a measure of draft–target agreement). Treating each\nposition as an independent trial, the expected number of tokens produced per target pass is\n`(1 − α^(γ+1)) / (1 − α)`. With α = 0.8 and γ = 5 that is\nabout 3.7 tokens per pass; with α = 0.5 it is about 2. The wall-clock gain is that figure divided by the\nrelative cost of the cycle (γ draft steps plus one slightly longer target pass), so the draft must be\nmuch cheaper than the target *and* agree with it often. Agreement is highest on predictable text, such\nas code, boilerplate, structured output and anything that copies from the prompt, and lowest on creative\ntext at high temperature. Increasing γ raises the ceiling but lowers the average acceptance of later\npositions, and past a point the extra verification compute costs more than it saves.\n\n### Where the draft comes from\n- **A separate small model** with the same tokenizer (needed so draft token ids are meaningful to the\n  target). A model from the same family, an order of magnitude or more smaller, is the usual choice.\n- **N-gram or prompt lookup**: propose the continuation that followed the same recent tokens earlier in\n  the context. No second model, near-zero cost, strong on RAG answers that quote sources and on code\n  edits that reproduce input, weak elsewhere.\n- **Extra heads on the target** (Medusa): small heads attached to the last hidden state predict tokens\n  at offsets +2, +3, ..., so drafting costs one target pass's worth of extra heads rather than a second\n  model. Candidates are verified as a tree in one pass.\n- **A feature-level draft head** (EAGLE): a light autoregressive module that predicts the next hidden\n  state rather than the next token, reusing the target's own representations for higher acceptance.\n\n### Why it stops helping\nAt high concurrency the server is already feeding the GPU many tokens per pass, and the arithmetic units\nare busy; verifying γ extra tokens per request then costs real compute and can lower total throughput.\nSpeculative decoding is a latency tool for workloads with few concurrent requests, or a throughput tool\nonly while the batch is still bandwidth-bound. Engines such as vLLM expose draft-model and n-gram modes\nwith a configurable number of speculative tokens, and the right setting is found by measuring\ntokens/second per request and per GPU at the real batch size.","example":"First an illustrative cost model (every number here is invented for the arithmetic, not a benchmark),\nthen a runnable verification step on toy distributions that exercises the accept, reject and bonus\npaths.\n\nSuppose a target pass takes 25 ms and a draft pass 2 ms, with γ = 5 and a verification pass that is\nslightly longer than a single-token pass, say 27 ms. A cycle costs 5 × 2 + 27 = 37 ms. At α = 0.8 it\nyields `(1 − 0.8⁶) / 0.2 ≈ 3.69` tokens, so about 10 ms per token against 25 ms baseline: a 2.5× gain.\nAt α = 0.5 it yields about 1.97 tokens, 18.8 ms per token, a 1.3× gain. At α = 0.3 it yields about\n1.43 tokens, 25.9 ms per token: *slower* than not speculating. The same arithmetic with a draft that\ncosts 8 ms instead of 2 turns the α = 0.8 case into 67 / 3.69 ≈ 18 ms per token. Acceptance rate and\ndraft cost are the two numbers to measure before enabling it.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef speculative_step(p_target, q_draft, draft_tokens, g=None):\n    \"\"\"p_target: [gamma+1, V] target probs at each verified position (ONE target pass);\n    q_draft: [gamma, V] draft probs; draft_tokens: [gamma]. Returns the emitted tokens,\n    which are distributed exactly as if sampled from the target alone.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for i, x in enumerate(draft_tokens):\n        p, q = p_target[i], q_draft[i]\n        if torch.rand((), generator=g) < min(1.0, (p[x] / q[x]).item()):\n            out.append(int(x))                                 # accept the draft token\n        else:\n            resid = torch.clamp(p - q, min=0)                  # residual corrects the distribution\n            out.append(int(torch.multinomial(resid / resid.sum(), 1, generator=g)))\n            return out                                         # stop at the first rejection\n    out.append(int(torch.multinomial(p_target[-1], 1, generator=g)))  # all accepted: bonus token\n    return out\n\nV, gamma = 50, 5\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(0)\ntarget = torch.softmax(2 * torch.randn(V, generator=g), -1)\ndraft = torch.softmax(target.log() + 0.5 * torch.randn(V, generator=g), -1)  # similar, not identical\nlengths = []\nfor _ in range(2000):\n    toks = torch.multinomial(draft, gamma, replacement=True, generator=g)\n    lengths.append(len(speculative_step(target.expand(gamma + 1, V), draft.expand(gamma, V), toks, g)))\nprint(\"mean tokens per target pass:\", sum(lengths) / len(lengths))   # between 1 and gamma+1\n# A stationary toy: real positions have different distributions, but the accept/reject logic is identical.\n```","tradeoffs":["Fewer target passes per token cut single-request latency, but the draft model's weights and KV cache take GPU memory and its passes take time; the gain flips negative when acceptance is low or the draft is not much cheaper than the target.","Lossless by construction with the rejection rule, whereas simply trusting the draft would be faster still but changes outputs; the guarantee costs the residual-sampling bookkeeping and cache rollback.","A separate draft model is flexible and reusable across targets in a family, while attached heads (Medusa, EAGLE) avoid a second model and achieve higher acceptance, at the cost of training heads per target model.","Larger γ raises the best-case tokens per pass but spends more verification compute on positions that are increasingly likely to be rejected; the optimum shifts lower as batch size grows."],"pitfalls":["Enabling speculation on a heavily batched server and watching throughput fall; verification tokens compete for compute that was no longer idle, so per-GPU tokens/second drops even as a lone request speeds up.","Pairing a draft model with a different tokenizer; the symptom is near-zero acceptance or outright errors because draft token ids do not mean the same thing to the target.","Measuring the speedup on greedy code completion and assuming it holds for creative writing at temperature 1; acceptance falls with entropy and the gain shrinks or inverts.","Forgetting to roll back KV-cache entries for rejected draft tokens; the symptom is corrupted continuations that reference text the model never emitted.","Treating a reported speedup as a property of the method rather than of the draft–target pair and the workload; the same engine setting can help one customer and hurt another."],"when":"Reach for it when per-request latency (time per output token) matters more than aggregate throughput:\ninteractive assistants, coding tools, agents making many sequential calls, or any deployment with few\nconcurrent requests per GPU. Expect the most from predictable outputs: code, structured data, RAG\nanswers that quote context, and templated text; n-gram drafting is nearly free to try there. Skip it or\nmeasure carefully when the server runs large batches or outputs are high-entropy. Measure acceptance\nrate, tokens per target pass, p50/p95 time per output token and per-GPU throughput before and after,\nat production concurrency. In interviews, the expected depth is the memory-bandwidth argument, the\naccept/reject rule and why it is lossless, the expected-tokens formula, and the conditions under which\nthe technique backfires.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enables draft-model or n-gram speculation in the serving engine, tunes γ, and reads acceptance-rate metrics to decide whether it is paying off.","architect":"Decides where speculation belongs in the fleet: latency-sensitive, low-concurrency pools get it, high-throughput batch pools do not, and the draft model becomes a managed artifact.","fde":"Demonstrates the latency gain on a customer's own prompts and warns when their workload's acceptance rate makes it a loss rather than a win."},"check":{"q":"A team enabled speculative decoding with a small draft model and saw a large speedup in their\nsingle-user demo, then deployed it to a shared endpoint and total throughput fell. Explain both results\nand what you would change.","a":"In the demo the batch size was one, so each target pass was memory-bandwidth bound and the\narithmetic units were idle; verifying γ draft tokens per pass used that free compute, and with a good\nacceptance rate they got several tokens per target pass. On the shared endpoint the engine already\nbatches many requests, so the GPU is close to compute-bound; the extra γ verification tokens per\nrequest now consume real compute, and the draft model's passes and memory add further cost, so\naggregate tokens per second drops even though a lone request would still be faster. I would measure\nacceptance rate and per-GPU throughput at production concurrency, lower γ or switch to a cheaper\ndrafter such as n-gram lookup, and most likely restrict speculation to a latency-sensitive pool with\nlow concurrency while leaving the high-throughput pool without it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the target model verify several draft tokens for roughly the cost of generating one?","options":["The draft tokens are already in the KV cache","Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so the weights are read once per pass and scoring extra positions in that pass is nearly free until compute saturates","The target model skips its attention layers during verification","Verification only runs the last transformer layer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A causal model computes distributions at every input position in parallel. When the pass is dominated by reading weights rather than arithmetic, adding a few positions barely changes its duration."},{"q":"What does the accept-with-probability min(1, p/q) rule guarantee?","options":["That at least one draft token is always accepted","That the draft model's output distribution is used when it is confident","That the emitted tokens follow exactly the target model's distribution, making speculation lossless","That greedy decoding is always used for verification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Combined with sampling from the residual max(0, p − q) on rejection, the rule is rejection sampling from the target distribution using the draft as a proposal, so outputs match the target exactly."},{"q":"Under which condition is speculative decoding most likely to slow a server down?","options":["Low temperature on code completion","A draft model from the same family as the target","High concurrency where each target pass is already compute-bound","Prompts that quote long passages from retrieved documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculation converts idle compute into extra tokens. When batching has already saturated compute, the verification tokens and draft passes cost real time and total throughput can fall."}]},{"key":"model-internals/mixture-of-experts","sources":["Shazeer et al., 2017 — Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer","Fedus, Zoph and Shazeer, 2021 — Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity","Jiang et al., 2024 — Mixtral of Experts","DeepSeek-AI, 2024 — DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report"],"why":"Scaling laws say loss keeps falling as parameters grow, but in a dense transformer every parameter is\ntouched by every token, so FLOPs per token grow in lockstep with parameter count. Doubling the model\ndoubles training compute per token and doubles the weight bytes read on every decode step. The\nfeed-forward block is the obvious place to attack: with the usual 4× expansion it holds roughly two thirds\nof the per-layer non-embedding parameters (8d² for the FFN versus 4d² for the Q, K, V and O projections),\nyet for any single token most of that capacity is irrelevant. The circuits that handle Python indentation\nare not needed while the model is writing French prose.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"make the dense FFN bigger and pay for it\", fails on the inference side as much as\nthe training side. Decode is memory-bandwidth bound (see the KV cache lesson), so a model that reads twice\nthe weights per token runs about twice as slowly at small batch no matter how good the kernels are. A\nsecond naive option, training separate specialist models and choosing one per request, fails because the\nuseful choice is per token and per layer rather than per request, and because separate models cannot share\nthe attention layers and embeddings that carry most of the general language ability.\n\nConditional computation resolves the tension: keep a large pool of parameters but let a learned router\nactivate a small subset for each token. Shazeer et al. made this trainable at scale in 2017, Switch\nTransformer simplified routing to a single expert per token and showed better loss per training FLOP than\ndense models, and open models such as Mixtral and DeepSeek-V3 made it the default architecture for the\nlargest open-weight systems.","how":"### The layer\nAttention is unchanged and shared by all tokens. The FFN in a layer is replaced by N expert FFNs\nE₁ … E_N, each shaped like an ordinary FFN (or narrower), plus a router: one linear map W_r from the hidden\nstate **x** to N logits. The router scores become a distribution `p = softmax(x·W_r)`, the top-k experts\nare selected, and the layer output is a weighted sum of their outputs:\n\n```\ny = Σ_{i ∈ topk(p)}  p_i · E_i(x)        (the k weights usually renormalised to sum to 1)\n```\n\nk is 1 in Switch Transformer and 2 in Mixtral. DeepSeek-V3 routes each token to 8 of 256 small routed\nexperts plus one always-on shared expert, which gives fine-grained specialisation while keeping a common\npathway every token passes through.\n\nRouting is per token and per layer: the same token can go to expert 3 in layer 10 and expert 7 in layer\n11, and adjacent tokens in a sentence usually take different paths. The router is trained end to end with\nthe rest of the network. Because top-k selection is not differentiable, gradient reaches the router only\nthrough the p_i weights that multiply the chosen experts' outputs. That is why the output is scaled by p_i\nrather than simply summed: without the multiplication the router would receive no learning signal.\n\n### Active versus total parameters\nCount Mixtral 8x7B: 8 experts per layer, top-2 routing. The total is about 47B parameters, not 8 × 7 =\n56B, because attention, embeddings and norms are shared across experts. Per token only 2 of the 8 FFNs run,\nso roughly 13B parameters are active. Training FLOPs per token and the weight bytes read per decode step at\nbatch 1 both track the 13B figure; loss tracks something much closer to the 47B figure. That gap is the\nwhole point of the architecture.\n\n### Load balancing\nLeft alone, routers collapse. A few experts that happen to be slightly better early in training receive\nmore tokens, learn faster, and attract still more tokens, until most experts are dead weight. Two\nmechanisms counter this:\n\n- **An auxiliary load-balancing loss** (Switch style). For each expert, multiply the fraction of tokens\n  routed to it, f_i, by the mean router probability it received, P_i; sum over experts and scale by N. The\n  sum is minimised when both are uniform, and because P_i is differentiable it pushes the router toward\n  spreading tokens. DeepSeek-V3 instead adds a per-expert bias to the routing scores and nudges it after each\n  step, which balances load without the small quality penalty an auxiliary loss costs.\n- **Expert capacity.** Each expert accepts at most `capacity_factor × tokens / N` tokens per batch.\n  Overflow tokens are dropped from that expert (they pass through the residual connection only) or sent to\n  their next choice. This exists because experts run as fixed-size batched matrix multiplications, often\n  on different devices, and one overloaded expert would stall all the others.\n\n### Why memory and parallelism change\nEvery expert's weights must be resident, because the next token might be routed anywhere. Mixtral 8x7B\ntherefore needs about 47B × 2 bytes ≈ 94 GB in fp16 for weights alone, more than one 80 GB card, even though\nit computes like a 13B model. At larger scale the experts of each layer are spread across devices (expert\nparallelism), and an all-to-all exchange moves each token's hidden state to the devices hosting its chosen\nexperts and back again. That communication, twice per MoE layer, is the dominant engineering cost of\ntraining and serving MoE models.\n\n### The batch-size effect at inference\nAt batch 1 a decode step reads only the k active experts per layer, so an MoE is far faster than a dense\nmodel of equal total size. As the batch grows, different tokens pick different experts until every expert\nis touched on every step; weight traffic then approaches that of the dense total and the advantage shifts\nfrom bandwidth to FLOPs. Capacity planning for an MoE must be done at the concurrency you will actually run.","example":"A minimal top-k MoE layer with a Switch-style balancing loss. With `d_model=512, d_ff=2048` one FFN has\n2 × 512 × 2048 ≈ 2.1M parameters; eight experts hold ≈ 16.8M in total, but each token runs through two,\nso ≈ 4.2M are active per token. The `aux` value is smallest when tokens are spread evenly.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn, torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass MoELayer(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model=512, d_ff=2048, n_experts=8, k=2):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.k, self.n = k, n_experts\n        self.router = nn.Linear(d_model, n_experts, bias=False)\n        self.experts = nn.ModuleList(\n            nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d_model, d_ff), nn.SiLU(), nn.Linear(d_ff, d_model))\n            for _ in range(n_experts))\n\n    def forward(self, x):                                  # x: [tokens, d_model]\n        probs = self.router(x).softmax(-1)                 # [tokens, n_experts]\n        topv, topi = probs.topk(self.k, dim=-1)            # [tokens, k]\n        topv = topv / topv.sum(-1, keepdim=True)           # renormalise over the chosen k\n        out = torch.zeros_like(x)\n        for e in range(self.n):                            # one batched matmul per expert\n            hit = (topi == e)                              # [tokens, k] bool\n            rows = hit.any(-1).nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]\n            if rows.numel() == 0:\n                continue\n            w = (topv * hit)[rows].sum(-1, keepdim=True)   # router weight for expert e\n            out[rows] += w * self.experts[e](x[rows])      # gradient reaches the router via w\n        frac = F.one_hot(topi, self.n).float().sum(1).mean(0)   # f_i: share of tokens sent to each expert\n        mean_p = probs.mean(0)                                  # P_i: mean router probability per expert\n        aux = self.n * (frac * mean_p).sum()                    # balancing loss, add lambda * aux to LM loss\n        return out, aux\n\nlayer = MoELayer()\ny, aux = layer(torch.randn(4096, 512))\nprint(y.shape, round(aux.item(), 3))\nprint(sum(p.numel() for p in layer.experts.parameters()) / 1e6, \"M expert params total\")\n```\n\nProduction kernels do not loop over experts in Python; they sort tokens by expert and run grouped matrix\nmultiplications, which is why engines such as vLLM and SGLang ship fused MoE kernels.","tradeoffs":["Quality per training FLOP improves because capacity grows without proportional compute, but weight memory grows with total parameters; when a single GPU's VRAM is the binding constraint, a dense model with the same memory footprint can be the better choice.","Decode at small batch is faster than a dense model of equal total size because only k experts are read per layer, but the gain shrinks as concurrency rises and every expert is touched on every step.","Expert parallelism lets total parameters exceed any one device, at the cost of an all-to-all exchange in every MoE layer; on slow interconnects that communication can cancel the compute savings.","Fine-tuning an MoE on a small dataset is reported to be less stable than fine-tuning a dense model, because the router can drift and overfit; freezing the router or using more data helps, but a workflow built around frequent per-customer fine-tunes is simpler with dense models.","Many narrow experts plus a shared expert (the DeepSeek design) specialise more finely than a few wide ones, but produce more small matrix multiplications and more routing overhead, which GPUs handle efficiently only with fused kernels."],"pitfalls":["Sizing serving hardware from active parameters; the symptom is an out-of-memory error at model load, because all experts must be resident regardless of how few run per token.","Reading \"8x7B\" as a 56B model, or benchmarking it against dense 56B models; shared attention makes it about 47B total and 13B active, and the two numbers answer different questions.","Expecting experts to be human-interpretable specialists (\"the code expert\", \"the legal expert\"); the Mixtral authors found routing correlates with token type and syntax far more than with topic, so you cannot prune \"the experts you don't need\" by domain.","Setting the capacity factor too low to save memory; the symptom is quality that degrades on inputs with an unusual token mix (code in a chat model, a rare language) because overflow tokens are silently dropped.","Router collapse during training or continued pre-training; the symptom is an expert-utilisation histogram with a few hot experts and many near zero, plus a loss plateau that the parameter count does not explain.","Benchmarking throughput at batch 1 and extrapolating to production concurrency; weight traffic per step rises with batch until all experts are read, so the dense-versus-MoE comparison can invert."],"when":"You rarely decide to train an MoE; you decide whether to deploy one. Reach for an MoE when you need\ntop-tier quality from open weights, you have a multi-GPU node or enough host memory, and your traffic\npattern leaves a bandwidth advantage at the batch sizes you run. Prefer a dense model when you are on a\nsingle card, when you fine-tune often on small data, or when your serving stack lacks fused MoE kernels and\nexpert-parallel support (a homegrown PyTorch loop will be slow). Measure tokens per second at your real\nconcurrency, peak memory including the KV cache, and quality on your own eval set rather than the model\ncard. In interviews expect \"why is Mixtral 47B and not 56B\", \"what do active versus total parameters\ngovern\", \"why is load balancing needed and how does it work\", and \"when can an MoE be slower than a dense\nmodel\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads the model card for total and active parameters, picks an engine with fused MoE kernels, and verifies memory from weights plus KV cache before promising a GPU count.","architect":"Chooses between MoE and dense for the open-weight tier from VRAM, interconnect, concurrency profile and fine-tuning frequency, and writes the batch-size effect into the capacity plan.","fde":"Explains to a customer why the \"13B-speed\" model on the datasheet still needs two 80 GB GPUs in their data centre, and why single-user latency numbers will not hold under shared load."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to self-host a hypothetical open MoE listed as \"about 140B total, 17B active\" on\ntheir existing single 80 GB GPU because \"it runs like a 17B model\". What do you tell them, and what would\nchange your answer?","a":"Active parameters describe compute and bandwidth per token, not memory. All 140B parameters must be\nresident because any token can be routed to any expert, so fp16 weights alone are about 280 GB, and even\n4-bit quantisation (roughly 70 GB) leaves almost no room for the KV cache on an 80 GB card. Their options\nare a multi-GPU node with expert or tensor parallelism, aggressive quantisation plus expert offloading to\nCPU memory with a large latency penalty, or a dense model in the 13–30B range that fits the card. The speed\nclaim also holds only at low concurrency; as batch grows, every expert is read each step. I would change the\nrecommendation if they have a multi-GPU box with fast interconnect, or if their concurrency is near one and\nthey accept quantised weights."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an MoE layer multiply each chosen expert's output by its router probability instead of simply summing the chosen outputs?","options":["To keep the output magnitude equal to that of a dense FFN","Because top-k selection is not differentiable, so the multiplication is the only path through which the router receives gradient","To penalise experts that receive too many tokens in a batch","Because experts have different hidden sizes and must be rescaled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The hard top-k choice has no gradient. Scaling each expert's output by its softmax probability makes the router's parameters part of the differentiable graph, so it can learn which expert helps. Load balancing is handled separately by the auxiliary loss or bias terms, not by this scaling."},{"q":"Mixtral 8x7B has about 47B parameters but computes like a roughly 13B model. Which statement about serving it is correct?","options":["It fits wherever a 13B fp16 model fits, since only 13B parameters are used per token","Its KV cache is eight times larger than that of a 13B dense model","All 47B parameters must be in memory, but at small batch each decode step reads only the two active experts per layer","It needs memory for 56B parameters because there are eight 7B experts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memory is set by total parameters because routing can pick any expert; compute and weight traffic per token at low batch are set by the active parameters. The total is 47B rather than 56B because attention and embeddings are shared, and the KV cache depends on attention shape, which the experts do not change."},{"q":"What failure does the load-balancing auxiliary loss prevent during training, and what is the observable symptom without it?","options":["Vanishing gradients; the symptom is a loss that never decreases","Router collapse onto a few experts; the symptom is a skewed expert-utilisation histogram and a loss plateau despite the parameter count","Overfitting of the attention layers; the symptom is a train/validation gap","Token dropping from capacity overflow; the symptom is NaN losses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a balancing term, early winners attract more tokens and the rest of the experts stop learning, so the model behaves like a much smaller dense network. Capacity overflow is a related but separate mechanism handled by the capacity factor, and it drops tokens rather than producing NaNs."}]},{"key":"model-internals/small-language-models","sources":["Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Gunasekar et al., 2023 — Textbooks Are All You Need","Hinton, Vinyals and Dean, 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","Liu et al., 2024 — MobileLLM: Optimizing Sub-billion Parameter Language Models for On-Device Use Cases"],"why":"A frontier model is the wrong tool for most of the requests a production system handles. A ticket\nclassifier, an entity extractor, a query rewriter or a PII redactor does not need encyclopaedic knowledge;\nit needs one narrow skill executed millions of times at low latency and low cost. Inference cost, however,\nscales roughly with parameters: every decode step reads every active weight, so a 70B model moves about\n20× the bytes of a 3.5B model for the same output token, and prefill FLOPs scale the same way.\n\nThree developments made small models worth building rather than merely tolerating. First, the Chinchilla\nresult showed earlier small models were badly under-trained, and the Llama family then showed that loss\nkeeps falling far past the compute-optimal token count; because inference dominates the lifetime cost of a\npopular model, spending extra training compute on a small model is repaid many times over. Second,\ncapability can be transferred: distillation trains the small model on a large teacher's output\ndistribution, and synthetic \"textbook\" data (the Phi line) or teacher-generated corpora give far more\nsignal per token than raw web text. Third, deployment constraints are real: on-device assistants,\nair-gapped networks, data-residency rules and offline operation simply cannot call a hosted frontier API.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. \"Just prompt the big model\" fails on cost, on\ntime-to-first-token, on availability, and on privacy. \"Just quantise the big model\" buys roughly 4× in\nmemory, not the 20× that separates a 70B from a 3B, and still needs a server GPU. A small model trained\nwell, fed the facts it needs through retrieval, and fine-tuned to the task is the option that actually\nfits the constraints.","how":"### What \"small\" changes architecturally\nAn SLM is the same decoder-only transformer with fewer layers, a smaller hidden size, and almost always\ngrouped-query attention. One structural difference matters at this scale: the embedding matrix. A 128k\nvocabulary with a 2,048-dimensional hidden state is about 262M parameters, roughly a fifth of a 1.2B model,\nso input and output embeddings are usually tied (one shared matrix) to avoid paying twice. Depth is kept\nwhere possible: MobileLLM reported that deep-and-thin layouts beat shallow-and-wide ones at sub-billion\nscale, because reasoning-like composition benefits from more sequential layers.\n\n### Training recipe\n- **Over-training.** Chinchilla-optimal is about 20 training tokens per parameter. Llama 3 8B was trained\n  on more than 15 trillion tokens, close to 2,000 tokens per parameter. The loss curve flattens but keeps\n  improving, and since serving cost per token is about 2N FLOPs, a smaller N that will serve trillions of\n  tokens justifies the extra training.\n- **Data quality.** With few parameters, every one must earn its place, so small models lean on heavily\n  filtered web data, code, and synthetic or curated educational text.\n- **Distillation.** Instead of one-hot next tokens, the student is trained to match the teacher's full\n  probability distribution (a KL term against teacher logits), which carries information about which wrong\n  answers are nearly right; or the student is trained on teacher-generated outputs (sequence-level\n  distillation). Reasoning distillation fine-tunes a small model on long chain-of-thought traces from a\n  reasoning model and transfers a surprising share of the capability.\n- **Post-training.** The same supervised fine-tuning and preference tuning as large models, with less\n  headroom: a small model follows complex, multi-constraint instructions less reliably.\n\n### What is lost, and why\nParameters are memory. Long-tail facts live in FFN weights, so fewer and smaller FFNs mean fewer facts,\nstored less reliably. The consequences are specific: SLMs hallucinate more on factual recall, degrade faster\non multi-step composition, are more sensitive to prompt wording, and lose more quality under aggressive\nquantisation than large models do. The compensation is architectural rather than model-level: supply the\nfacts (retrieval), supply the computation (tools), narrow the job (fine-tuning), and put a larger model\nbehind a router for the cases that need it.\n\n### The inference arithmetic\nWeights cost `params × bytes_per_param`: a 3B model is 6 GB in bf16, 3 GB in int8, and about 2 GB at 4-bit\nonce scales are included. Decode is bandwidth-bound, so single-stream tokens per second is roughly memory\nbandwidth divided by bytes read per step. As illustrative orders of magnitude: a laptop GPU with a few\nhundred GB/s moves a 2 GB model over 100 times a second, so 100+ tokens/s is plausible; a phone with tens of\nGB/s lands in the tens of tokens/s; on a server GPU with several TB/s the weights are almost free per\nrequest and the KV cache and batching become the limits. This is why \"runs on a phone\" and \"cheap on a\nserver\" are both true, for different reasons.\n\n### Specialist versus generalist\nA fine-tuned 1–8B model routinely matches or beats a zero-shot frontier model on a narrow task when three\nconditions hold: the task is well defined, you have thousands of labelled examples, and production inputs\nlook like the training data. It does not when the task is open-ended, needs novel reasoning, or depends on\nworld knowledge the fine-tuning set and the prompt do not contain.","example":"A support organisation receives 2 million tickets a month and wants each one classified into 40 queues\nand three fields extracted. Each ticket is about 700 input tokens and the answer about 60 output tokens,\nso the monthly volume is 1.4B input and 120M output tokens. All prices and rates below are illustrative\nplaceholders to show the shape of the calculation, not quotes.\n\nFrontier API path: at a placeholder $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, the bill\nis $4,200 + $1,800 = $6,000 per month, with multi-second latency per ticket and the tickets leaving the\ncompany's network.\n\nSLM path: fine-tune a 3B instruct model with LoRA on 8,000 labelled tickets (a few GPU-hours). Serve it in\nbf16 (6 GB of weights) on one 24 GB GPU with batched inference; sequences are short, so the KV cache is\nsmall and batches of 32–64 fit easily. Average load is 2M / (30 × 86,400 s) ≈ 0.8 tickets per second, with\nbursts an order of magnitude higher, well inside what one card handles with continuous batching. At a\nplaceholder $1 per GPU-hour that is about $730 per month, roughly 8× cheaper, with sub-second latency and\nno data egress.\n\nThe decision is made on the golden set, not the invoice: run both models on 1,000 held-out tickets. If the\nSLM is within the tolerance the business accepts on accuracy and its errors are not concentrated in the\nexpensive queues, ship it. Route the low-confidence remainder (a small logit margin between the top two\nclasses, or a schema-validation failure) to the frontier model; in practice that is a minority of traffic,\nso the blended cost stays close to the SLM figure while the quality ceiling is preserved.","tradeoffs":["Cost and latency fall roughly in proportion to parameter count, but factual breadth and multi-step reasoning fall too; the trade flips the moment the task needs knowledge that is not supplied in context.","Fine-tuning is cheap enough to do per task or per customer on a single GPU, but every fine-tune is a model to version, evaluate and redeploy, whereas prompting a frontier model has no such pipeline.","On-device or air-gapped deployment gives privacy and offline operation, but hardware fragmentation and quantisation variance make quality less predictable than on a controlled server fleet.","Over-training makes small models better per parameter but costs more training compute per parameter; it pays only at high inference volume, which is why model vendors, not application teams, absorb that cost.","A router or cascade captures most of the savings while keeping a frontier-quality ceiling, but adds a new failure mode: a miscalibrated router sends hard cases to the small model and nobody notices until an audit."],"pitfalls":["Judging an SLM with zero-shot prompts written for a frontier model; the symptom is \"it fails\" on tasks it would handle well after a few-shot prompt or a small fine-tune, and a premature conclusion that SLMs are useless.","Asking an SLM for facts from its weights; the symptom is confident fabrication of dates, citations and API names. Give it the source text and let it extract.","Comparing candidates on a public benchmark that may be in the small model's training data; the symptom is a benchmark score far above the in-house eval on your own data.","Quantising to 4-bit and skipping re-evaluation; the symptom is subtle regressions in JSON validity and multilingual output, because small models lose more under quantisation than large ones.","Serving single-stream on a server GPU; the symptom is a 3B model whose cost per token rivals that of a well-batched 30B model, because the card is bandwidth-bound and mostly idle at batch 1 while the larger model amortises each weight read over dozens of requests.","Front-loading a 6k-token system prompt plus retrieved context onto a small model; the symptom is slow prefill and answers that ignore the middle of the context, since small models degrade faster with long inputs."],"when":"Reach for an SLM when the task is narrow and high-volume, the inputs carry the facts the answer needs,\nlatency or cost budgets are tight, data cannot leave a device or network, or you have labelled data to\nfine-tune on. Avoid it as the only model for open-ended assistance, novel reasoning, or knowledge-heavy\nquestion answering without retrieval. Measure task accuracy on your golden set against a frontier\nbaseline, calibration of its confidence signal for routing, p95 latency at target concurrency, and cost per\nsuccessful task. Day to day this appears as \"can we move this step off the API\"; in interviews as \"when\nwould you fine-tune a 3B model instead of prompting a frontier model, and how would you know it worked\".\nThe architecture decision is covered in system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs, and the capability transfer in\ntraining-adaptation/knowledge-distillation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fine-tunes a 1–8B model with LoRA on task data, quantises and serves it with vLLM or llama.cpp, and wires logit-margin escalation to a larger model for low-confidence cases.","architect":"Owns the model portfolio: which pipeline steps run on SLMs, where the cascade boundary sits, and how per-task fine-tunes are versioned and gated by evals before release.","fde":"Finds the customer's high-volume narrow workloads, proves an SLM on their data with a side-by-side eval, and handles the on-prem or edge constraints that made a hosted API unusable."},"check":{"q":"A bank wants to classify documents and extract fields on-prem across 5 million pages a month and\nsays \"we will just run the biggest open model we can fit\". How would you argue for an SLM, and what would\nmake you concede?","a":"Frame it as cost per correct extraction and throughput, not model size. The task is narrow, the\ninputs contain the answers, and the bank already has labelled outputs from its back office, so a fine-tuned\n3–8B model at int8 on one or two GPUs can process 5 million pages with headroom and sub-second latency,\nwhile a 70B model on the same hardware runs at a fraction of the throughput with no accuracy advantage on\nin-distribution forms. Propose a golden set built with their SMEs, fine-tune, compare both on it, and route\nlow-confidence or schema-invalid outputs to the larger model. I would concede if the eval shows a material\naccuracy gap, if the documents are heterogeneous free text that needs world knowledge, or if volume is low\nenough that the big model is affordable anyway."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a 3B model trained on 10 trillion tokens outperform an older 7B model trained on 1 trillion, even though both are \"small\"?","options":["Because the 3B model has a larger vocabulary","Because loss keeps falling far past the compute-optimal token count, and vendors now over-train small models since inference, not training, dominates lifetime cost","Because smaller models have less capacity to overfit","Because quantisation works better on 3B models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chinchilla-optimal is about 20 tokens per parameter, but the curve keeps improving beyond it. A 7B model on 1T tokens is near-optimal and under-trained by modern standards; a 3B on 10T tokens has absorbed far more data per parameter, and its lower serving cost is what pays for that training."},{"q":"A team fine-tunes a 1B model to answer medication-dosage questions from memory and sees frequent confident errors. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["The learning rate was too high","The model needs a longer context window","Parametric knowledge is what shrinks first with size; the facts should be supplied through retrieval, with the small model doing extraction and formatting","1B models cannot be fine-tuned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Small models have few weights in which to store long-tail facts, so recall from memory is their weakest mode. Putting the dosage tables in context turns the task into extraction, which a 1B model does well. Hyperparameters and context length do not create knowledge that is not there."},{"q":"Which workload is the weakest fit for a small language model?","options":["High-volume ticket classification with 20,000 labelled examples","On-device dictation cleanup with no network access","Open-ended research assistance that depends on broad world knowledge and multi-step synthesis","Query rewriting in front of a search index"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The first, second and fourth are narrow, in-distribution tasks where the needed information is in the input. Open-ended research needs breadth of knowledge and long chains of reasoning, which are exactly the capabilities that shrink fastest with parameter count."}]},{"key":"model-internals/scaling-laws","sources":["Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models","Schaeffer, Miranda and Koyejo, 2023 — Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage?","Muennighoff et al., 2023 — Scaling Data-Constrained Language Models"],"why":"Deciding how big a model to train and how long to train it used to be craft. The cost of guessing wrong is\nenormous: a frontier run is on the order of 10²⁵ FLOPs (illustrative) and months of accelerator time, so you\ncannot grid-search the design. Scaling laws turn the question into extrapolation. Train a ladder of small\nmodels spanning several orders of magnitude of compute, fit a curve, and read off the best configuration for\nthe budget you actually have.\n\nKaplan et al. found in 2020 that loss is a clean power law in N, D and C across many orders of magnitude,\nand that architectural details such as depth versus width matter far less than scale. That result licensed\nthe \"just scale it\" strategy of the following years. But their fit also recommended growing parameters\nmuch faster than data as compute grows, which produced under-trained giants such as Gopher (280B\nparameters on about 300B tokens). Hoffmann et al. repeated the study in 2022 with a learning-rate schedule\nmatched to each run's length and found that the compute-optimal recipe scales N and D roughly equally,\nabout 20 tokens per parameter. Chinchilla, 70B parameters on 1.4T tokens, beat Gopher at the same training\ncompute. Nothing about the architecture changed; the budget split did.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"bigger is always better\", fails twice. At a fixed budget it wastes compute on\nparameters that never see enough data to be useful. And it ignores that every parameter must be paid for\nagain on every token the model ever serves, which for a popular model is far more compute than training.","how":"### The compute identity\nA forward pass costs about 2N FLOPs per token (one multiply-add per parameter); the backward pass costs\nabout twice that; so training costs approximately `C = 6 N D` FLOPs. Inference costs about 2N FLOPs per\ngenerated token, plus attention, which is small unless contexts are very long. This identity is the bridge\nbetween the training-side and serving-side arguments below.\n\n### The loss fit\nChinchilla's parametric form is\n\n```\nL(N, D) = E + A / N^α + B / D^β\n```\n\nE is the irreducible entropy of the text under the tokenizer, A/N^α is the approximation error from finite\ncapacity, and B/D^β is the estimation error from finite data. Their published fit gave E ≈ 1.69, A ≈ 406.4,\nB ≈ 410.7, α ≈ 0.34 and β ≈ 0.28 for their data and tokenizer. The constants do not transfer to other\nsetups; the shape does. Minimising L subject to 6ND = C gives N_opt ∝ C^a and D_opt ∝ C^b with a ≈ b ≈ 0.5,\nwhich is where the constant tokens-per-parameter ratio comes from.\n\n### Three ways to fit it\nHoffmann et al. used three methods and got the same answer: fix N and vary training length, taking the\nlower envelope of the curves; draw IsoFLOP curves (at fixed C, vary N and find the loss-minimising size); and\nfit the parametric form directly. The agreement is what made the result convincing. Method matters: Kaplan's\ndifferent exponents came partly from using one learning-rate schedule regardless of run length, which\npenalises long runs, and partly from counting parameters without embeddings, which distorts the small-model\nend of the ladder. If you ever fit your own law, tune the schedule per run and state what you count.\n\n### Inference-aware over-training\nCompute-optimal means \"cheapest training for a given loss\". It ignores serving. If a model will generate T\ntokens over its life, lifetime compute is roughly `6ND + 2NT`. When T is much larger than D, which is true\nfor any widely used model, shrinking N and raising D at the same training budget raises the first term only\nslightly and cuts the second term in proportion. This is why Llama 3 8B was trained on more than 15T tokens,\nclose to 2,000 tokens per parameter, and why strong small models exist at all. The loss curve flattens past\nthe optimum but does not stop improving.\n\n### What the laws do and do not predict\nThey predict pre-training loss, equivalently perplexity, on held-out text. They do not directly predict\ndownstream task accuracy. Metrics with thresholds (exact match, pass/fail) can jump abruptly while loss\nfalls smoothly; this is the \"emergent abilities\" phenomenon, and Schaeffer et al. argued that much of the\ndiscontinuity comes from the metric, since smoother measures such as the probability assigned to the correct\nanswer improve gradually. The laws also assume a fixed data distribution: filtered or synthetic data shifts\nthe constants, repeated data behaves like fresh data for only a few epochs before returns diminish sharply\n(Muennighoff et al.), and mixture-of-experts models change what N means, since active and total parameters\nscale loss and cost differently. Finally, reasoning models added a third lever: for some tasks, spending more\ninference compute per query buys more accuracy than a larger base model, which is covered in the test-time\ncompute lesson.","example":"Using Chinchilla's published fit as an illustrative model of the curve's shape, compare a compute-optimal\nrun with a 10× smaller over-trained run at the same 10²⁴ FLOP training budget, then add the cost of\nserving 10¹³ tokens. The small model is about 0.02 nats worse in loss and 10× cheaper per served token,\nwhich makes it cheaper over its lifetime by more than 2×.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nE, A, B, alpha, beta = 1.69, 406.4, 410.7, 0.34, 0.28   # Chinchilla's fit; constants are setup-specific\n\ndef loss(N, D):\n    return E + A / N**alpha + B / D**beta\n\ndef chinchilla_optimal(C, tokens_per_param=20):\n    N = math.sqrt(C / (6 * tokens_per_param))           # 6 * N * D = C with D = 20 N\n    return N, tokens_per_param * N\n\nC = 1e24                                                # training budget in FLOPs (illustrative)\nN_opt, D_opt = chinchilla_optimal(C)\nN_small = N_opt / 10\nD_small = C / (6 * N_small)                             # same budget, ten times the tokens\nT = 1e13                                                # tokens served over the model's life (illustrative)\n\nfor tag, N, D in [(\"compute-optimal\", N_opt, D_opt), (\"over-trained\", N_small, D_small)]:\n    print(f\"{tag:16s} N={N/1e9:5.1f}B  D={D/1e12:5.1f}T  loss={loss(N, D):.3f}  \"\n          f\"train={6*N*D:.1e}  serve={2*N*T:.1e}  lifetime={6*N*D + 2*N*T:.2e} FLOPs\")\n# compute-optimal  N= 91.3B  D=  1.8T  loss=1.918  train=1.0e+24  serve=1.8e+24  lifetime=2.83e+24 FLOPs\n# over-trained     N=  9.1B  D= 18.3T  loss=1.936  train=1.0e+24  serve=1.8e+23  lifetime=1.18e+24 FLOPs\n```\n\nThe decision then moves to the question the law cannot answer: does 0.02 nats of loss matter on the tasks\nyou care about? That is measured with task evals, not predicted.","tradeoffs":["Compute-optimal training minimises training cost per unit of loss, but yields a large model that is expensive to serve; it flips to over-training as soon as lifetime served tokens exceed a few multiples of the training tokens.","Extrapolating from a ladder of small runs is cheap and reliable for loss, but reliability degrades when you change the data mix, tokenizer or architecture, or extrapolate many orders of magnitude past the fitted range.","Spending on data quality shifts the whole curve down, often more cheaply than extra compute, but the shift cannot be measured without training ladder models on the new data.","Test-time compute improves hard-task accuracy without retraining, but it is paid per query forever; it wins for low-volume hard queries and loses to a better base model for high-volume easy ones."],"pitfalls":["Treating 20 tokens per parameter as a law of nature; it is a fit for Chinchilla's data and objective, and the ratio for a model you will deploy is set by inference economics, often in the hundreds or thousands.","Expecting downstream accuracy to follow the smooth loss curve; the symptom is a benchmark flat near zero across several scales and then jumping, leading either to \"emergence\" claims or to abandoning a capability one scale too early.","Counting parameters inconsistently, with or without embeddings, or active versus total for MoE; the symptom is fits that disagree with published ones by a large factor, especially at the small end.","Fitting on runs that share one learning-rate schedule regardless of length; the symptom is long runs that look worse than they are, biasing the fit toward bigger models, which is a large part of the Kaplan/Chinchilla discrepancy.","Counting repeated epochs as fresh tokens; beyond a few repetitions the returns fall off sharply, so the D in your formula is not the D the model effectively saw."],"when":"Most practitioners never fit a scaling law; they use its implications. Use them to choose which model to\ndeploy (a heavily over-trained 8B can match a compute-optimal 20B), to estimate inference cost from 2N\nFLOPs per token, to sanity-check claims (\"70B parameters trained on 100B tokens\" means badly under-trained),\nand to frame the choice between a bigger model and more test-time compute. If you do continued pre-training\nor large fine-tunes, the data-constrained results tell you how many epochs are worth running. If you train\nfrom scratch, run a ladder and fit before committing the budget. In interviews, be able to derive 6ND,\nexplain why Chinchilla overturned Kaplan, and explain why Llama-style models are trained far past the\ncompute-optimal point.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses 2N FLOPs per token and bytes per decode step to estimate cost and latency of candidate models, and reads tokens-per-parameter off a model card as a quick proxy for how well-trained it is.","architect":"Frames model-size decisions as lifetime cost, training plus inference, and decides where a reasoning mode substitutes for a larger base model within the portfolio.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"bigger is better\" instinct into a measured accuracy-versus-cost curve on their task, and explains why a well-trained small model can beat an older large one."},"check":{"q":"Your company has a fixed training budget and a product that will serve a very high token volume for\nyears. A colleague proposes training the Chinchilla-optimal model size. What do you push back on, and how\ndo you quantify it?","a":"Chinchilla optimality minimises training compute for a target loss and ignores serving. Write\nlifetime cost as 6ND + 2NT where T is tokens served; when T is far larger than D the inference term dominates,\nso it is worth shrinking N and raising D at the same training budget and accepting a slightly higher loss.\nQuantify it with the fitted L(N, D): at 10²⁴ FLOPs the optimum is about 90B parameters on 1.8T tokens,\nwhile a 9B model on 18T tokens is roughly 0.02 nats worse yet 10× cheaper per served token. Then check\nwhether that loss gap shows up on the downstream evals that matter, note that over-training has diminishing\nreturns, and confirm you can actually source 18T tokens without heavy repetition."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4T tokens) outperform Gopher (280B parameters, 300B tokens) at similar training compute?","options":["It used a better tokenizer","Gopher was under-trained: at fixed compute, loss is minimised by scaling parameters and tokens roughly equally, about 20 tokens per parameter","Larger models overfit on web text","It used mixture-of-experts layers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both runs spent similar compute, but Gopher put it into parameters that saw barely one token each. Rebalancing toward data at the same budget lowers loss; neither tokenizer nor architecture was the lever."},{"q":"A vendor's 7B model was trained on 2T tokens. Roughly how far is that from Chinchilla-optimal, and why might it be deliberate?","options":["At the optimum; 7B × 300 ≈ 2T","Under-trained; a 7B model needs about 7T tokens to be optimal","About 14× past the roughly 140B-token optimum; serving cost scales with parameters, so a smaller over-trained model is cheaper over its lifetime","Over-training is a mistake because loss stops improving after 20 tokens per parameter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chinchilla-optimal for 7B is about 7B × 20 = 140B tokens, so 2T is roughly 14× more. Loss keeps falling past the optimum, and since inference FLOPs are about 2N per token, a smaller model that will serve trillions of tokens repays the extra training many times over."},{"q":"Which quantity do scaling laws predict most reliably?","options":["Accuracy on a multiple-choice benchmark","Held-out pre-training loss, that is cross-entropy per token","Hallucination rate","Instruction-following quality after RLHF"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The laws are fits of pre-training loss against N, D and C. Thresholded task metrics can jump discontinuously as loss falls smoothly, and post-training quality depends on data and reward signals the laws do not model."}]},{"key":"model-internals/perplexity","sources":["Jelinek et al., 1977 — Perplexity: a measure of the difficulty of speech recognition tasks","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers (perplexity of fixed-length models)"],"why":"The language-modelling objective is to assign high probability to real text, so the natural score is the\nprobability a model gives a held-out corpus. That number is useless on its own: it is the product of\nthousands of probabilities, astronomically small, and it shrinks with text length. Taking the log turns the\nproduct into a sum, dividing by token count removes the length dependence, and negating gives the\ncross-entropy loss the model was trained on. Exponentiating that loss gives perplexity. A loss of 2.0 nats\nis a perplexity of e² ≈ 7.4, which you can read as \"at each step the model is as uncertain as if it were\nchoosing uniformly among 7.4 options\". That interpretability is the reason the metric has outlived the\nspeech-recognition systems it was invented for.\n\nWhy not next-token accuracy? Because many continuations are valid. A model that assigns 30% to the actual\nnext token and spreads the rest over sensible alternatives is better than one that assigns 31% and then\ngarbage, and accuracy cannot tell them apart. Perplexity scores the whole distribution, it is differentiable\n(it is the training loss in disguise), and it needs no labels: any text is an evaluation set. At pre-training\nscale it is the only dense, cheap signal available. Downstream benchmarks are expensive, noisy and arrive\nlate; perplexity is computed on every batch.","how":"### Definition\nFor a token sequence x₁ … x_T under a model p:\n\n```\nPPL = exp( −(1/T) · Σ_t log p(x_t | x_<t) )\n```\n\nIf the cross-entropy H is measured in nats, PPL = e^H; if in bits, PPL = 2^H. Perplexity 1 means the model\npredicted every token with certainty; perplexity equal to the vocabulary size means uniform guessing.\n\nThe computation uses teacher forcing: feed the real text, take the logits at every position, apply softmax,\nand read off the probability of the token that actually came next. One forward pass yields all T\nprobabilities, and no sampling is involved, so temperature plays no role unless you deliberately rescale\nthe logits.\n\n### Reading the number\nPerplexity is a geometric mean of inverse probabilities, which means a handful of very surprising tokens\ndominate it. A rare proper noun, a typo or a number the model could not have known contributes far more\nthan hundreds of easy function words. That sensitivity is useful when you want to detect distribution shift\nand a nuisance when you want to compare two similar models, so always evaluate on enough text that the\nestimate is stable. As an order of magnitude, modern models on clean English prose sit in the single digits\nto low teens depending on tokenizer and domain, code is lower, and random strings are far higher; treat\nthese as illustrative, not as targets.\n\n### Why the tokenizer matters\nPer-token loss depends on how much text each token carries. A tokenizer with a larger vocabulary produces\nlonger tokens: each one is harder to predict, so per-token loss rises, but there are fewer of them, so total\nloss on the document can be identical. Two models with different tokenizers therefore cannot be compared on\nper-token perplexity. The tokenizer-independent alternatives are bits per byte (total negative log-likelihood\nin bits divided by the UTF-8 byte count of the text) or bits per character. Older literature also reports\nper-word perplexity, which is different again. Whenever you see a perplexity, ask which unit it is per.\n\n### Context length and striding\nThe probability of token t conditions on everything before it, so a model evaluated with a 1,024-token\nwindow sees less context than with 8,192 and scores worse on the same text. For documents longer than the\nwindow you must slide. Chopping the text into non-overlapping chunks is cheap but pessimistic, because the\nfirst tokens of every chunk have almost no context. The standard recipe is a sliding window with a stride:\nmove the window forward by `stride` tokens, feed the whole window, and score only the last `stride` tokens.\nA smaller stride gives each token more context at the cost of more forward passes. Report window and stride\nwith every number.\n\n### What it does not measure\n- Instruction-tuned and RLHF models are optimised to produce preferred responses, not to model the\n  distribution of web text, so their perplexity on raw text often rises while their usefulness improves.\n  Low perplexity does not mean a good assistant.\n- Factuality: a fluent false sentence can have low perplexity.\n- Generation quality: the perplexity of a model's own samples under itself is low by construction.\n\n### What it is used for in practice\n1. **Training.** The loss curve is log-perplexity; plateaus, spikes and divergence show up there first.\n2. **Quantisation and pruning.** Measure perplexity on a fixed text (WikiText-2 is the common choice in\n   llama.cpp-style tooling) before and after. A small rise is the usual price of 8-bit or 4-bit weights; a\n   large rise means the compression damaged the model, and the task evals will confirm it later.\n3. **Long-context checks.** Perplexity as a function of position: if it climbs past the training length,\n   the model is not using the extended window.\n4. **Data work.** Filtering documents whose perplexity under a reference model is far outside the normal\n   range, flagging anomalous or adversarial prompts, and checking contamination: a suspiciously low\n   perplexity on a benchmark's test set suggests the model has seen it.\n5. **Domain fit.** Among candidate base models, the one with the lowest bits per byte on a sample of your\n   corpus is a reasonable first filter before expensive task evals.","example":"A sliding-window perplexity evaluation with Hugging Face Transformers. Each window of `max_len` tokens is\nfed to the model, but only the final `trg_len` tokens are scored; earlier tokens are masked with `-100` so\nthey serve as context only. The script also reports bits per byte, which is comparable across tokenizers.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"                              # any causal LM\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\n\ntext = open(\"eval.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read()\nids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()    # [1, T]\nT, max_len, stride = ids.size(1), 2048, 512\nnll_sum, n_scored, prev_end = 0.0, 0, 0\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for begin in range(0, T, stride):\n        end = min(begin + max_len, T)\n        trg_len = end - prev_end                         # tokens not yet scored\n        window = ids[:, begin:end]\n        labels = window.clone()\n        labels[:, :-trg_len] = -100                      # context only; HF shifts labels internally\n        out = model(window, labels=labels)               # out.loss = mean NLL over scored tokens\n        nll_sum += out.loss.item() * trg_len             # first token of the first window is a tiny approximation\n        n_scored += trg_len\n        prev_end = end\n        if end == T:\n            break\n\nppl = math.exp(nll_sum / n_scored)\nbits_per_byte = nll_sum / math.log(2) / len(text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\nprint(f\"tokens={n_scored} window={max_len} stride={stride} ppl={ppl:.2f} bpb={bits_per_byte:.3f}\")\n```\n\nRun it before and after quantising the same model with the same `eval.txt`, window and stride, and the\ndifference is a meaningful regression signal. Run it across two models with different tokenizers and only\nthe `bpb` line is comparable.","tradeoffs":["A dense, cheap, label-free signal at any scale, but blind to correctness, helpfulness and safety; it flips to task metrics the moment you leave pre-training.","Comparable across checkpoints of one model and tokenizer, but not across tokenizers or vocabularies; bits per byte restores comparability at the cost of the intuitive \"number of choices\" reading.","Sensitive to a few high-surprise tokens, which makes it a good detector of distribution shift and a noisy judge of small quality differences; average over enough text and report the evaluation size.","Sliding-window evaluation gives the fairest number but costs up to `window / stride` times more forward passes than chunking; chunking is cheap but systematically pessimistic."],"pitfalls":["Comparing per-token perplexity across models with different tokenizers; the symptom is a model with a 32k vocabulary that \"beats\" one with 128k on the same text purely because its tokens are shorter.","Reporting perplexity without the dataset, tokenizer, window and stride; the symptom is numbers that nobody can reproduce and that drift by double-digit percentages between evaluations.","Using perplexity to choose a chat model; the symptom is a base model that \"wins\" and then cannot follow instructions.","Evaluating on text that was in the training set; the symptom is an implausibly low number that does not predict generalisation, which is also the contamination test seen from the other side.","Mixing nats and bits; the symptom is a \"loss of 2.0\" reported as perplexity 4 (2²) rather than 7.39 (e²), or the reverse.","Scoring padding tokens or forgetting that labels must be shifted by one position; the symptom is a perplexity near 1 (padding predicted perfectly) or an absurdly high one."],"when":"Use perplexity when the question is \"how well does this model fit this distribution of text\": during\npre-training and continued pre-training, when checking a quantised or pruned model against the original,\nwhen choosing a base model for domain adaptation, and when detecting shifted or anomalous inputs. Do not use\nit as the acceptance metric for a product feature; use task accuracy, faithfulness and human preference\nthere. Measure it with a fixed recipe, dataset, tokenizer, window and stride, checked into the repository so\nevery number is reproducible. In interviews, derive it from cross-entropy, explain why it is not comparable\nacross tokenizers, explain why RLHF models score worse on it, and name two production uses beyond training\ncurves.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs a fixed perplexity script on a standard text before and after quantisation and puts the delta in the pull request next to the task evals.","architect":"Sets the rule that perplexity is a gate for model-level changes such as quantisation, pruning and continued pre-training, but never a substitute for task-level acceptance criteria.","fde":"Uses bits per byte on a sample of the customer's documents to show whether a candidate open model \"speaks their language\" before investing in a full evaluation."},"check":{"q":"Two open models report WikiText perplexities of 5.2 and 6.8. A colleague says the first is clearly\nbetter for your legal-contract summarisation product. What is wrong with that reasoning, and what would you\ndo instead?","a":"Three things. The numbers are comparable only if the tokenizers, window and stride match, and a\nlarger vocabulary mechanically raises per-token perplexity. WikiText is encyclopaedia prose, not contracts,\nso fit to the actual domain is unmeasured. And summarisation quality depends on instruction-following and\nfaithfulness, which perplexity does not capture; an RLHF model can have worse perplexity and better\nsummaries. Instead, compute bits per byte on a sample of the firm's contracts as a quick domain-fit filter,\nthen run a task evaluation with faithfulness, coverage and human preference on one or two hundred\nsummaries. Bring perplexity back later only as a regression check when quantising the chosen model."},"quiz":[{"q":"A model's average cross-entropy on a test set is 1.5 nats per token. What is its perplexity, and what does that number mean?","options":["1.5; the model is 1.5 times better than chance","2^1.5 ≈ 2.8; the model needs 2.8 bits per token","e^1.5 ≈ 4.5; on average the model is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among about 4.5 tokens","1/1.5 ≈ 0.67; the average probability of the correct token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Perplexity is the exponential of the cross-entropy in the same base. With a loss in nats that is e^1.5 ≈ 4.5. The 2^1.5 option is the right formula for a loss measured in bits, which is the classic unit confusion, and the others are not transformations of the loss at all."},{"q":"Why can you not directly compare per-token perplexity between a model with a 32k-token vocabulary and one with a 128k vocabulary?","options":["Larger vocabularies make the softmax numerically unstable","Tokens carry different amounts of text, so per-token loss differs even when the two models assign identical probability to the document; compare bits per byte instead","The 128k model always has lower perplexity because it has more parameters","Perplexity is only defined for byte-level tokenizers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total log-probability of a document is tokenizer-independent, but dividing it by a different token count gives a different per-token average. Normalising by bytes or characters removes the tokenizer from the comparison."},{"q":"After RLHF, a model's perplexity on raw web text increased while users rate its answers higher. What is the best interpretation?","options":["The RLHF run damaged the model and should be rolled back","Perplexity was computed incorrectly","Perplexity measures fit to the web-text distribution, which RLHF deliberately moves the model away from toward preferred responses; use task and preference metrics for the chat model","Users are wrong; perplexity is the ground truth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Preference tuning changes the output distribution on purpose, so fit to raw text getting worse is expected and not a defect. The right evaluation for an assistant is the task it is meant to perform, which perplexity on web text does not measure."}]},{"key":"model-internals/hallucination","sources":["Ji et al., 2023 — Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation","Maynez et al., 2020 — On Faithfulness and Factuality in Abstractive Summarization","Manakul, Liusie and Gales, 2023 — SelfCheckGPT: Zero-Resource Black-Box Hallucination Detection for Generative Large Language Models","Farquhar et al., 2024 — Detecting hallucinations in large language models using semantic entropy"],"why":"A next-token model learns p(next token | context) from text. Nothing in that objective distinguishes \"the\ncapital of France is Paris\", seen millions of times, from \"the 2019 revenue of a small private company\",\nseen once or never. At inference the model must emit a token regardless. When the true answer is not\nrecoverable from the weights, the distribution still peaks somewhere plausible, and the sampler emits that\nwith the same fluency as a well-known fact. Three mechanisms reinforce this. Parametric knowledge is lossy\ncompression: rare facts are stored weakly or not at all, and there is no explicit \"not stored\" signal to\nread. Autoregression commits: once a wrong token is sampled, say a fabricated paper title, every later\ntoken is conditioned on it, and the model's strong prior for coherence makes it elaborate rather than\nretract, so errors snowball. And post-training sharpens the problem in a specific way: human raters and\nbinary-graded benchmarks reward complete, confident answers and penalise hedging, so the policy learns that\na guess scores better than an abstention, exactly as students guess on exams with no negative marking. The\nGPT-4 technical report showed calibration getting worse after RLHF.\n\nThe naive fixes fail for the same reasons. \"Train on more data\" shrinks the long tail but cannot close it.\n\"Set temperature to zero\" removes sampling noise, but greedy decoding still takes the argmax of a wrong\ndistribution; it cuts variance, not bias. \"Tell it not to hallucinate\" helps at the margin, because the\nmodel cannot reliably introspect on what it knows. \"Fine-tune on the facts\" can teach some facts, but\nfine-tuning on knowledge the base model lacks has been reported to increase hallucination elsewhere. The\ndurable fix is architectural: supply evidence, verify outputs, and make abstention cheap.","how":"### A taxonomy that determines the fix\n- **Faithfulness hallucination**: the output is not supported by the source it was given. The NLG\n  literature (Maynez et al., Ji et al.) splits this further into *intrinsic* (the output contradicts the\n  source, such as a summary that reverses a figure in the document) and *extrinsic* (the output adds content\n  the source neither states nor contradicts, such as an invented figure, which may or may not be true in the\n  world). Both are detectable by comparing against the source; reduced by grounding and verification.\n- **Factuality hallucination**: the output contradicts the world, judged against reality rather than against\n  a given source, such as a wrong date or an invented citation in a closed-book answer. Needs external\n  knowledge to detect; reduced by retrieval, tools and abstention. Note the axes are different: an extrinsic\n  addition can be factually true, and a faithful summary of a wrong document is factually wrong.\n- **Structural hallucination**: invented tool names, parameters that do not exist, API functions that were\n  never defined. Common in agents and code generation; eliminated almost entirely by schema validation.\n\nThe split matters because each type has a different evaluation and a different mitigation, and a\n\"hallucination rate\" that mixes them is not actionable.\n\n### Where in the pipeline it arises\n1. **Knowledge.** The question is outside the training distribution or after the training cutoff.\n2. **Retrieval.** In a RAG system the retriever returns irrelevant, partial, conflicting or stale passages,\n   and a model instructed to answer bridges the gaps with its priors. A large share of \"model\n   hallucinations\" reported for RAG products are retrieval failures.\n3. **Decoding.** High temperature or top-p widens the tail; long generations accumulate risk; the most\n   surprising tokens are usually the fabricated specifics, such as numbers, names and URLs.\n4. **Prompt pressure.** \"Always answer\", \"be definitive\", role-play framing, and questions with false\n   premises (\"Why did X do Y?\" when X never did) all push the distribution toward fabrication.\n5. **Sycophancy.** The user asserts something and the model agrees, because agreement was rewarded in\n   preference tuning.\n\n### Detection mechanisms\n- **Sampling consistency.** Sample N answers; facts the model actually knows are stable across samples,\n  fabrications vary (SelfCheckGPT). Costs N× compute and misses confident, consistent errors.\n- **Semantic entropy.** Cluster the samples by meaning and compute entropy over clusters. High entropy flags\n  unreliable answers better than token-level entropy, because paraphrases of one answer no longer count as\n  disagreement.\n- **Token-level signals.** Low log-probability on entity and number tokens is a useful cheap flag, but\n  these probabilities are miscalibrated after RLHF, so thresholds must be tuned per model.\n- **Claim-level verification.** For grounded tasks, split the answer into atomic claims and check each\n  against the retrieved passages with an NLI model or an LLM judge, reporting an unsupported-claim rate. This\n  is the faithfulness metric in evals, and it is the most actionable measurement because it names the\n  claim that failed.\n- **Tool-based verification.** Run the code, call the API, look up the citation. Cheapest and most reliable\n  wherever a checker exists.\n\n### Reduction mechanisms\n- **Grounding.** Retrieve evidence, instruct the model to quote or cite spans, verify the citations after\n  generation, and show the sources to the user.\n- **Abstention paths.** Put an explicit \"insufficient information\" option in the output schema and score it\n  as correct on unanswerable items in your evals, so neither prompt iteration nor fine-tuning trains it away.\n- **Constrained outputs.** Enumerations, JSON schemas and tool-call validation against the real tool\n  registry remove whole classes of structural hallucination.\n- **Decoding.** Lower temperature for factual and extractive steps; keep sampling diversity for creative\n  ones.\n- **Verification loops.** Draft, generate verification questions, answer them independently, revise.\n  Reasoning models perform a version of this inside their thinking trace.\n- **Training-side.** Preference data that rewards calibrated abstention and penalises confident errors more\n  than \"I don't know\"; this is a vendor-level lever, but it tells you what to look for in model choice.\n- **Product design.** Surface confidence and sources, make verification one click, keep a human in the loop\n  for consequential outputs.","example":"A claim-level faithfulness check for a RAG answer. It costs one call to extract claims plus one call per\nclaim, so it is run as an offline eval on sampled traffic and as an online gate only for high-stakes\ncategories. The judge is itself a model and can be wrong in the same direction as the generator, so its\nverdicts must be calibrated against human labels before the rate goes on a dashboard.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\ndef llm(prompt, temperature=0.0):\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider here and return the text.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef extract_claims(answer):\n    out = llm(\"Split this answer into atomic factual claims. \"\n              f\"Return a JSON list of strings.\\n\\nANSWER:\\n{answer}\")\n    return json.loads(out)\n\ndef verify(claim, passages):\n    ctx = \"\\n\\n\".join(f\"[{i}] {p}\" for i, p in enumerate(passages))\n    out = llm(\"Decide whether the CLAIM is supported by the PASSAGES. Reply with JSON \"\n              '{\"verdict\": \"supported\" | \"contradicted\" | \"not_found\", \"source\": <index or null>}.\\n\\n'\n              f\"PASSAGES:\\n{ctx}\\n\\nCLAIM: {claim}\")\n    return json.loads(out)\n\ndef faithfulness_report(answer, passages):\n    claims = extract_claims(answer)\n    verdicts = [verify(c, passages) for c in claims]\n    unsupported = [(c, v[\"verdict\"]) for c, v in zip(claims, verdicts) if v[\"verdict\"] != \"supported\"]\n    return {\"claims\": len(claims), \"unsupported\": unsupported,\n            \"unsupported_rate\": len(unsupported) / max(1, len(claims))}\n\nretrieved_passages = [\"Refunds are issued within 14 days of a returned item being received.\"]\nanswer_text = \"Refunds take 14 days and are paid to the original card.\"   # second claim is unsupported\nreport = faithfulness_report(answer_text, retrieved_passages)\nif report[\"unsupported_rate\"] > 0:\n    # Options: regenerate with the unsupported claims listed as \"do not state\",\n    # return only the supported claims with their [source] indices, or route to review.\n    ...\n```\n\nThe same decomposition gives you error analysis for free: unsupported claims that are \"not_found\" with\nweak retrieval scores point at the retriever, while \"contradicted\" claims point at the generator.","tradeoffs":["Retrieval grounding cuts factual hallucination sharply, but moves the failure to retrieval quality; when the corpus lacks the answer, a model told to \"answer from context\" either abstains or bridges the gap, so a check is still needed.","Sampling-based detection is model-agnostic and needs no labels, but multiplies cost by N and only catches instability, not confident errors the model learned consistently from training data.","Lower temperature reduces variance on factual tasks, but does not fix a wrong argmax and hurts tasks that need diversity; the trade flips for brainstorming and creative generation.","Rewarding abstention reduces confident errors, but overdone it produces a model that refuses or hedges on answerable questions; users punish both failures, so tune the abstention rate to your task's cost asymmetry.","Claim-level verification gives precise, actionable numbers, but adds latency and a second model that can be wrong; it earns its cost on high-stakes outputs and as an offline eval, rarely on every chat turn."],"pitfalls":["Blaming the model for a retrieval failure; the symptom is hallucinations clustered on questions whose top-k passages have low relevance scores or whose answers are not in the corpus at all.","Reporting a hallucination rate from an LLM judge that was never calibrated against humans; the symptom is a judge that shares the generator's priors and a dashboard reading 2% while SMEs find 15%.","Treating temperature 0 as \"hallucination off\"; the symptom is deterministic, repeatable fabrications that look like facts precisely because they are consistent.","Scoring \"I don't know\" as a failure in evals and demos; the symptom is that after a round of prompt or fine-tuning iteration, abstentions vanish and confident errors rise.","Fine-tuning on domain facts the model never saw to \"teach it the knowledge\"; the symptom is a model that learned the format and a few facts and now fabricates fluently about the rest.","Accepting a cited answer without checking that the citation supports the claim; the symptom is real document titles attached to statements they do not contain, since citation hallucination survives retrieval.","Letting an agent invent tool names or arguments; the symptom is \"function not found\" errors or calls with parameters that do not exist, fixed by validating every call against the live schema and reflecting the error back."],"when":"You do not choose whether hallucination applies; you choose how much to spend against it per use case. For\nlow-stakes creative or conversational features, light measures suffice: sources where available and a\ntemperature appropriate to the task. For extraction and summarisation, ground every output in the input and\nmeasure faithfulness on a golden set. For factual question answering, use retrieval with citations, an\nabstention path and offline claim-level verification. For agents, validate every tool call against the real\nschema and verify consequential actions before executing them. Measure the unsupported-claim rate, the\nabstention rate on unanswerable items, calibration of confidence against correctness, and per-category\nerror analysis. In interviews, explain the mechanism (objective, decoding, incentives), the\nfaithfulness/factuality split and the intrinsic/extrinsic subdivision, three mitigations with their costs,\nand why temperature 0 is not a fix.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the grounding pipeline, adds an \"insufficient evidence\" output path, validates tool calls against schemas, and ships a claim-level faithfulness eval that runs in CI.","architect":"Sets the per-use-case hallucination budget and verification tier, decides where human review is mandatory, and makes abstention a first-class product state rather than an error.","fde":"Runs error analysis with customer SMEs to separate retrieval failures from generation failures, and calibrates the judge on their labelled examples before any hallucination rate appears in a report."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that your RAG assistant \"hallucinates about 10% of the time\" on their policy\ndocuments. Walk through how you would diagnose and reduce it, and name one thing you would refuse to do.","a":"First get the failing examples labelled and split them. Did retrieval return the passage containing\nthe answer? If not, it is a corpus or retrieval problem: chunking, embeddings, missing documents. If it did,\ndid the model contradict the passage (intrinsic) or add detail the passage does not support (extrinsic)?\nBoth are faithfulness failures with different fixes. Was the question\nanswerable at all, in which case abstention is the correct output and the eval must reward it? Then fix\nretrieval first, since it is usually the largest share; require citations with quoted spans; add an\n\"insufficient information\" path; lower the temperature on the answer step; add claim-level verification for\nhigh-stakes categories; and calibrate the LLM judge against the SME labels before reporting any rate. I\nwould refuse to fine-tune the model on the policy documents as a knowledge fix, or to add \"do not\nhallucinate\" to the prompt and call it done."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does setting temperature to 0 not eliminate hallucination?","options":["Temperature only affects the first generated token","Greedy decoding still picks the most probable token from a distribution that may be wrong; it removes sampling variance, not the underlying knowledge gap","Most APIs ignore the temperature parameter","Temperature 0 disables retrieval"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Temperature rescales the distribution the sampler draws from. When the model does not know the answer, the peak of that distribution is already a fabrication, and taking the argmax reproduces it every time. That is why greedy decoding yields consistent, not correct, errors."},{"q":"In a RAG system, which observation most strongly indicates that a reported \"hallucination\" is actually a retrieval failure?","options":["The answer is longer than usual","The model was run at a low temperature","The retrieved passages do not contain the needed information, and the model's answer fills the gap with plausible detail","The answer contradicts a passage that was retrieved"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the evidence was never retrieved, no generator could have produced a grounded answer, so the fix is in chunking, embeddings or corpus coverage. Contradicting a retrieved passage is the opposite case, a faithfulness failure of the generator."},{"q":"Why can post-training with human preferences or binary-graded benchmarks increase confident wrong answers?","options":["Reward models prefer short answers","Hedging and abstention are scored as failures, so the policy learns that a confident guess has higher expected reward than \"I don't know\"","RLHF removes the model's access to its training data","It raises the default sampling temperature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When an abstention earns zero and a guess earns a reward whenever it happens to be right, the optimal policy guesses. Calibration measurably worsens under such training, which is why evals must give credit for correct abstention on unanswerable items."}]},{"key":"model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","sources":["Wei et al., 2022 — Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models","Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models","Snell et al., 2024 — Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally Can Be More Effective Than Scaling Model Parameters","DeepSeek-AI, 2025 — DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning"],"why":"A decoder-only transformer spends the same compute on every token: L layers, one pass. Whether the next\ntoken completes \"2 + 2 =\" or \"the smallest prime factor of 8,633 is\", the depth of computation is\nidentical. Problems that need many sequential steps therefore cannot be solved in one forward pass once\nthey exceed what the layer stack can compose; the model guesses. Chain-of-thought prompting (Wei et al.,\n2022) was the first clear evidence: asking the model to write intermediate steps lets it use its own output\nas working memory, turning one hard prediction into many easy ones. Each emitted token is another forward\npass, so a longer trace is literally more compute spent on the problem.\n\nTwo further observations turned a prompting trick into a scaling axis. Self-consistency (Wang et al., 2022)\nsamples several chains and majority-votes the final answer; accuracy rises with samples because wrong paths\ntend to disagree with each other while correct paths converge. And coverage keeps growing: the chance that\nat least one of N samples is correct rises roughly log-linearly in N over a wide range, so anything that\ncan recognise a correct answer, from a unit test to a reward model, can harvest it. Snell et al. then\nshowed in 2024 that for a fixed compute budget, spending it at inference time can beat spending it on a\nlarger model, with the best allocation depending on how hard the question is.\n\nWhy not just prompt for chain of thought? Prompted reasoning is shallow: the model imitates what reasoning\nlooks like in its pre-training data, rarely backtracks, and does not know when to stop. Reasoning models\n(OpenAI's o-series, DeepSeek-R1, and the extended-thinking modes of Anthropic and Google models) train\nthe behaviour with reinforcement learning on tasks whose answers can be checked, such as maths, code and\nlogic. Rewarding the correct final answer is enough for the policy to learn to explore alternatives, verify\nits own steps and self-correct inside the trace; the DeepSeek-R1 report describes these behaviours emerging\nwithout being demonstrated. The naive alternative, a bigger base model, is expensive to pre-train and still\ndoes fixed compute per token, so it does not remove the underlying constraint.","how":"### Tokens as computation\nEach generated token costs a forward pass, about 2N FLOPs plus attention over the growing context, and its\nresult is written into the context where later steps can read it through attention. A 2,000-token thinking\ntrace is therefore 2,000 sequential passes of extra computation with an explicit scratchpad. The serial\ndepth comes from the token dimension, not the layer dimension, which is why chain of thought provably\nextends what a fixed-depth transformer can compute. The exchange rate is explicit: cost and latency grow\nlinearly with trace length, and the trace occupies context and KV-cache memory.\n\n### Four strategies for spending inference compute\n1. **Sequential.** One path, more steps: longer chain of thought, self-critique, revision loops.\n2. **Parallel.** N independent samples at temperature above zero, aggregated by majority vote on the final\n   answer (which needs a canonical answer) or by a verifier or reward model choosing the best (best-of-N).\n3. **Search.** Expand solutions step by step, score partial solutions with a process reward model, and\n   keep the best candidates (beam search or tree search over steps). Most useful on hard problems where whole\n   samples rarely contain a fully correct path.\n4. **Trained reasoning.** The model internalises the first three in a single long trace through RL, and\n   at inference you control a budget rather than orchestrating samples yourself.\n\nSnell et al. found the best strategy depends on difficulty: sequential revision wins on easy questions where\nthe first attempt is nearly right, parallel sampling and search win on hard ones where diverse attempts are\nneeded, and a difficulty-aware policy that picks per question is reported to be several times more\ncompute-efficient than any fixed strategy.\n\n### Verifiers\nOutcome reward models score only the final answer; process reward models score each step, and Lightman et\nal. found step-level supervision more reliable for maths. Wherever a ground-truth checker exists, such as\nunit tests, a calculator or a symbolic solver, use it: it is exact and free. That is also why maths and code\nare where reasoning models improved most; the reward signal there is verifiable, so RL has something clean\nto optimise. Majority voting needs discrete answers; for free-form outputs you fall back to an LLM judge or\npairwise comparison, which brings its own error rate.\n\n### How a reasoning model is trained, in outline\nStart from a pre-trained and usually instruction-tuned model. Run RL on problems with checkable answers.\nDeepSeek-R1 used GRPO: sample a group of responses per prompt, score each with rule-based rewards for\ncorrectness and format, compute each response's advantage relative to the group's mean, and update the\npolicy toward higher-advantage responses. Trace length grows over training as the model discovers that\nchecking its work pays. A later stage adds supervised fine-tuning on curated traces plus another RL round\nfor readability and general tasks. Distillation then transfers much of the capability: fine-tuning smaller\nmodels on traces from the big reasoner gives small models that reason far better than their size suggests,\nwith the gains concentrated on the distilled domains.\n\n### What you control at inference\nA thinking budget or effort level, under vendor-specific names; whether the trace is returned, hidden or\nsummarised; temperature and N for your own parallel sampling; and stopping rules such as an agreement\nthreshold. Thinking tokens are billed as output tokens, the whole trace precedes the first answer token, and\nthe trace competes with your prompt for context.\n\n### Limits\n- **Overthinking.** On easy questions long traces add cost without accuracy and can even hurt; some models\n  loop in very long traces.\n- **Faithfulness.** The visible trace is not guaranteed to reflect the factors that actually drove the\n  answer (Turpin et al., 2023); it is not an audit log.\n- **Domain.** Gains concentrate where answers are verifiable; on open-ended writing or on questions\n  bottlenecked by knowledge rather than reasoning, retrieval helps more than thinking.\n- **Variance.** Results fluctuate between runs more than single-shot answers do, so evaluate with several\n  runs and report pass@k or pass^k, not one number.","example":"Self-consistency with early stopping and an escalation signal. If a single chain is right 70% of the time\nand wrong chains scatter across different values, a majority of five is right well above 70% (with\nindependent samples and a binary outcome the figure is about 84%, and scattered wrong answers make it\nbetter). Agreement doubles as a confidence signal: high agreement stops spending early, low agreement\nroutes the question to a stronger model or a human, which is a practical version of difficulty-aware\nallocation.\n\n```python\nimport collections, re\n\ndef llm(prompt, temperature=0.7):\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider here and return the text.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef solve(question, n_max=8, n_min=3, stop_agreement=0.8):\n    prompt = f\"{question}\\nReason step by step, then finish with a line 'ANSWER: <value>'.\"\n    votes, spent = collections.Counter(), 0\n    for i in range(1, n_max + 1):\n        out = llm(prompt)\n        spent += len(out.split())                       # proxy; use the provider's token count in practice\n        m = re.search(r\"ANSWER:\\s*(.+)\", out)\n        if m:\n            votes[m.group(1).strip().rstrip(\".\")] += 1   # normalise the answer string before voting\n        if i >= n_min and votes:\n            best, count = votes.most_common(1)[0]\n            if count / i >= stop_agreement:              # paths converged: stop spending\n                return {\"answer\": best, \"agreement\": count / i, \"samples\": i, \"tokens\": spent}\n    best, count = votes.most_common(1)[0] if votes else (None, 0)\n    return {\"answer\": best, \"agreement\": count / n_max, \"samples\": n_max, \"tokens\": spent,\n            \"escalate\": count / n_max < 0.5}             # low agreement: route to a stronger model or review\n\nprint(solve(\"A train leaves at 09:40 and arrives at 13:05. How many minutes is the journey?\"))\n```\n\nSwap the vote for a verifier when one exists: run the unit tests on each sampled patch and return the first\nthat passes, which turns coverage directly into accuracy.","tradeoffs":["Accuracy on verifiable multi-step tasks rises with inference budget, but cost and latency rise linearly with tokens; the trade flips when the task is easy, high-volume or latency-bound, where a fine-tuned small model wins.","Parallel sampling parallelises well, so latency stays near one sample, but cost multiplies by N; sequential long traces can need fewer total tokens for the same gain on some problems, but the latency is the bill.","Verifier-based selection extracts far more from N samples than majority vote, but needs a verifier; with tests or solvers it is nearly free, with an LLM judge you have added error and cost.","Reasoning models give large out-of-the-box gains on maths, code and planning, but their traces are billed and consume context, and on extraction or classification they add cost with little gain.","Distilled small reasoners deliver much of the capability cheaply, but inherit the teacher's trace style and tendency to overthink, and the gains are strongest on the domains they were distilled on."],"pitfalls":["Turning reasoning on for every request; the symptom is cost per request rising several-fold and p95 latency exploding with no measurable quality change on the simple tasks that dominate traffic.","Treating the thinking trace as an explanation; the symptom is a trace that reads soundly while the answer was driven by a bias the trace never mentions.","Majority-voting free-text answers; the symptom is near-zero agreement because no two strings match; normalise the answer or use a judge.","Evaluating with a single run; the symptom is swings of several points between runs of the \"same\" eval; report means over several seeds or pass^k.","Forgetting the trace in context budgeting; the symptom is truncated answers or length errors deep in agent loops as thinking tokens fill the window.","Loading a reasoning model's prompt with chain-of-thought instructions and reasoning few-shots; several vendors advise against it because it can degrade results; keep the prompt plain and let the model think.","Assuming the budget knob is linear in quality; the symptom is paying for 4× the tokens and getting the accuracy of 2× because nobody measured the accuracy-versus-tokens curve on the real task."],"when":"Reach for test-time compute when the task has multi-step structure and a checkable, or at least judgeable,\nanswer: maths, code generation and debugging, planning inside agents, complex data transformations, and\nhard classifications where a rationale changes the verdict. Measure the accuracy-versus-tokens curve on\nyour own eval at several budgets and sample counts, pick the knee, and add difficulty-aware routing: a cheap\nmodel first, escalation on low agreement or a failed verifier. Do not use it for latency-bound chat, simple\nextraction, or questions bottlenecked by knowledge, where retrieval wins. In interviews, explain why extra\ntokens are extra compute, contrast parallel and sequential strategies and when each wins, sketch how\nR1-style RL works, and raise the faithfulness caveat unprompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements thinking budgets, sampling with early stopping and verifier gates, tracks thinking tokens in cost dashboards, and keeps traces out of the next prompt unless they are needed.","architect":"Decides which tasks earn a reasoning tier, defines routing between fast and reasoning models by difficulty signals, and sets latency and cost budgets per tier.","fde":"Shows a customer the accuracy-versus-cost curve on their own hard cases, so the choice between a bigger model, a reasoning mode and more samples is made with their numbers rather than a benchmark."},"check":{"q":"A team's coding agent solves 60% of tasks with a standard model. They can switch to a reasoning\nmodel at roughly five times the token cost, sample the standard model five times and pick with the test\nsuite, or fine-tune. How do you reason about the choice?","a":"Start from the verifier: they have unit tests, so best-of-five with the suite as selector is cheap\nand exact, coverage rises steeply with N, the five samples parallelise so latency stays near one attempt,\nand the cost is similar to the reasoning model's. Its weakness is incomplete tests, which let wrong patches\npass. The reasoning model likely raises single-shot accuracy and helps most on tasks needing long-horizon\nplanning, where sampling rarely finds a complete solution, and where tests are weak. The pragmatic design is\nto combine them: standard model first with test-based selection, escalation to the reasoning model on\ntasks that fail. Fine-tuning helps only if failures are stylistic or convention-related and there is data.\nDecide on pass@1, pass@5 and cost per solved task measured on their task set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does writing out intermediate steps let a transformer solve problems it fails on when asked for the answer directly?","options":["The steps are copied from similar problems in the training data","Each generated token is another forward pass, so the trace adds sequential computation and an external working memory beyond the fixed per-token depth","It lowers the effective sampling temperature","Intermediate steps reduce the size of the KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A forward pass has fixed depth, so multi-step problems need more passes. Emitting tokens provides them, and attention lets later steps read earlier results. The trace grows the KV cache rather than shrinking it, and temperature is unchanged."},{"q":"When does parallel sampling with majority vote fail to help, even with many samples?","options":["When the problem is a maths word problem","When the temperature is above zero","When the model's errors are systematic, so wrong paths converge on the same wrong answer, or when answers are free text that cannot be matched","When the model is a reasoning model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Voting works because independent wrong paths disagree while correct ones agree. A shared misconception breaks that assumption, and free-text answers break the matching step; a verifier or a judge is needed in both cases."},{"q":"Which claim about a reasoning model's thinking trace is correct?","options":["It is a faithful record of the computation and can serve as an audit log","It is billed as output tokens, consumes context, and may not reflect the factors that actually drove the answer","It is free because it is discarded before the answer is produced","Longer traces always improve accuracy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Traces are generated tokens with all the usual costs, and research on chain-of-thought faithfulness shows they can omit the real reasons behind an answer. Longer is not always better; on easy tasks it adds cost and can reduce accuracy."}]}]});
